      "id": "ee0bea01-ca8e-4d38-8e29-f9018736c3ae",
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "The fix is fully verified end-to-end:\n\n- **`forecast`** is `np.float32(0.0)` \u2014 a proper binary label, not the raw `\"no wave\"` string.\n- **`ds_index`** is a matched timestamp from the wave catalog.\n- Dataset length is `6` (respecting `max_number_of_samples`).\n\n**Summary of the fix:**\n1. `configs.py` \u2014 added the missing `ds_class_column` field to `FlareDataConfig` so the YAML key you added stops being an \"unknown key\" error.\n2. `datasets/wave_dataset.py` \u2014 `waveDSDataset` now reads the label from `ds_class_column` instead of a hardcoded `\"intensity\"` column, with a clear `ValueError` if it's not supplied.\n3. Notebook cells `f76d377c` and `70e9c5b2` \u2014 pass `ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column` and a `label_transform=lambda s: (s == \"wave\").astype(np.float32)` to turn the `\"wave\"`/`\"no wave\"` strings into binary `1.0`/`0.0` labels; also fixed the undefined `FlareDSDataset` \u2192 `waveDSDataset` typo in the later `return_surya_stack=True` cell.\n\nI ran the notebook cell-by-cell through block #5 (and a couple beyond) in your `jovyan-Surya_WS` kernel \u2014 no errors, and the returned label is exactly what a binary wave classifier needs. You should be good to continue running the rest of the notebook (the Surya-stack cells will trigger S3 downloads, which just take longer).",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789479961.6813436,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false
    },
