              "prompt": "I'm working in a repo called surya_workshop (NASA-IMPACT Surya heliophysics foundation model workshop). Read /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/CLAUDE.md first for context on the repo layout and conventions.\n\nI need to understand the established \"baseline\" pattern used by the template downstream app so I can replicate it for a new classification task. Please thoroughly explore `/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/template/` (this is the reference template; there may also be other downstream apps like `downstream_apps/flare_forecasting` or similar \u2014 check what siblings of `template` exist under `downstream_apps/`).\n\nSpecifically report on:\n1. Is there a notebook or script named something like `1_baseline_template.ipynb`/`.py`? Read it fully and describe what it does step by step \u2014 especially: what is the \"trainable baseline\" it builds (I believe it's a linear/logistic baseline on top of some transformed/pooled features), what class/module implements it, where that class lives (`models/`?), and how its trainable parameters are defined (plain nn.Module? sklearn? something else).\n2. How does `lightning_modules/` wrap this baseline model \u2014 read the LightningModule class fully: what does `training_step`/`validation_step` look like, how is the loss chosen, and how do the four metric modes (`train_loss`, `val_loss`, `train_metrics`, `val_metrics`) mentioned in CLAUDE.md get wired up in practice (look at `metrics/` directory too).\n3. How is the baseline's train/val loop actually run \u2014 is there a dedicated script (e.g. `2_train_baseline.py` or a `--train_baseline` CLI flag mentioned in CLAUDE.md) or is it done inline in the notebook with a plain PyTorch loop / Lightning Trainer?\n4. What config fields (in `configs/config_script.yaml` and the app's `configs.py`) are specific to the baseline (e.g. hyperparameters, which channel(s) it uses, pooling kernel size, etc.)?\n5. How does `destandardize_channels()` (mentioned in CLAUDE.md, in the context of `scaler.inverse_transform()` feeding \"the linear baseline\") work and where is it defined/used? This seems directly relevant since I want to compute a running difference on a specific channel (aia193) and I'll need to go from z-scored/signum-log space back to something physically meaningful, or understand why the existing baseline does or doesn't do this.\n\nAlso check: does `workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py` or anything else define a generic small \"head\" module (e.g. a linear/logistic layer) that's reused across apps, which I should reuse rather than reinvent?\n\nReport back with concrete file paths, class/function names, and short code excerpts (not full file dumps) so I can design a similar baseline for a binary wave-classification task. Keep the report focused and under 600 words plus code excerpts."
