            "raw_input": {
              "prompt": "I'm working in a repo called surya_workshop (NASA-IMPACT Surya heliophysics foundation model workshop, standalone fork). Read /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/CLAUDE.md first for background on the data pipeline and config conventions.\n\nI need to understand exactly how `HelioNetCDFDataset` (in `/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`) handles multiple input timesteps, because I want to compute a running difference between two consecutive timesteps of a single channel (AIA 193) inside a downstream dataset/model.\n\nPlease answer with concrete detail, citing file:line:\n\n1. How do `time_delta_input_minutes` (a list, e.g. `[0]` currently, but I may need `[-12, 0]` or similar) and `n_input_timestamps` / `cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim` relate to each other? Is `n_input_timestamps` required to equal `len(time_delta_input_minutes)`, and where is that enforced/validated?\n2. What is the exact shape and dimension order of the `'ts'` tensor returned by `__getitem__` (I believe it's `[C, T, H, W]` per a comment I saw in a notebook \u2014 confirm channel count C=13, and how the time dimension T indexes into `time_delta_input_minutes`, e.g. is index 0 the earliest or the latest offset?)\n3. How does the dataset pick actual on-disk timestamps to satisfy multiple `time_delta_input_minutes` offsets \u2014 does it require both frames to exist in the NetCDF/HDF5 index (i.e., could requesting `[-12, 0]` silently drop samples near the start of the index, or fail loudly if the earlier frame is missing)? Look at `valid_indices` construction.\n4. How are channels ordered inside the `C` dimension \u2014 is there a fixed list (e.g. `cfg.data.channels`) and how would I find the index of `aia193` specifically at runtime (a helper function, or just `cfg.data.channels.index(\"aia193\")`)?\n5. What does `dataset.inverse_transform_data()` vs `scaler.inverse_transform()` do exactly (CLAUDE.md mentions signum-log-then-zscore forward pipeline, with `scaler.inverse_transform()` undoing only the z-score \u2192 signum-log space, and `inverse_transform_data()` undoing both \u2192 physical units). Show the actual code for both. Which one would I want if I'm computing a running difference for a classical/baseline feature (not necessarily physical units, just something reasonable to feed a pooled logistic regression)?\n6. Is there a reusable average-pooling utility anywhere in `workshop_infrastructure/` (e.g. in `models/` or `utils.py`), or would a 32x32 mean pool with weights 1/1024 just be `torch.nn.AvgPool2d(kernel_size=32)`?\n\nReport back with file:line citations and short code excerpts. Keep it focused, under 600 words plus code excerpts.",
