            "raw_output": "1\t# CLAUDE.md\n2\t\n3\tThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n4\t\n5\t## Overview\n6\t\n7\tThis is **surya_workshop**, a standalone repo built around the [Surya](https://github.com/NASA-IMPACT/Surya.git) foundation model for heliophysics (a NASA-IMPACT / IBM AI4Science collaboration). The repo provides:\n8\t- `workshop_infrastructure/` \u2014 shared config, dataset loaders, dataset/dataloader builders, PEFT utilities, and data pipeline scripts. Also contains a **vendored copy** of the 366M-parameter Surya backbone under `workshop_infrastructure/models/`. There is no `Surya/` submodule: the code was copied in so the repo runs standalone, which means it can drift from upstream without any diff signal.\n9\t- `downstream_apps/` \u2014 template and concrete downstream fine-tuning applications\n10\t- `analysis/` \u2014 research scripts (embedding probing/ablation); not part of the workshop template path\n11\t\n12\tThe objective of this repo is to allow future Surya users an easy to modify set of templates that they can use to build their own finetunign applications.  Most of the reusable infrastructure should be in the `workshop_infrastructure/` folder. \n13\t\n14\tThe primary directives of any code development should be:\n15\t\n16\t1. Clarity.\n17\t2. Reusability.\n18\t3. Simplicity.\n19\t4. Functionality\n20\t\n21\tAs a secondary objective, this repository should help people develop good AI development\n22\tpractices in scientific AI.\n23\t\n24\t## Environment Setup\n25\t\n26\t```bash\n27\tconda env create -f environment.yml\n28\tconda activate surya_ws\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\tPython 3.12+ required. Key dependencies: PyTorch, PyTorch Lightning, PEFT (LoRA), WandB, SunPy, xarray, Dask, fsspec.\n32\t\n33\t## Common Commands\n34\t\n35\t```bash\n36\t# Fine-tune a downstream model (from repo root).\n37\t# --config defaults to the app's own configs/config_script.yaml.\n38\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0,1 python -m downstream_apps.template.3_finetune_template_1D \\\n39\t  --batch-size 2 --max-epochs 20\n40\t\n41\t# Quick sanity run: cap the dataset with max_samples in the YAML, then\n42\tCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 python -m downstream_apps.template.3_finetune_template_1D \\\n43\t  --max-epochs 2 --no-wandb\n44\t\n45\t# Benchmark S3 throughput to pick s3_boto3_* settings for this machine\n46\tpython -m workshop_infrastructure.benchmark_s3 \\\n47\t  s3://nasa-surya-bench/2011/01/20110131_0000.nc --anon --quick\n48\t\n49\t# Linting / formatting\n50\tblack --line-length 100 .\n51\tisort .\n52\tmypy .\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\t`pytest` is installed in the environment, but no test suite exists yet \u2014 verify changes by running the training script with `max_samples` capped (see above).\n56\t\n57\t## Architecture\n58\t\n59\t### Core Model (`workshop_infrastructure/models/`)\n60\t\n61\t**HelioSpectFormer** is a spatiotemporal transformer with two novel block types:\n62\t\n63\t1. **Spectral Gating** (`spectformer.py`): FFT-based global filtering \u2014 transforms patches to frequency domain, applies learnable complex weights, then iFFT back.\n64\t2. **Long-Short Attention** (`transformer_ls.py`): Combines local windowed attention (`window_size=2`) with global attention via dynamic projection (`dp_rank=4`). Efficient for 4096\u00d74096 solar images.\n65\t\n66\tInput: 13-channel SDO stacks (8 AIA wavelengths + 5 HMI magnetic components), patch size 16, embed_dim 1280.\n67\tArchitecture: 2 spectral gating blocks + 8 long-short attention blocks.\n68\t\n69\t### Downstream Fine-tuning Pattern\n70\t\n71\tEach downstream task follows this pattern:\n72\t- `configs.py` \u2014 a `DataConfig` subclass holding **only** the task-specific config fields. Everything generic (and `load_config()` itself) lives in `workshop_infrastructure/configs.py` and is never copied.\n73\t- `datasets/` \u2014 task dataset inheriting from `HelioNetCDFDataset` (see `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`)\n74\t- `models/` \u2014 task-specific head\n75\t- `lightning_modules/` \u2014 PyTorch Lightning wrapper with loss and metrics\n76\t- `metrics/` \u2014 custom metric implementations. Four modes: `train_loss` (backpropagated), `val_loss` (**what ModelCheckpoint monitors**; defaults to `train_loss`), `train_metrics` and `val_metrics` (reported only \u2014 they do *not* select checkpoints)\n77\t- `configs/config_script.yaml` \u2014 single YAML drives everything\n78\t- `N_*.py` / `N_*.ipynb` \u2014 numbered scripts/notebooks for step-by-step workflow\n79\t\n80\tDataset and DataLoader construction is **not** re-implemented per app: `build_helio_dataloaders()` in `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py` maps the config onto the ~20 `HelioNetCDFDataset` arguments, and the app passes only its task-specific kwargs.\n81\t\n82\t### LoRA Fine-tuning\n83\t\n84\tPEFT LoRA is applied to attention and feed-forward layers (rank=8, alpha=8, dropout=0.1, target modules: q/k/v/out_proj, fc1/fc2). The `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py` `apply_peft_lora()` helper handles this.\n85\t\n86\tThree regimes, both selected from the `model:` config section:\n87\t- `use_lora: true` \u2014 LoRA adapters (default)\n88\t- `use_lora: false, freeze_backbone: true` \u2014 linear probe, head only\n89\t- `use_lora: false, freeze_backbone: false` \u2014 full fine-tuning\n90\t\n91\t### Data Pipeline\n92\t\n93\t```\n94\tNetCDF files (SDO, 4096\u00d74096, 13 channels, 12-min cadence)\n95\t  \u2193 CSV index (path, timestamp, label)  \u2190  data/indices/\n96\t  \u2193 HelioNetCDFDataset (local, or S3 via data.s3_mode: download | simplecache | stream)\n97\t  \u2193 Signum-log normalization: sign(x)*log(1+|x|) per channel\n98\t  \u2193 DataLoader \u2192 HelioSpectformer1D \u2192 task head\n99\t```\n100\t\n101\tScalers (normalization stats per channel) are stored in `assets/scalers.yaml` and loaded at dataset init time by `build_scalers()`. That function always resolves scaler classes from the vendored `workshop_infrastructure.datasets.transformations`, deliberately ignoring the stale `base:` field each entry records \u2014 normalization must not depend on what happens to be installed.\n102\t\n103\t**Three spaces, two different \"inverse\" operations.** The forward pipeline is signum-log *then* z-score, so:\n104\t- `scaler.inverse_transform()` undoes the z-score only \u2192 **signum-log** space. This is what `destandardize_channels()` feeds the linear baseline.\n105\t- `dataset.inverse_transform_data()` undoes both \u2192 **physical** units (DN, Gauss), for plotting or physical-space losses.\n106\t\n107\tNever assume one is the other; the reference block is at the top of `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`.\n108\t\n109\tAssets download on first run via `workshop_infrastructure/assets.py:ensure_assets()`. The two `download_*.sh` scripts are thin wrappers over its CLI.\n110\t\n111\t### Configuration\n112\t\n113\tAll runtime parameters live in a single YAML file (`configs/config_script.yaml`), parsed by `load_config()` in `workshop_infrastructure/configs.py` into a typed `TrainingConfig`. Sections: `data`, `model` (incl. LoRA and time embedding), `training`, `output`, `logging`.\n114\t\n115\tTwo properties matter when editing configs:\n116\t- **Unknown keys raise.** A key not present on the target dataclass is an error naming the valid alternatives, never a silent no-op. Task-specific keys require a field on the app's `DataConfig` subclass.\n117\t- **Paths are relative to the config file** and resolved at load time, so a checked-in config works from any working directory. `s3_cache_dir` is the exception \u2014 it expands `~`/`$VARS` but is never anchored to the repo.\n118\t\n119\t**Reproducibility.** `training.seed` and `training.deterministic` (`false` | `warn` | `true`, **default `false`** for throughput \u2014 determinism costs ~20% wall time) control it. Results are therefore NOT reproducible out of the box; `warn` is the setting to use when comparing runs. `3_finetune_template_1D.py` sets `CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG=:4096:8` **before importing torch** \u2014 this is required for deterministic cuBLAS and is inert if moved after the import, so do not \"tidy\" it into the other imports. The notebooks' first cell does the same. `build_helio_dataloaders()` passes an explicit `generator` and `worker_init_fn`; without them the shuffle order depends on ambient global RNG state. `deterministic: true` is incompatible with `model.learned_flow: true` (`F.grid_sample` has no deterministic CUDA backward).\n120\t\n121\tCLI overrides are deliberately limited to what varies between runs of one config: `--max-epochs`, `--batch-size`, `--s3-cache-dir`, `--deterministic {false,warn,true}`, plus the `--no-wandb` and `--train_baseline` toggles.\n122\t\n123\t### Distributed Training\n124\t\n125\tDDP via PyTorch Lightning. Use `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` to select GPUs. Logging is rank-aware to avoid duplicate WandB/CSV entries.\n126\t\n127\t## Key File Locations\n128\t\n129\t| Purpose | Path |\n130\t|---|---|\n131\t| Core model architecture (vendored) | `workshop_infrastructure/models/helio_spectformer.py` |\n132\t| Base dataset loader | `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py` |\n133\t| Dataset/DataLoader builders | `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py` |\n134\t| Config dataclasses + `load_config()` | `workshop_infrastructure/configs.py` |\n135\t| Asset download (scalers, weights) | `workshop_infrastructure/assets.py` |\n136\t| LoRA application utility | `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py` |\n137\t| Downstream adapter model | `workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py` |\n138\t| Fine-tuning entry point | `downstream_apps/template/3_finetune_template_1D.py` |\n139\t| Model weights (HuggingFace) | `nasa-impact/surya` |\n140\t| Pretrained checkpoint | `downstream_apps/template/assets/surya.366m.v1.pt` |\n141\t"
          },
