            "raw_output": [
              {
                "text": "<cell id=\"d651fb69\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type># PyTorch Lightning baseline template\n\nby Andr\u00e9s Mu\u00f1oz-Jaramillo\n\nThis notebook is meant to act as a template to train and use a simple regression model to define a baseline that can be compared with a DS application.\n\nIt focuses on the concept of defining a PyTorch model, a PyTorch lightning training loop and the deffinition of metrics of performance.\n\nThis notebook assumes familiarity with the concepts of datasets and dataloaders contained in the **_0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb_**</cell id=\"d651fb69\">\n<cell id=\"f6bade9c\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Set your cuda visible device\n\n**IMPORTANT:** Since we are sharing resources, please make sure that the cuda visible device you put here is the one assigned to your team and your machine.   </cell id=\"f6bade9c\">\n<cell id=\"6d6af87a\">import os\nos.environ[\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = \"0\"</cell id=\"6d6af87a\">\n<cell id=\"4ed04321\"># Must be set BEFORE torch is imported: cuBLAS reads this once, when it initializes, so\n# setting it later has no effect. It is what lets training.deterministic work without a\n# cuBLAS warning on every run. (Restart the kernel if torch was already imported.)\nimport os\nos.environ.setdefault(\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\", \":4096:8\")\n\nimport sys\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\n\nimport torch\nimport yaml\n\nimport lightning as L\nfrom lightning.pytorch.callbacks import ModelCheckpoint\nfrom lightning.pytorch.loggers import CSVLogger, WandbLogger\n\n# Append base path.  May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to the wokshop_infrastructure folder.\nsys.path.append(\"../../\")\n \n# Append Surya path. May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to surya's release code.\n\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers  # Data scaling utilities for Surya stacks\n\ntorch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')\n\n</cell id=\"4ed04321\">\n<cell id=\"04135ff7\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Load configuration\n\nSurya was designed to read a configuration file that defines many aspects of the model\nincluding the data it uses we use this config file to set default values that do not\nneed to be modified, but also to define values specific to our downstream application</cell id=\"04135ff7\">\n<cell id=\"63c1fe60\"># The config is the single source of truth. load_flare_config() parses it into a typed\n# object, exactly as the training script 3_finetune_template_1D.py does, so the same YAML\n# behaves identically here and in production. Notebook 0 walks through what it contains.\nfrom downstream_apps.template.configs import load_flare_config\n\ncfg = load_flare_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")\nprint(f\"Loaded config for job: {cfg.job_id}\")\n</cell id=\"63c1fe60\">\n<cell id=\"cell-6\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Download assets\n\nThe config says where the assets belong, so it is loaded first. `ensure_assets()` fetches only what is missing from HuggingFace, so re-running this is free.\n</cell id=\"cell-6\">\n<cell id=\"cell-7\"># One implementation, shared by the notebooks, the training script and the\n# download_*.sh wrappers: workshop_infrastructure/assets.py.\n# The linear baseline needs no backbone, so skip the 1.8 GB weights.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.assets import ensure_assets\n\nensure_assets(cfg, which=[\"scalers\"])\n\n# Now that scalers.yaml is guaranteed to be on disk, load it. build_scalers()\n# accepts the resolved path directly.\nscalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\nprint(f\"Loaded scalers for {len(scalers)} channels.\")\n</cell id=\"cell-7\">\n<cell id=\"af6574dc\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define Downstream (DS) datasets\n\nThis child class takes as input all expected HelioFM parameters, plus additonal parameters relevant to the downstream application.  Here we focus in particular to the DS index and parameters necessary to combine it with the HelioFM index.\n\nAnother important component of creating a dataset class for your DS is normalization.  Here we use a log normalization on xray flux that will act as the output target.  Making log10(xray_flux) strictly positive and having 66% of its values between 0 and 1\n\nIn this case we will define both a training and a validation dataset using the indices pointed at in the config\n\n**_Important:  In this notebook we sets max_number_of_samples=6 to potentially avoid going through the whole dataset as we explore it.  Keep in mind this for the future in case the database seems smaller than you expect_**\n</cell id=\"af6574dc\">\n<cell id=\"34db2cc6\">from downstream_apps.template.datasets.template_dataset import FlareDSDataset</cell id=\"34db2cc6\">\n<cell id=\"70e9c5b2\"># build_helio_dataloaders() constructs the train and validation datasets and wraps them\n# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\n# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\n# like written out. Only the flare-specific arguments are passed here, which is exactly\n# the list you replace when you fork the template.\n#\n# It also handles two details that are easy to get wrong by hand: the validation set gets\n# phase=\"val\" (no random channel masking or flips), and only the training loader shuffles.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_dataloaders\n\ntrain_data_loader, val_data_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\n    cfg,\n    FlareDSDataset,\n    scalers=scalers,\n    num_workers=4,          # fewer workers than the script: notebooks start faster\n    #### Downstream (DS) specific parameters\n    return_surya_stack=True,\n    max_number_of_samples=6,\n    ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path,\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n)\n\nbatch_size = cfg.batch_size\nprint(f\"train: {len(train_data_loader.dataset)} samples | \"\n      f\"val: {len(val_data_loader.dataset)} samples | batch_size: {batch_size}\")\n</cell id=\"70e9c5b2\">\n<cell id=\"79fda7cf\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Training and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the index they read and in `phase`: `phase=\"val\"` turns off the random channel masking and vertical flips used for training augmentation.\n\nThe loaders use `multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"` \u2014 the dataset holds an S3 client that does not survive `fork`, and spawn also avoids lockups in shared environments.\n</cell id=\"79fda7cf\">\n<cell id=\"48bb2912\"># Inspect a single batch to confirm shapes before training.\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\nprint({k: (tuple(v.shape) if hasattr(v, \"shape\") else type(v).__name__) for k, v in batch.items()})\n</cell id=\"48bb2912\">\n<cell id=\"1d4d2dea\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define simple baseline model\n\nDefining a simple baseline is important to understand what value is bringing the AI model to the problem.  \n\nIt is always very good to have a very simple baseline model.  Ideally one that cannot overfit the data.  This is a very good way of really measuring the value added of complex models.   Classical machine learning excels here:\n\n- Regressions and logistic regressions.\n- Climatological averages.\n- Persistance.\n- Simple transformations.\n\nSimple models avoid excesively optimistic assessments of the capatiblities of a complex models and for many problems are actually remarkably hard to beat.\n\nIn this example we define a simple regression acting on the intensity of each channel.  Note that we invert the normalization to deal with strictly positive quantities.  As with the dataset we will be importing the model from a module so that we can use it within training scripts later on.</cell id=\"1d4d2dea\">\n<cell id=\"3f5fd639\">from downstream_apps.template.models.simple_baseline import RegressionFlareModel</cell id=\"3f5fd639\">\n<cell id=\"18d87992\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>We can now test that this model manipulates a batch as expected and returns an estimate of flare intensity\n\nNote that the simple regression model definition requires knowing the number of channels and timesteps so here we pull that information from the configuration intializing the model.</cell id=\"18d87992\">\n<cell id=\"b0b6ce02\">n_input_timestamps = cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim\nn_channels = len(cfg.data.channels)\n\n# RegressionFlareModel only needs the flattened input dimension.\n# It expects 'ts' in signum-log space \u2014 use destandardize_channels() before calling forward().\nmodel = RegressionFlareModel(n_input_timestamps * n_channels)\n</cell id=\"b0b6ce02\">\n<cell id=\"3fee1e68\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Now we can pass the input stack 'ts' to the model to transform it into our regression output.   Note that since this model has not been trained and was initalized randomly.  The output here has no real meaning.  It only acts as a test that our model forward doesn't have dimension problems.\n\nDimension problemns are the dominant source of error in this kind of work.\n\nNote that our output has now the size of our batch.</cell id=\"3fee1e68\">\n<cell id=\"d7d97841\">from downstream_apps.template.models.simple_baseline import destandardize_channels\n\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\n\n# RegressionFlareModel works in signum-log space, not normalized space.\n# destandardize_channels undoes the per-channel z-score but KEEPS the log compression \u2014\n# raw DN values span too many orders of magnitude to be good features for one linear layer.\n# (For true physical units, use train_dataset.inverse_transform_data() instead. See the\n#  \"THE THREE SPACES\" block in workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py.)\nbatch_logspace = destandardize_channels(batch, channel_order=cfg.data.channels, scalers=scalers)\noutput = model.forward(batch_logspace)[:, 0]  # Get rid of singleton dimension\noutput\n</cell id=\"d7d97841\">\n<cell id=\"36f32e64\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define your metrics\n\nMetrics are a very important part of training AI models.   They provide your models with the quantitification of error, which in turn shifts the weights towards better pefrorming models.  They also provide a way for you to monitor performance, identify overfitting, and quantify value added. \n\nWe now initialize the metrics class which allows you to control what metrics do you want to use as \"loss\" (i.e. the metrics that backpropagate through your model) and which ones for monitoring performance.  As with other components, this takes the form of a loaded module that can be later use in a training script</cell id=\"36f32e64\">\n<cell id=\"34c502ec\">from downstream_apps.template.metrics.template_metrics import FlareMetrics</cell id=\"34c502ec\">\n<cell id=\"d152b127\">train_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"train_loss\")\n# val_loss is the quantity logged as \"val_loss\" and used to pick the best checkpoint.\n# It defaults to the same MSE as train_loss \u2014 override FlareMetrics.val_loss to change it.\nval_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"val_loss\")\ntrain_evaluation_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"train_metrics\")\n# Reported only: val_metrics do NOT influence checkpoint selection.\nvalidation_evaluation_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"val_metrics\")</cell id=\"d152b127\">\n<cell id=\"0550062c\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Now they can be evaluated in our model's output and our ground truth.   First the loss that actually will backpropagate, in this case Mean Squared Errror</cell id=\"0550062c\">\n<cell id=\"2baf9d36\">train_loss_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])</cell id=\"2baf9d36\">\n<cell id=\"3dc5eb6d\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Then a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we can keep an eye on. Note that reporting lots of metrics during training will slow the training process.  I'm including it her as an example, but oftentimes is better to put the diagnostics only in the validation evaluation metrics.\n\nHere we are caclulating the Root Relative Squared Error https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/regression/rse.html \n\nA value below one means the prediction is better than predicting the average.  It is unlikely that this metric will be lower than one with a randomly initialized model</cell id=\"3dc5eb6d\">\n<cell id=\"796559f1\">train_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])</cell id=\"796559f1\">\n<cell id=\"3d65e7cd\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>In the validation evaluation metrics we report both MSE and RRSE</cell id=\"3d65e7cd\">\n<cell id=\"0a0bbb35\">validation_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])</cell id=\"0a0bbb35\">\n<cell id=\"537871ed\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define your PyTorch ligthning module\n\nIn this workshop we will use PyTorch lightning to train our models.  PyTorch lighting reduces the amount of code required to implement a training loop in comparison to PyTorch (at the expense of control and versatility).  \n\nOpening the FlareLightningModule shows a simple Lightning model implementation.  It consists of:\n\n- An initialization of the class (metrics, model, and learning rate).\n- The forward code that runs evaluation of the model.\n- Training and validation steps.\n- Configuration of optimizers.</cell id=\"537871ed\">\n<cell id=\"9d730cf3\">from downstream_apps.template.lightning_modules.pl_simple_baseline import FlareLightningModule</cell id=\"9d730cf3\">\n<cell id=\"3321963d\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Set your global seeds\n\nSince training AI models generally uses stochastic gradient descent, it is a good idea to fix your random seeds so that your training exercise is reproducible.    </cell id=\"3321963d\">\n<cell id=\"cc28903d\">L.seed_everything(42, workers=True)</cell id=\"cc28903d\">\n<cell id=\"119ec413\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Intialize Lightning module\n\nNow we properly initalize the Lightning module to enable training, including passing the dictionary of metrics</cell id=\"119ec413\">\n<cell id=\"553565e7\">from functools import partial\n\nmetrics = {\n    'train_loss': train_loss_metrics,\n    'val_loss': val_loss_metrics,\n    'train_metrics': train_evaluation_metrics,\n    'val_metrics': validation_evaluation_metrics,\n}\n\n# Wire up the inverse transform so FlareLightningModule applies it before every model call.\npreprocess_fn = partial(\n    destandardize_channels,\n    channel_order=cfg.data.channels,\n    scalers=scalers,\n)\n\nlit_model = FlareLightningModule(\n    model, metrics, lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size, preprocess_fn=preprocess_fn\n)\n</cell id=\"553565e7\">\n<cell id=\"7b9c76c8\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Logging\n\nIn order to properly compare experiments against each other, it is very useful to log evaluation metrics in a place where they can be compared against other training runs.  In this workshop we will use Weights and Biases (WandB). \n\nThe first time you run WandB in a machine it will ask you to login to WandB.  You should have received an invitation to our project.  In order to login you must:\n\n- Select option 2 (existing account).   In VScode the dialog opens a box at the top of your screen.\n- Click on get API Key (this will open a browser).\n- Generate API Key.\n- Paste it in the dialog box at the top of your VSCode</cell id=\"7b9c76c8\">\n<cell id=\"48c4d138\">project_name = cfg.wandb_project\nrun_name = \"baseline_experiment_1\"  # give your run a descriptive name\n\nwandb_logger = WandbLogger(\n    entity=cfg.wandb_entity,  # set wandb_entity in the config; null = personal account\n    project=project_name,\n    name=run_name,\n    log_model=False,\n    save_dir=\"./wandb/wandb_tmp\",\n)\n\ncsv_logger = CSVLogger(\"runs\", name=project_name)\n</cell id=\"48c4d138\">\n<cell id=\"7e24e547\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Initialize trainer\n\nWith the loggers done, now the trainer needs to be defined.  The trainer defines several properties of your training run. Here we define:\n\n- The max number of epochs (one epoch represents your model seeing your entire training dataset).\n- Define where the training run will take place (auto uses the GPU if possible, if not, CPU).\n- The loggers.\n- The callbacks (here we save the model with the lowest validation loss).\n- Logging frequency (because we are working with a small dataset it needs to be small).</cell id=\"7e24e547\">\n<cell id=\"b008c604\">max_epochs = 2\n\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Trainer\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntrainer = L.Trainer(\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\n    accelerator=\"auto\",\n    devices=\"auto\",\n    logger=[wandb_logger, csv_logger],\n    callbacks=[\n        ModelCheckpoint(\n            monitor=\"val_loss\",\n            mode=\"min\",\n            save_top_k=1,\n        )\n    ],\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\n)</cell id=\"b008c604\">\n<cell id=\"1088d108\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Fit the model\n\nFinally we fit the model.  We pass the Lighting module, and our dataloaders.</cell id=\"1088d108\">\n<cell id=\"f8a6e448\">trainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)</cell id=\"f8a6e448\">\n<cell id=\"ebcc1a23\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Conclusion\n\nWith this we have now integrated our dataset, dataloaders, metrics, and baseline into an end-2-end training loop.  The next step is to substitute the simple model with Surya.</cell id=\"ebcc1a23\">",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "read",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_019xxRBbctCmyoggaCaWsVRD"
          },
          {
            "title": "Read simple_baseline.py",
            "kind": "read",
            "status": "completed",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_014e7mTcNqfF43ihWEJMxFXd",
            "raw_output": "1\t\"\"\"\n2\tA simple linear regression model to be used as a baseline for flare forecasting.\n3\t\"\"\"\n4\t\n5\timport torch\n6\timport torch.nn as nn\n7\tfrom einops import rearrange\n8\t\n9\t\n10\tdef destandardize_channels(batch: dict, channel_order: list, scalers: dict) -> dict:\n11\t    \"\"\"Return a new batch dict with 'ts' moved from normalized space to signum-log space.\n12\t\n13\t    This undoes the per-channel z-score ONLY. The signum-log compression applied by the\n14\t    dataset is deliberately left in place, so the result is\n15\t    ``sign(x*s) * log1p(|x*s|)`` \u2014 not raw DN/Gauss. Values spanning many orders of\n16\t    magnitude make poor features for a single linear layer, so log space is what the\n17\t    baseline wants.\n18\t\n19\t    If you need true physical units (plotting, a physical-space loss), use\n20\t    ``HelioNetCDFDataset.inverse_transform_data()`` instead, which undoes both stages.\n21\t    See the \"THE THREE SPACES\" block in ``workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py``.\n22\t\n23\t    Args:\n24\t        batch: Batch dict containing at minimum a 'ts' key with shape (B, C, T, H, W).\n25\t        channel_order: Channel names in the same order as the C dimension of 'ts'.\n26\t        scalers: Dict mapping channel name -> scaler with an inverse_transform method.\n27\t\n28\t    Returns:\n29\t        A new batch dict with 'ts' replaced by the de-standardized (signum-log) tensor.\n30\t    \"\"\"\n31\t    x = batch[\"ts\"].clone()\n32\t    with torch.no_grad():\n33\t        for i, channel in enumerate(channel_order):\n34\t            x[:, i, ...] = scalers[channel].inverse_transform(x[:, i, ...])\n35\t    return {**batch, \"ts\": x}\n36\t\n37\t\n38\tclass RegressionFlareModel(nn.Module):\n39\t    def __init__(self, input_dim: int):\n40\t        \"\"\"\n41\t        Initializes the RegressionFlareModel.\n42\t\n43\t        Args:\n44\t            input_dim (int): The size of the input vector after channel and time dimensions are flattened.\n45\t\n46\t        Note:\n47\t            This model expects 'ts' in the batch dict to already be in **signum-log** space\n48\t            (channel z-scores undone, log compression retained). Use\n49\t            destandardize_channels() to pre-process normalized SDO inputs before passing\n50\t            them here (e.g., via the preprocess_fn argument of FlareLightningModule).\n51\t        \"\"\"\n52\t        super().__init__()\n53\t        self.linear = nn.Linear(input_dim, 1)\n54\t\n55\t    def forward(self, x: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n56\t        \"\"\"\n57\t        Performs a forward pass through the model.\n58\t\n59\t        Args:\n60\t            x (dict): Batch dict with 'ts' of shape (B, C, T, H, W) in signum-log space.\n61\t\n62\t        B - Batch size\n63\t        C - Channels\n64\t        T - Time steps\n65\t        H - Height\n66\t        W - Width\n67\t        \"\"\"\n68\t        x = x[\"ts\"]\n69\t\n70\t        # Collapse input stack spatially and take absolute value for strictly positive flare fluxes\n71\t        x = x.abs().mean(dim=[3, 4])\n72\t\n73\t        # Rearrange in preparation for linear layer\n74\t        x = rearrange(x, \"b c t -> b (c t)\")\n75\t\n76\t        return self.linear(x)",
            "raw_input": {
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/template/models/simple_baseline.py"
            },
            "locations": [
              "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/template/models/simple_baseline.py"
            ]
          },
          {
            "status": "completed",
            "title": "Read pl_simple_baseline.py",
            "raw_output": "1\t\"\"\"\n2\tpl_simple_baseline.py\n3\t\n4\tA minimal PyTorch Lightning wrapper for training a flare prediction model.\n5\t\n6\tThis module defines a single LightningModule (FlareLightningModule) that:\n7\t  - Calls a user-provided PyTorch model on batched inputs (batch[\"ts\"])\n8\t  - Computes one or more training/validation losses via a user-provided loss function\n9\t  - Logs scalar losses and evaluation metrics using Lightning's built-in logging\n10\t  - Configures a simple Adam optimizer\n11\t\n12\tIntended use:\n13\t  - Provide a clean, readable baseline training loop in Lightning\n14\t  - Separate \"model architecture\" from \"training mechanics\"\n15\t  - Demonstrate how to log multiple losses/metrics consistently\n16\t\n17\tKey batch contract:\n18\t  - batch[\"ts\"]       : torch.Tensor input stack (e.g., [B, C, T, H, W])\n19\t  - batch[\"forecast\"] : torch.Tensor target values (e.g., [B] or [B,])\n20\t\n21\tOptional preprocessing:\n22\t  - If ``preprocess_fn`` is provided to ``__init__``, it is called on the batch dict\n23\t    before every model call. This is the intended hook for input transformations (such\n24\t    as inverse-normalizing SDO channels) that should not live inside the model.\n25\t\n26\tKey metrics contract (the `metrics` dict passed to __init__):\n27\t  - metrics[\"train_loss\"]    : callable(output, target) -> (loss_dict, weight_list)\n28\t        Backpropagated. Logged as \"train_loss\".\n29\t  - metrics[\"val_loss\"]      : callable(output, target) -> (loss_dict, weight_list)\n30\t        Optional. Logged as \"val_loss\" and therefore what ModelCheckpoint monitors.\n31\t        Falls back to metrics[\"train_loss\"] when absent.\n32\t  - metrics[\"train_metrics\"] : callable(output, target) -> (metric_dict, weight_list)\n33\t  - metrics[\"val_metrics\"]   : callable(output, target) -> (metric_dict, weight_list)\n34\t        Reported only. These do NOT affect checkpoint selection \u2014 \"val_loss\" does.\n35\t\n36\tWhere:\n37\t  - loss_dict / metric_dict map string names -> torch scalar tensors\n38\t  - weight_list is a list-like of floats (or tensors) aligned with the dict iteration order\n39\t    used by this baseline to form a weighted sum loss.\n40\t\"\"\"\n41\t\n42\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n43\t\n44\tfrom typing import Any, Callable, Dict, Mapping, Optional, Tuple\n45\t\n46\timport lightning as L\n47\timport torch\n48\t\n49\t\n50\t# Type aliases for clarity in documentation / teaching.\n51\tLossDict = Mapping[str, torch.Tensor]\n52\tMetricDict = Mapping[str, torch.Tensor]\n53\tWeights = Any  # often a list[float] or list[torch.Tensor]\n54\t\n55\t\n56\tclass FlareLightningModule(L.LightningModule):\n57\t    \"\"\"\n58\t    PyTorch LightningModule for flare prediction training.\n59\t\n60\t    This class wraps:\n61\t      (1) a user-provided PyTorch model (nn.Module-like) and\n62\t      (2) a set of loss/metric callables packaged in the `metrics` dictionary.\n63\t\n64\t    Parameters\n65\t    ----------\n66\t    model:\n67\t        A callable model (typically torch.nn.Module) that accepts the batch input tensor\n68\t        `x = batch[\"ts\"]` and returns predictions `output`.\n69\t\n70\t    metrics:\n71\t        Dictionary containing the training loss function and metric functions.\n72\t\n73\t        Required keys:\n74\t          - \"train_loss\": callable(output, target) -> (losses, weights)\n75\t              losses: dict[str, torch.Tensor] scalar losses\n76\t              weights: list-like aligned with iteration order of losses.keys()\n77\t          - \"train_metrics\": callable(output, target) -> (metrics, weights)\n78\t          - \"val_metrics\": callable(output, target) -> (metrics, weights)\n79\t\n80\t        Optional key:\n81\t          - \"val_loss\": callable(output, target) -> (losses, weights)\n82\t              The validation objective. Defaults to \"train_loss\" when not supplied, so\n83\t              older metrics dicts keep working unchanged.\n84\t\n85\t        The module uses:\n86\t          - train_loss in training_step, backpropagated and logged as \"train_loss\"\n87\t          - val_loss in validation_step, logged as \"val_loss\" \u2014 the quantity\n88\t            ModelCheckpoint monitors\n89\t          - train_metrics logged during training_step (if weights is non-empty)\n90\t          - val_metrics logged during validation_step (if weights is non-empty).\n91\t            Reported only; they do not influence checkpoint selection.\n92\t\n93\t    lr:\n94\t        Learning rate for the Adam optimizer.\n95\t\n96\t    batch_size:\n97\t        Optional batch size passed to Lightning's `self.log(..., batch_size=...)`.\n98\t        This improves correct averaging behavior when using distributed settings\n99\t        or variable batch sizes.\n100\t\n101\t    preprocess_fn:\n102\t        Optional callable applied to the batch dict before every model call.\n103\t        Signature: ``(batch: dict) -> dict``. Use this to apply input\n104\t        transformations (e.g., ``destandardize_channels``) without\n105\t        embedding them in the model itself.\n106\t    \"\"\"\n107\t\n108\t    def __init__(\n109\t        self,\n110\t        model: torch.nn.Module,\n111\t        metrics: Dict[str, Callable[..., Tuple[Dict[str, torch.Tensor], Weights]]],\n112\t        lr: float,\n113\t        batch_size: Optional[int] = None,\n114\t        preprocess_fn: Optional[Callable[[Dict], Dict]] = None,\n115\t    ):\n116\t        super().__init__()\n117\t        self.batch_size = batch_size\n118\t        self.model = model\n119\t        self.preprocess_fn = preprocess_fn\n120\t\n121\t        # Loss callables: return (loss_dict, weight_list)\n122\t        self.training_loss = metrics[\"train_loss\"]\n123\t        # \"val_loss\" is optional: falling back to train_loss keeps a metrics dict written\n124\t        # before this key existed working, with identical behavior.\n125\t        self.validation_loss = metrics.get(\"val_loss\", metrics[\"train_loss\"])\n126\t\n127\t        # Metric callables: return (metric_dict, weight_list)\n128\t        self.training_evaluation = metrics[\"train_metrics\"]\n129\t        self.validation_evaluation = metrics[\"val_metrics\"]\n130\t\n131\t        self.lr = lr\n132\t\n133\t    @staticmethod\n134\t    def _combine_losses(loss_dict: LossDict, weights: Weights) -> torch.Tensor:\n135\t        \"\"\"Return a weighted sum of the losses in ``loss_dict``.\n136\t\n137\t        ``weights`` must be aligned with ``loss_dict.keys()`` iteration order.\n138\t        Raises ``ValueError`` if ``loss_dict`` is empty.\n139\t        \"\"\"\n140\t        loss = None\n141\t        for n, key in enumerate(loss_dict.keys()):\n142\t            component = loss_dict[key] * weights[n]\n143\t            loss = component if loss is None else (loss + component)\n144\t        if loss is None:\n145\t            raise ValueError(\"loss_dict is empty; cannot compute a scalar loss.\")\n146\t        return loss\n147\t\n148\t    def forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n149\t        \"\"\"\n150\t        Forward pass used by Lightning and by explicit calls in steps.\n151\t\n152\t        Parameters\n153\t        ----------\n154\t        batch:\n155\t            Batch dict (at minimum contains ``\"ts\"`` and ``\"forecast\"``).\n156\t\n157\t        Returns\n158\t        -------\n159\t        torch.Tensor\n160\t            Model predictions for the batch.\n161\t        \"\"\"\n162\t        return self.model(batch)\n163\t\n164\t    def training_step(self, batch: Dict[str, Any], batch_idx: int) -> torch.Tensor:\n165\t        \"\"\"\n166\t        Runs one training step on a single batch.\n167\t\n168\t        Workflow\n169\t        --------\n170\t        1) Extract inputs and targets from the batch:\n171\t              x = batch[\"ts\"]\n172\t              target = batch[\"forecast\"]\n173\t        2) Compute model output:\n174\t              output = self(x)\n175\t        3) Compute per-component losses and combine via provided weights:\n176\t              training_losses, training_loss_weights = training_loss(output, target)\n177\t        4) Log:\n178\t              - total weighted loss as \"train_loss\" (progress bar)\n179\t              - each component loss as \"train_loss_<name>\"\n180\t              - training metrics as \"train_metric_<name>\" (if any)\n181\t\n182\t        Notes\n183\t        -----\n184\t        - Targets are reshaped to shape [B, 1] by unsqueeze(1) to match a common\n185\t          \"single output per sample\" convention.\n186\t        - The loss combination depends on dict iteration order; ensure loss dict\n187\t          insertion order is consistent if that matters.\n188\t\n189\t        Returns\n190\t        -------\n191\t        torch.Tensor\n192\t            The scalar training loss used for backpropagation.\n193\t        \"\"\"\n194\t        target = batch[\"forecast\"].unsqueeze(1).float()\n195\t\n196\t        if self.preprocess_fn is not None:\n197\t            batch = self.preprocess_fn(batch)\n198\t        output = self(batch)\n199\t        training_losses, training_loss_weights = self.training_loss(output, target)\n200\t        loss = self._combine_losses(training_losses, training_loss_weights)\n201\t\n202\t        # Log aggregate loss and component losses.\n203\t        self.log(\"train_loss\", loss, prog_bar=True, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n204\t        for key in training_losses.keys():\n205\t            self.log(f\"train_loss_{key}\", training_losses[key], prog_bar=False, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n206\t\n207\t        # Log evaluation metrics (optional).\n208\t        training_evaluation_metrics, training_evaluation_weights = self.training_evaluation(output, target)\n209\t        if len(training_evaluation_weights) > 0:\n210\t            for key in training_evaluation_metrics.keys():\n211\t                self.log(f\"train_metric_{key}\", training_evaluation_metrics[key], prog_bar=False, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n212\t\n213\t        return loss\n214\t\n215\t    def validation_step(self, batch: Dict[str, Any], batch_idx: int) -> None:\n216\t        \"\"\"\n217\t        Runs one validation step on a single batch.\n218\t\n219\t        Workflow\n220\t        --------\n221\t        1) Extract inputs and targets\n222\t        2) Compute output\n223\t        3) Compute validation losses and combine via weights\n224\t        4) Log:\n225\t              - total weighted loss as \"val_loss\" (progress bar)\n226\t              - each component loss as \"val_loss_<name>\"\n227\t              - validation metrics as \"val_metric_<name>\" (if any)\n228\t\n229\t        Notes\n230\t        -----\n231\t        - The loss is computed with `self.validation_loss`, which comes from\n232\t          metrics[\"val_loss\"] and falls back to metrics[\"train_loss\"] when that key is\n233\t          absent. Supply a distinct \"val_loss\" callable to monitor something other than\n234\t          the training objective.\n235\t        - \"val_loss\" is what ModelCheckpoint monitors. The `val_metrics` logged at the end\n236\t          of this method are reported only and do not affect checkpoint selection.\n237\t        - No value is returned (Lightning uses logs for validation tracking).\n238\t        \"\"\"\n239\t        target = batch[\"forecast\"].unsqueeze(1).float()\n240\t\n241\t        if self.preprocess_fn is not None:\n242\t            batch = self.preprocess_fn(batch)\n243\t        output = self(batch)\n244\t        val_losses, val_loss_weights = self.validation_loss(output, target)\n245\t        loss = self._combine_losses(val_losses, val_loss_weights)\n246\t\n247\t        # Log aggregate loss and component losses.\n248\t        self.log(\"val_loss\", loss, prog_bar=True, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n249\t        for key in val_losses.keys():\n250\t            self.log(f\"val_loss_{key}\", val_losses[key], prog_bar=False, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n251\t\n252\t        # Log evaluation metrics (optional).\n253\t        val_evaluation_metrics, val_evaluation_weights = self.validation_evaluation(output, target)\n254\t        if len(val_evaluation_weights) > 0:\n255\t            for key in val_evaluation_metrics.keys():\n256\t                self.log(f\"val_metric_{key}\", val_evaluation_metrics[key], prog_bar=False, batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True)\n257\t\n258\t    def configure_optimizers(self) -> torch.optim.Optimizer:\n259\t        \"\"\"\n260\t        Configure the optimizer used by Lightning.\n261\t\n262\t        Returns\n263\t        -------\n264\t        torch.optim.Optimizer\n265\t            Adam optimizer over all module parameters with learning rate `self.lr`.\n266\t        \"\"\"\n267\t        return torch.optim.Adam(self.parameters(), lr=self.lr)\n268\t",
