            "diffs": [
              {
                "old_text": "# Fix `KeyError: 'intensity'` in the wave-classification dataset\n\n## Context\n\n`downstream_apps/test/` was forked from the flare-forecasting template, which predicts a\ncontinuous X-ray `intensity` label. You're repurposing it to classify EUV waves\n(`downstream_apps/test/data/lcpf_aia_waves.csv`, a binary `class` column with values\n`\"wave\"` / `\"no wave\"`, 914 each).\n\n`configs/config_script.yaml` already has a new key added for this:\n```yaml\nds_class_column: class #Class added wave or on wave by Diego.\n```\nbut two things weren't updated to match, and a third bug is latent further down the\nnotebook:\n\n1. **`configs.py`** (`FlareDataConfig`) has no `ds_class_column` field. Unknown YAML keys\n   raise per this repo's config rules \u2014 a fresh kernel restart will fail at the config-load\n   cell (`63c1fe60`), before even reaching cell 5.\n2. **`datasets/wave_dataset.py`** still hardcodes the column name `\"intensity\"` (lines 74,\n   76) instead of accepting `ds_class_column` and reading that. This is the direct cause of\n   the `KeyError: 'intensity'` \u2014 the wave CSV has no `intensity` column, only `class`.\n3. The label is a **string** (`\"wave\"`/`\"no wave\"`), but `__getitem__` does\n   `.astype(np.float32)` on it (line 136). Once the column name is fixed, this will raise a\n   `ValueError` casting a string to float. It needs a numeric encoding, and the existing\n   `label_transform` hook (already designed for exactly this \u2014 see its docstring) is the\n   right place: applied at the call site, not hardcoded in the dataset class.\n4. Further down the notebook, cell `70e9c5b2` still instantiates `FlareDSDataset`, a name\n   that was never imported (only `waveDSDataset` was, in cell `34db2cc6`). That cell would\n   `NameError` once you get that far \u2014 fixing it now avoids you hitting the same\n   investigation again in ten minutes.\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. `downstream_apps/test/configs.py`\nAdd the missing field to `FlareDataConfig`, next to the other `ds_*` alignment fields:\n```python\nds_class_column: str = \"\"\n```\nNo `PATH_FIELDS` change needed \u2014 it's a column name, not a path.\n\n### 2. `downstream_apps/test/datasets/wave_dataset.py`\n- Add `ds_class_column: str | None = None` to `__init__`'s signature (alongside\n  `ds_time_column`), and raise the same style of `ValueError` as `ds_flare_index_path` if\n  it's `None`.\n- Replace the two hardcoded `\"intensity\"` lookups (lines 74/76) with\n  `self.ds_index[ds_class_column]`.\n- Update the docstring: `label_transform` currently says it applies to \"the `intensity`\n  column\" \u2014 reword to \"the column named by `ds_class_column`\". Also fix the stale\n  `FlareDSDataset` name in the `Raises:` docstring (line 35) to `waveDSDataset`.\n- Leave the internal field/return names (`normalized_intensity`, `sample[\"forecast\"]`)\n  alone \u2014 renaming them is a cosmetic, cross-file change (lightning module / metrics in a\n  later notebook would need matching updates) and isn't needed to fix this bug. Happy to do\n  that separately if you want the naming to read as classification end-to-end.\n\n### 3. `downstream_apps/test/0_dataset_dataloader_template_diego.ipynb`\n- Cell `f76d377c` (`waveDSDataset(...)`, block #5): add\n  ```python\n  ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column,\n  label_transform=lambda s: (s == \"wave\").astype(np.float32),\n  ```\n  The lambda encodes `\"wave\"` \u2192 `1.0`, `\"no wave\"` \u2192 `0.0` \u2014 a binary label suitable for\n  `BCEWithLogitsLoss` later. (`np` is already imported in cell `4ed04321`.)\n- Cell `70e9c5b2` (the `return_surya_stack=True` re-init, further down): same two new\n  kwargs, and fix `FlareDSDataset` \u2192 `waveDSDataset`.\n\n## Verification\n\nIn the `jovyan-Surya_WS` conda env, restart the kernel and run all cells through the\ndataloader section:\n- Config-load cell should print `Loaded config for job: wave_classification` without an\n  unknown-key error.\n- Cell 5 (`train_dataset = waveDSDataset(...)`) should construct without `KeyError`.\n- `train_dataset.__len__()` should return a small number (\u2264 6, since\n  `max_number_of_samples=6`).\n- `train_dataset.__getitem__(0)['forecast']` should be a `np.float32` scalar (`0.0` or\n  `1.0`), not a string.\n- The `return_surya_stack=True` cell and its dataloader cells should run without\n  `NameError`.",
                "new_text": "# Two trainable baselines for wave classification\n\n## Context\n\nThe wave-classification dataset pipeline (`waveDSDataset`, label convention `wave=1.0` /\n`no wave=0.0`) is now working end-to-end. Before fine-tuning the full Surya backbone on\nthis task, you want two cheap, trainable baselines to know what \"better than nothing\"\nlooks like on the validation set:\n\n1. **Constant-probability baseline** \u2014 a single trainable scalar that learns the class\n   base rate, ignoring the image entirely. (You called this \"probability that the image is\n   a CME or not\" \u2014 read here as the wave/no-wave prior, consistent with the label\n   convention already agreed on.)\n2. **Running-difference baseline** \u2014 AIA193 at the current timestep minus AIA193 one\n   cadence step earlier, mean-pooled with a 32\u00d732 kernel (`AvgPool2d(kernel_size=32)`, i.e.\n   uniform weights `1/1024`), fed into a trainable logistic regression.\n\nBoth reuse the existing template scaffolding already forked into `test/`\n(`models/simple_baseline.py`, `lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py`,\n`metrics/template_metrics.py`) rather than building a new training loop. That scaffolding\nis currently the *unmodified regression* version (predicts continuous flare intensity via\nMSE) \u2014 it needs converting to binary classification (BCE loss, accuracy/AUROC metrics).\n`test/1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` is your own copy of the baseline notebook but is\nstill untouched (imports `downstream_apps.template.*`, builds `FlareDSDataset`,\n`RegressionFlareModel`) \u2014 this plan turns it into the runnable two-baseline notebook.\n\n**Resolved design question \u2014 the shared `time_dim` config:** Baseline 2 needs 2 input\ntimesteps, but `model.time_embedding.time_dim: 1` in `config_script.yaml` is also what\nsizes the full Surya backbone's patch embedding for the *later* fine-tuning run sharing\nthis same file. Confirmed directly in `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py:103`\nthat `build_helio_dataloaders()` merges `task_kwargs` *after* the config-derived\ndefaults (`common = {**_base_dataset_kwargs(cfg, scalers), **task_kwargs}`), so passing\n`n_input_timestamps=2` and `time_delta_input_minutes=[-12, 0]` explicitly as call-site\nkwargs overrides the config-derived values for this dataset only \u2014 `cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim`\nin the YAML stays `1`, untouched for the future Surya run. Also confirmed in\n`workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py:617-623`: the last entry of\n`time_delta_input_minutes` is always appended as the \"now\" frame, and the rest are\nsampled from `time_delta_input_minutes[:-1]`. With only one historical candidate (`-12`),\nthe sample is deterministic \u2014 `ts[:, :, 0]` = 12 minutes ago, `ts[:, :, -1]` = now. AIA\ncadence is 12 minutes (per `CLAUDE.md`), so `-12` is exactly one prior frame.\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. `test/models/simple_baseline.py` \u2014 add two model classes\n\nKeep the existing `RegressionFlareModel` / `destandardize_channels` as-is (harmless, still\nimportable). Add:\n\n```python\nclass ConstantProbabilityModel(nn.Module):\n    \"\"\"Trainable baseline: a single learned logit, independent of the input.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.logit = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(1))\n\n    def forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n        batch_size = batch[\"forecast\"].shape[0]\n        return self.logit.expand(batch_size, 1)\n\n\nclass RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(nn.Module):\n    \"\"\"Trainable baseline: AIA193 running difference (now - one cadence step earlier),\n    32x32 mean-pooled, then logistic regression.\n\n    Expects batch[\"ts\"] with shape (B, C, T=2, H, W), T ordered [t_prev, t_now] (see\n    HelioNetCDFDataset.__getitem__ sampling \u2014 the last T index is always \"now\").\n    Call with signum-log-space input (destandardize_channels), matching the convention\n    RegressionFlareModel already uses.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, channel_index: int, img_size: int, pool_kernel: int = 32):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.channel_index = channel_index\n        self.pool = nn.AvgPool2d(kernel_size=pool_kernel)\n        pooled_side = img_size // pool_kernel\n        self.linear = nn.Linear(pooled_side * pooled_side, 1)\n\n    def forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n        x = batch[\"ts\"][:, self.channel_index, :, :, :]  # (B, T, H, W)\n        diff = x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]                          # now - prev\n        pooled = self.pool(diff.unsqueeze(1))              # (B, 1, H', W')\n        return self.linear(pooled.flatten(start_dim=1))\n```\n\n`img_size` is derived from the actual batch shape in the notebook (see below), not\nhardcoded from `cfg.model.img_size` \u2014 keeps the model correct even if the dataset ever\ncrops/resizes.\n\n### 2. `test/metrics/template_metrics.py` \u2014 convert `FlareMetrics` to classification\n\nReplace the MSE/RRSE bodies with binary-classification equivalents, keeping the same\nfour-mode structure (`train_loss`, `val_loss`, `train_metrics`, `val_metrics`) and the\nsame `(dict, weights)` return contract \u2014 `FlareLightningModule` needs no changes at all,\nit's already metric-agnostic:\n\n- `train_loss` / `val_loss`: `torch.nn.functional.binary_cross_entropy_with_logits(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))`, keyed `\"bce\"`.\n- `train_metrics` / `val_metrics`: `torchmetrics.classification.BinaryAccuracy()` and\n  `BinaryAUROC()` (cached on `self` like the existing `self._rrse`, moved to device via\n  the same `_ensure_device` pattern). Both accept raw logits directly \u2014 torchmetrics'\n  binary metrics auto-apply sigmoid when inputs fall outside `[0, 1]`, so no explicit\n  `.sigmoid()` call is needed and it stays consistent with what `BCEWithLogitsLoss` sees.\n\n### 3. `test/1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` \u2014 rewrite as the two-baseline notebook\n\nFix the stale bits and swap in the two new baselines:\n\n- Fix imports: `downstream_apps.template.*` \u2192 `downstream_apps.test.*` throughout (config,\n  dataset, model, lightning module, metrics).\n- Dataset import: `waveDSDataset` (not `FlareDSDataset`).\n- Single shared dataloader build (both baselines reuse it):\n  ```python\n  train_data_loader, val_data_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\n      cfg, waveDSDataset, scalers=scalers, num_workers=4,\n      return_surya_stack=True,\n      max_number_of_samples=cfg.data.max_samples,\n      time_delta_input_minutes=[-12, 0],   # override: 2 timesteps for this baseline notebook only\n      n_input_timestamps=2,                 # cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim stays 1 in the YAML\n      ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column,\n      label_transform=lambda s: (s == \"wave\").astype(np.float32),\n      ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path,\n      ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n      ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n      ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n  )\n  ```\n- Pull one batch to confirm shapes (same pattern as the existing cell), then:\n  ```python\n  n_channels = len(cfg.data.channels)\n  aia193_index = cfg.data.channels.index(\"aia193\")\n  img_size = batch[\"ts\"].shape[-1]\n  ```\n- Baseline 1: `ConstantProbabilityModel()`, no `preprocess_fn` needed (it never touches\n  `ts`).\n- Baseline 2: `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(channel_index=aia193_index, img_size=img_size)`,\n  with `preprocess_fn=partial(destandardize_channels, channel_order=cfg.data.channels, scalers=scalers)`\n  \u2014 same log-space convention `RegressionFlareModel` already uses.\n- Metrics: one shared `metrics` dict per baseline built from the new classification\n  `FlareMetrics` (same four-mode wiring as today).\n- Two separate `FlareLightningModule` + `Trainer` instances, one per baseline, each\n  `trainer.fit(...)` on the shared `train_data_loader`/`val_data_loader`, with distinct\n  WandB run names (e.g. `baseline_constant_prob`, `baseline_running_diff`) so validation\n  metrics are comparable side by side.\n\n## Verification\n\nIn the `jovyan-Surya_WS` kernel, run the notebook cells in order (one-by-one via the\nJupyter MCP tools, checking output after each \u2014 `run_all_cells` raced against kernel\nrestart last time):\n\n- Dataloader cell: confirm `batch[\"ts\"].shape` has `T=2` (was `T=1` before).\n- `ConstantProbabilityModel`: forward pass returns a `(B, 1)` tensor equal to the same\n  scalar for every row before training.\n- `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`: forward pass runs without shape errors; `pooled_side`\n  matches `img_size // 32`.\n- `FlareMetrics(\"train_loss\")(output, target)` returns a finite BCE scalar; `(\"train_metrics\")`\n  returns `accuracy` in `[0, 1]` and `auroc` in `[0, 1]`.\n- `trainer.fit(...)` completes `max_epochs` for both baselines without error; `val_loss`\n  (BCE) is logged and decreases at least somewhat from a randomly-initialized starting\n  point for one of the two baselines over a couple epochs on the small (`max_samples`)\n  dataset.",
                "path": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/i-m-running-a-ipynb-melodic-seal.md"
              }
            ],
