      "body": "`normalized_intensity` is the internal column name in `wave_dataset.py:79-81` \u2014 it's what `label_transform` writes into, and what `__getitem__` reads into `sample[\"forecast\"]`. In the original flare-regression template it literally held a normalized (log10, mean-centered, std-scaled) flare intensity \u2014 a continuous number. In your wave-classification fork, `label_transform` is now `lambda s: (s == \"wave\").astype(np.float32)` (set in the notebook's dataloader-build call), so that same column now holds a binary `0.0`/`1.0` wave/no-wave label \u2014 it's neither \"normalized\" nor an \"intensity\" anymore. Purely a leftover name from the copy.\n\nSince you want full cleanup except `\"forecast\"`, I'd rename this internal column too (it never touches the external `batch[\"forecast\"]` key, just the two lines inside `wave_dataset.py` that produce it) \u2014 to something like `label`. Let me check how far \"flare\" spreads into the config/YAML layer before finalizing the plan.",
