    {
      "body": "That's the notebook's \"Logging\" section \u2014 markdown cell `7b9c76c8` right before `make_loggers()` (cell `48c4d138`), where `WandbLogger(entity=cfg.wandb_entity, project=project_name, ...)` actually gets constructed. Since you already have a WandB account, here's what to do:\n\n**1. Get an API key**\nGo to https://wandb.ai/authorize (while logged into your account) and copy the API key shown there.\n\n**2. Authenticate this machine/kernel**\nEither:\n- Run `wandb login` in a terminal on this machine and paste the key when prompted, **or**\n- Just run the `make_loggers`/`WandbLogger(...)` cell as-is \u2014 the first time it runs, WandB will print an interactive prompt (in the notebook output or terminal running the kernel) asking you to choose:\n  - `(1)` Create a W&B account\n  - `(2)` Use an existing W&B account \u2190 pick this\n  - `(3)` Log in anonymously\n  \n  Then paste the API key from step 1. This matches exactly what the markdown cell above it already describes.\n\nAlternative, non-interactive way (useful if the prompt doesn't render well in your notebook UI): set the env var before that cell runs:\n```python\nimport os\nos.environ[\"WANDB_API_KEY\"] = \"<your key>\"\n```\n\n**3. Check `entity`/`project` in the config**\nIn `configs/config_script.yaml`:\n```yaml\nlogging:\n  wandb_project: wave_classification\n  wandb_entity: null      # null = log under your own personal account\n```\n- If you were invited to a **team/org** project, set `wandb_entity` to that team's slug (visible in the URL of the team's WandB page, e.g. `wandb.ai/<entity>/...`) \u2014 otherwise the run logs under your personal account instead of the shared team space.\n- `wandb_project` just needs to be a project name you (or the team) will recognize; WandB creates it automatically on first log if it doesn't exist.\n\nOnce login succeeds once, credentials are cached locally (`~/.netrc` or similar), so you won't be prompted again on subsequent runs in this environment.",
