            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_014ErMhJmKopkNqGASf9picp",
            "raw_output": "{\"result\":[{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"# Two trainable baselines for wave classification\\n\\nby Andr\u00e9s Mu\u00f1oz-Jaramillo (template), adapted for wave classification by Diego\\n\\nThis notebook defines two simple, trainable baselines for the EUV wave classification\\ntask, to be evaluated on the validation set before fine-tuning the full Surya backbone:\\n\\n1. **Constant-probability baseline** \u2014 a single learned scalar that predicts the class\\n   base rate (wave vs. no wave), ignoring the image entirely.\\n2. **Running-difference baseline** \u2014 AIA193(now) - AIA193(one cadence step earlier),\\n   mean-pooled 32x32, then a logistic regression.\\n\\nIt focuses on the concept of defining a PyTorch model, a PyTorch lightning training loop\\nand the definition of metrics of performance.\\n\\nThis notebook assumes familiarity with the concepts of datasets and dataloaders contained\\nin **_0_dataset_dataloader_template_diego.ipynb_**\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"d651fb69\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Set your cuda visible device\\n\\n**IMPORTANT:** Since we are sharing resources, please make sure that the cuda visible device you put here is the one assigned to your team and your machine.   \",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"f6bade9c\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"import os\\nos.environ[\\\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\\\"] = \\\"0\\\"\",\"execution_count\":30,\"cell_id\":\"6d6af87a\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# Must be set BEFORE torch is imported: cuBLAS reads this once, when it initializes, so\\n# setting it later has no effect. It is what lets training.deterministic work without a\\n# cuBLAS warning on every run. (Restart the kernel if torch was already imported.)\\nimport os\\nos.environ.setdefault(\\\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\\\", \\\":4096:8\\\")\\n\\nimport sys\\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\\n\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport torch\\nimport yaml\\n\\nimport lightning as L\\nfrom lightning.pytorch.callbacks import ModelCheckpoint\\nfrom lightning.pytorch.loggers import CSVLogger, WandbLogger\\n\\n# Append base path.  May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\\n# It gives the notebook access to the wokshop_infrastructure folder.\\nsys.path.append(\\\"../../\\\")\\n \\n# Append Surya path. May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\\n# It gives the notebook access to surya's release code.\\n\\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers  # Data scaling utilities for Surya stacks\\n\\ntorch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')\\n\",\"execution_count\":31,\"cell_id\":\"4ed04321\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Load configuration\\n\\nSurya was designed to read a configuration file that defines many aspects of the model\\nincluding the data it uses we use this config file to set default values that do not\\nneed to be modified, but also to define values specific to our downstream application\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"04135ff7\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# The config is the single source of truth. load_wave_config() parses it into a typed\\n# object, exactly as the training script 3_finetune_template_1D.py does, so the same YAML\\n# behaves identically here and in production. Notebook 0 walks through what it contains.\\nfrom downstream_apps.test.configs import load_wave_config\\n\\ncfg = load_wave_config(\\\"./configs/config_script.yaml\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Loaded config for job: {cfg.job_id}\\\")\\n\",\"execution_count\":32,\"cell_id\":\"63c1fe60\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"error\",\"text\":\"ImportError: cannot import name 'load_wave_config' from 'downstream_apps.test.configs' (/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/../../downstream_apps/test/configs.py)\\n\\u001b[0;31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[0;31mImportError\\u001b[0m                               Traceback (most recent call last)\\nCell \\u001b[0;32mIn[32], line 4\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[1;32m      1\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# The config is the single source of truth. load_wave_config() parses it into a typed\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m      2\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# object, exactly as the training script 3_finetune_template_1D.py does, so the same YAML\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m      3\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# behaves identically here and in production. Notebook 0 walks through what it contains.\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[0;32m----> 4\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mfrom\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;250m \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;21;01mdownstream_apps\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01m.\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01mtest\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01m.\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01mconfigs\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;250m \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;28;01mimport\\u001b[39;00m load_wave_config\\n\\u001b[1;32m      6\\u001b[0m cfg \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m load_wave_config(\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m./configs/config_script.yaml\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m)\\n\\u001b[1;32m      7\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28mprint\\u001b[39m(\\u001b[38;5;124mf\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124mLoaded config for job: \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;132;01m{\\u001b[39;00mcfg\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mjob_id\\u001b[38;5;132;01m}\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m)\\n\\n\\u001b[0;31mImportError\\u001b[0m: cannot import name 'load_wave_config' from 'downstream_apps.test.configs' (/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/../../downstream_apps/test/configs.py)\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Download assets\\n\\nThe config says where the assets belong, so it is loaded first. `ensure_assets()` fetches only what is missing from HuggingFace, so re-running this is free.\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"29d61848\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# One implementation, shared by the notebooks, the training script and the\\n# download_*.sh wrappers: workshop_infrastructure/assets.py.\\n# The linear baseline needs no backbone, so skip the 1.8 GB weights.\\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.assets import ensure_assets\\n\\nensure_assets(cfg, which=[\\\"scalers\\\"])\\n\\n# Now that scalers.yaml is guaranteed to be on disk, load it. build_scalers()\\n# accepts the resolved path directly.\\nscalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\\nprint(f\\\"Loaded scalers for {len(scalers)} channels.\\\")\\n\",\"execution_count\":33,\"cell_id\":\"a1ca2193\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"Loaded scalers for 13 channels.\\n\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define Downstream (DS) dataset\\n\\nThis child class (`waveDSDataset`) takes as input all expected HelioFM parameters, plus\\nadditional parameters relevant to the downstream application. Here we focus in particular\\non the DS index (the wave/no-wave catalog) and the parameters necessary to combine it with\\nthe HelioFM index.\\n\\nThe label is the binary `class` column (`\\\"wave\\\"` / `\\\"no wave\\\"`), encoded to `1.0` / `0.0`\\nvia `label_transform` at the call site.\\n\\nWe use both a training and a validation dataset using the indices pointed at in the\\nconfig. `max_number_of_samples` is driven by `cfg.data.max_samples` (currently a small cap\\nfor quick experiments \u2014 see the config's `Development` section).\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"af6574dc\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset\",\"execution_count\":34,\"cell_id\":\"34db2cc6\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# build_helio_dataloaders() constructs the train and validation datasets and wraps them\\n# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\\n# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\\n# like written out. Only the wave-specific arguments are passed here.\\n#\\n# time_delta_input_minutes / n_input_timestamps are overridden here to 2 timesteps\\n# (t_prev, t_now) for the running-difference baseline below. task_kwargs override the\\n# config-derived defaults inside build_helio_dataloaders(), so this only affects this\\n# dataset build \u2014 cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim stays 1 in the YAML, unaffected, for\\n# the later full-Surya fine-tuning run that shares this same config file.\\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_dataloaders\\n\\ntrain_data_loader, val_data_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\\n    cfg,\\n    waveDSDataset,\\n    scalers=scalers,\\n    num_workers=4,          # fewer workers than the script: notebooks start faster\\n    #### Downstream (DS) specific parameters\\n    return_surya_stack=True,\\n    max_number_of_samples=cfg.data.max_samples,\\n    time_delta_input_minutes=[-12, 0],   # t_prev (one AIA cadence step earlier), t_now\\n    n_input_timestamps=2,\\n    ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column,\\n    label_transform=lambda s: (s == \\\"wave\\\").astype(np.float32),\\n    ds_wave_index_path=cfg.data.wave_index_path,\\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\\n)\\n\\nbatch_size = cfg.batch_size\\nprint(f\\\"train: {len(train_data_loader.dataset)} samples | \\\"\\n      f\\\"val: {len(val_data_loader.dataset)} samples | batch_size: {batch_size}\\\")\\n\",\"execution_count\":35,\"cell_id\":\"70e9c5b2\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"error\",\"text\":\"AttributeError: 'FlareDataConfig' object has no attribute 'wave_index_path'\\n\\u001b[0;31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[0;31mAttributeError\\u001b[0m                            Traceback (most recent call last)\\nCell \\u001b[0;32mIn[35], line 25\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[1;32m      1\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# build_helio_dataloaders() constructs the train and validation datasets and wraps them\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m      2\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m      3\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[0;32m   (...)\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[1;32m      9\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# dataset build \u2014 cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim stays 1 in the YAML, unaffected, for\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     10\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# the later full-Surya fine-tuning run that shares this same config file.\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     11\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mfrom\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;250m \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;21;01mworkshop_infrastructure\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01m.\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01mdatasets\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01m.\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;21;01mbuilders\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;250m \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;28;01mimport\\u001b[39;00m build_helio_dataloaders\\n\\u001b[1;32m     13\\u001b[0m train_data_loader, val_data_loader \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m build_helio_dataloaders(\\n\\u001b[1;32m     14\\u001b[0m     cfg,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     15\\u001b[0m     waveDSDataset,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     16\\u001b[0m     scalers\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39mscalers,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     17\\u001b[0m     num_workers\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;241m4\\u001b[39m,          \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# fewer workers than the script: notebooks start faster\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     18\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;66;03m#### Downstream (DS) specific parameters\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     19\\u001b[0m     return_surya_stack\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;28;01mTrue\\u001b[39;00m,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     20\\u001b[0m     max_number_of_samples\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39mcfg\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mdata\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mmax_samples,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     21\\u001b[0m     time_delta_input_minutes\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m[\\u001b[38;5;241m-\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;241m12\\u001b[39m, \\u001b[38;5;241m0\\u001b[39m],   \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# t_prev (one AIA cadence step earlier), t_now\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     22\\u001b[0m     n_input_timestamps\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;241m2\\u001b[39m,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     23\\u001b[0m     ds_class_column\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39mcfg\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mdata\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mds_class_column,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     24\\u001b[0m     label_transform\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;28;01mlambda\\u001b[39;00m s: (s \\u001b[38;5;241m==\\u001b[39m \\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124mwave\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m)\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mastype(np\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mfloat32),\\n\\u001b[0;32m---> 25\\u001b[0m     ds_wave_index_path\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m\\u001b[43mcfg\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mdata\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mwave_index_path\\u001b[49m,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     26\\u001b[0m     ds_time_column\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39mcfg\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mdata\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mds_time_column,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     27\\u001b[0m     ds_time_tolerance\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39mcfg\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mdata\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mds_time_tolerance,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     28\\u001b[0m     ds_match_direction\\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39mcfg\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mdata\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mds_match_direction,\\n\\u001b[1;32m     29\\u001b[0m )\\n\\u001b[1;32m     31\\u001b[0m batch_size \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m cfg\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mbatch_size\\n\\u001b[1;32m     32\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28mprint\\u001b[39m(\\u001b[38;5;124mf\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124mtrain: \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;132;01m{\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;28mlen\\u001b[39m(train_data_loader\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mdataset)\\u001b[38;5;132;01m}\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;124m samples | \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     33\\u001b[0m       \\u001b[38;5;124mf\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124mval: \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;132;01m{\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;28mlen\\u001b[39m(val_data_loader\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mdataset)\\u001b[38;5;132;01m}\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;124m samples | batch_size: \\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;132;01m{\\u001b[39;00mbatch_size\\u001b[38;5;132;01m}\\u001b[39;00m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m)\\n\\n\\u001b[0;31mAttributeError\\u001b[0m: 'FlareDataConfig' object has no attribute 'wave_index_path'\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Training and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the index they read and in `phase`: `phase=\\\"val\\\"` turns off the random channel masking and vertical flips used for training augmentation.\\n\\nThe loaders use `multiprocessing_context=\\\"spawn\\\"` \u2014 the dataset holds an S3 client that does not survive `fork`, and spawn also avoids lockups in shared environments.\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"79fda7cf\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"train_data_loader.dataset.__getitem__(1)\",\"execution_count\":7,\"cell_id\":\"a930ff19-8884-4aac-bbaa-1cf4b8cfc14f\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"6a984b10-1fc6-4906-a3e6-ddf303885d2e\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# Inspect a single batch to confirm shapes before training.\\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\\nprint({k: (tuple(v.shape) if hasattr(v, \\\"shape\\\") else type(v).__name__) for k, v in batch.items()})\\n\\n# Derive model dimensions directly from the batch/config rather than hardcoding them.\\nn_channels = len(cfg.data.channels)\\naia193_index = cfg.data.channels.index(\\\"aia193\\\")\\nimg_size = batch[\\\"ts\\\"].shape[-1]\\nprint(f\\\"n_channels={n_channels} | aia193_index={aia193_index} | img_size={img_size}\\\")\\n\",\"execution_count\":8,\"cell_id\":\"48bb2912\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define simple baseline models\\n\\nDefining a simple baseline is important to understand what value is bringing the AI model\\nto the problem.\\n\\nIt is always very good to have a very simple baseline model. Ideally one that cannot\\noverfit the data. This is a very good way of really measuring the value added of complex\\nmodels. Classical machine learning excels here:\\n\\n- Regressions and logistic regressions.\\n- Climatological averages.\\n- Persistance.\\n- Simple transformations.\\n\\nSimple models avoid excesively optimistic assessments of the capatiblities of complex\\nmodels and for many problems are actually remarkably hard to beat.\\n\\nHere we define two such baselines:\\n\\n1. `ConstantProbabilityModel` \u2014 a single trainable scalar (logit) that ignores the input\\n   entirely and learns the wave/no-wave base rate.\\n2. `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` \u2014 AIA193(now) - AIA193(one cadence step earlier),\\n   mean-pooled with a 32x32 kernel, then a linear layer (logistic regression via\\n   `BCEWithLogitsLoss`).\\n\\nAs with the dataset, we import both from a module so they can be reused in training\\nscripts later on.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"1d4d2dea\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.test.models.simple_baseline import (\\n    ConstantProbabilityModel,\\n    RunningDifferenceLogisticModel,\\n    destandardize_channels,\\n)\",\"execution_count\":9,\"cell_id\":\"3f5fd639\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"We can now test that both models manipulate a batch as expected and return a wave\\nprobability logit.\\n\\n`ConstantProbabilityModel` needs no configuration \u2014 it ignores the image. `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`\\nneeds the AIA193 channel index and the frame size, both pulled directly from the batch/config\\nabove.\\n\\nNote that since neither model has been trained yet and was initialized randomly, the\\noutput here has no real meaning. It only acts as a test that the forward pass doesn't have\\ndimension problems \u2014 dimension problems are the dominant source of error in this kind of\\nwork.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"18d87992\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# Baseline 1: a single learned logit, independent of the input.\\nmodel_constant = ConstantProbabilityModel()\\n\\n# Baseline 2: AIA193 running difference, 32x32 mean-pooled, then logistic regression.\\n# It expects 'ts' in signum-log space \u2014 use destandardize_channels() before calling forward().\\nmodel_running_diff = RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(channel_index=aia193_index, img_size=img_size)\\n\",\"execution_count\":10,\"cell_id\":\"b0b6ce02\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Now we can pass a batch to each model to confirm it returns one logit per sample. Note\\nthat our outputs have the size of our batch.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3fee1e68\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"batch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\\n\\n# RunningDifferenceLogisticModel works in signum-log space, not normalized space.\\n# destandardize_channels undoes the per-channel z-score but KEEPS the log compression \u2014\\n# raw DN values span too many orders of magnitude to be good features for one linear layer.\\n# (For true physical units, use train_dataset.inverse_transform_data() instead. See the\\n#  \\\"THE THREE SPACES\\\" block in workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py.)\\nbatch_logspace = destandardize_channels(batch, channel_order=cfg.data.channels, scalers=scalers)\\n\\noutput_constant = model_constant.forward(batch)[:, 0]              # ignores 'ts' entirely\\noutput_running_diff = model_running_diff.forward(batch_logspace)[:, 0]\\n\\nprint(\\\"constant baseline output (logits):\\\", output_constant)\\nprint(\\\"running-difference baseline output (logits):\\\", output_running_diff)\\n\",\"execution_count\":11,\"cell_id\":\"d7d97841\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define your metrics\\n\\nMetrics are a very important part of training AI models. They provide your models with\\nthe quantitification of error, which in turn shifts the weights towards better\\npefrorming models. They also provide a way for you to monitor performance, identify\\noverfitting, and quantify value added.\\n\\nWe now initialize the metrics class which allows you to control what metrics do you want\\nto use as \\\"loss\\\" (i.e. the metrics that backpropagate through your model) and which ones\\nfor monitoring performance. `WaveMetrics` has been converted for this task to binary\\nclassification: BCE-with-logits for the loss, accuracy + AUROC for reporting. As with\\nother components, this takes the form of a loaded module that can be later used in a\\ntraining script.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"36f32e64\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.test.metrics.template_metrics import WaveMetrics\",\"execution_count\":12,\"cell_id\":\"34c502ec\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"def make_metrics() -> dict:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Build a fresh dict of WaveMetrics instances.\\n\\n    Each of the two baselines below needs its own dict: the accuracy/AUROC torchmetrics\\n    objects inside WaveMetrics accumulate internal state across calls, so sharing one\\n    instance between two independently-trained models would mix their statistics.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return {\\n        'train_loss': WaveMetrics(\\\"train_loss\\\"),\\n        # val_loss is the quantity logged as \\\"val_loss\\\" and used to pick the best checkpoint.\\n        # It defaults to the same BCE as train_loss \u2014 override WaveMetrics.val_loss to change it.\\n        'val_loss': WaveMetrics(\\\"val_loss\\\"),\\n        'train_metrics': WaveMetrics(\\\"train_metrics\\\"),\\n        # Reported only: val_metrics do NOT influence checkpoint selection.\\n        'val_metrics': WaveMetrics(\\\"val_metrics\\\"),\\n    }\\n\\ndemo_metrics = make_metrics()\",\"execution_count\":13,\"cell_id\":\"d152b127\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Now they can be evaluated on a model's output and our ground truth. First the loss that\\nactually will backpropagate \u2014 binary cross-entropy with logits \u2014 demonstrated here on the\\nrunning-difference baseline's output.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"0550062c\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"demo_metrics[\\\"train_loss\\\"](output_running_diff, batch[\\\"forecast\\\"])\",\"execution_count\":14,\"cell_id\":\"2baf9d36\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Then a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we\\ncan keep an eye on. Note that reporting lots of metrics during training will slow the\\ntraining process. I'm including it here as an example, but oftentimes it is better to put\\nthe diagnostics only in the validation evaluation metrics.\\n\\nHere we are calculating accuracy (fraction correctly classified at a 0.5 probability\\nthreshold). Since torchmetrics' binary metrics auto-apply sigmoid to logit inputs, this\\nworks directly on the raw model output.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3dc5eb6d\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"demo_metrics[\\\"train_metrics\\\"](output_running_diff, batch[\\\"forecast\\\"])\",\"execution_count\":15,\"cell_id\":\"796559f1\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"In the validation evaluation metrics we report both accuracy and AUROC.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3d65e7cd\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"demo_metrics[\\\"val_metrics\\\"](output_running_diff, batch[\\\"forecast\\\"])\",\"execution_count\":16,\"cell_id\":\"0a0bbb35\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define your PyTorch ligthning module\\n\\nIn this workshop we will use PyTorch lightning to train our models.  PyTorch lighting reduces the amount of code required to implement a training loop in comparison to PyTorch (at the expense of control and versatility).  \\n\\nOpening the WaveLightningModule shows a simple Lightning model implementation.  It consists of:\\n\\n- An initialization of the class (metrics, model, and learning rate).\\n- The forward code that runs evaluation of the model.\\n- Training and validation steps.\\n- Configuration of optimizers.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"537871ed\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.test.lightning_modules.pl_simple_baseline import WaveLightningModule\",\"execution_count\":17,\"cell_id\":\"9d730cf3\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Set your global seeds\\n\\nSince training AI models generally uses stochastic gradient descent, it is a good idea to fix your random seeds so that your training exercise is reproducible.    \",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3321963d\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"L.seed_everything(42, workers=True)\",\"execution_count\":18,\"cell_id\":\"cc28903d\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Intialize Lightning modules\\n\\nNow we properly initialize one Lightning module per baseline, each with its own metrics\\ndict (see `make_metrics()` above) so their statistics don't mix.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"119ec413\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from functools import partial\\n\\n# Baseline 1: constant probability \u2014 never touches 'ts', so no preprocess_fn needed.\\nlit_model_constant = WaveLightningModule(\\n    model_constant, make_metrics(), lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size,\\n)\\n\\n# Baseline 2: running difference \u2014 needs the signum-log-space inverse transform wired up\\n# so WaveLightningModule applies it before every model call.\\npreprocess_fn = partial(\\n    destandardize_channels,\\n    channel_order=cfg.data.channels,\\n    scalers=scalers,\\n)\\nlit_model_running_diff = WaveLightningModule(\\n    model_running_diff, make_metrics(), lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size,\\n    preprocess_fn=preprocess_fn,\\n)\\n\",\"execution_count\":19,\"cell_id\":\"553565e7\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Logging\\n\\nIn order to properly compare experiments against each other, it is very useful to log evaluation metrics in a place where they can be compared against other training runs.  In this workshop we will use Weights and Biases (WandB). \\n\\nThe first time you run WandB in a machine it will ask you to login to WandB.  You should have received an invitation to our project.  In order to login you must:\\n\\n- Select option 2 (existing account).   In VScode the dialog opens a box at the top of your screen.\\n- Click on get API Key (this will open a browser).\\n- Generate API Key.\\n- Paste it in the dialog box at the top of your VSCode\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"7b9c76c8\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"import os\\nos.environ[\\\"WANDB_API_KEY\\\"] = \\\"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\\\"\",\"execution_count\":26,\"cell_id\":\"3cc51047-bc27-4aca-ba8e-36041e33ff6e\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"8d394e34-56a7-468d-b621-6a16b939c93e\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"project_name = cfg.wandb_project\\n\\ndef make_loggers(run_name: str) -> list:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Build a fresh WandB + CSV logger pair for one baseline's run.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    wandb_logger = WandbLogger(\\n        entity=cfg.wandb_entity,  # set wandb_entity in the config; null = personal account\\n        project=project_name,\\n        name=run_name,\\n        log_model=False,\\n        save_dir=\\\"./wandb/wandb_tmp\\\",\\n    )\\n    csv_logger = CSVLogger(\\\"runs\\\", name=f\\\"{project_name}_{run_name}\\\")\\n    return [wandb_logger, csv_logger]\\n\",\"execution_count\":27,\"cell_id\":\"48c4d138\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Initialize trainer\\n\\nWith the loggers done, now the trainer needs to be defined.  The trainer defines several properties of your training run. Here we define:\\n\\n- The max number of epochs (one epoch represents your model seeing your entire training dataset).\\n- Define where the training run will take place (auto uses the GPU if possible, if not, CPU).\\n- The loggers.\\n- The callbacks (here we save the model with the lowest validation loss).\\n- Logging frequency (because we are working with a small dataset it needs to be small).\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"7e24e547\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"max_epochs = 2\\n\\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Trainer \u2014 one per baseline, so each gets its own checkpoint/logger state and their\\n# validation metrics are directly comparable side by side.\\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\\ntrainer_constant = L.Trainer(\\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\\n    accelerator=\\\"auto\\\",\\n    devices=\\\"auto\\\",\\n    logger=make_loggers(\\\"baseline_constant_prob\\\"),\\n    callbacks=[ModelCheckpoint(monitor=\\\"val_loss\\\", mode=\\\"min\\\", save_top_k=1)],\\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\\n)\\n\\ntrainer_running_diff = L.Trainer(\\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\\n    accelerator=\\\"auto\\\",\\n    devices=\\\"auto\\\",\\n    logger=make_loggers(\\\"baseline_running_diff\\\"),\\n    callbacks=[ModelCheckpoint(monitor=\\\"val_loss\\\", mode=\\\"min\\\", save_top_k=1)],\\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\\n)\\n\",\"execution_count\":28,\"cell_id\":\"b008c604\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Fit the models\\n\\nFinally we fit each baseline. We pass its Lightning module, and the shared dataloaders\\n(both baselines see the same train/val split, so their validation performance is directly\\ncomparable).\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"1088d108\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"trainer_constant.fit(lit_model_constant, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\\ntrainer_running_diff.fit(lit_model_running_diff, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\\n\",\"execution_count\":29,\"cell_id\":\"f8a6e448\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Conclusion\\n\\nWe now have two trainable baselines \u2014 constant probability and AIA193 running difference\\n\u2014 trained and evaluated on the same train/val split. Compare their `val_loss` (BCE),\\n`val_metric_accuracy` and `val_metric_auroc` (in WandB or `runs/`) to see whether the\\nrunning-difference feature adds anything over just learning the class prior. The next step\\nis to substitute these with Surya and see whether it can outperform both.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"ebcc1a23\"}]}",
            "title": "mcp__Jupyter_MCP_Server__read_notebook_cells",
            "kind": "other"
          }
        ]
      },
      "id": "554ecccf-9040-47da-a84d-989b77795829",
