    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "ddff7b80-54e6-4901-bbab-3f0aa5e89730",
      "body": "I found it. There are actually two related errors here, and they're both symptoms of the same root cause \u2014 let me show you.\n\n**Error 1** (execution 32, cell loading the config):\n```\nImportError: cannot import name 'load_wave_config' from 'downstream_apps.test.configs'\n```\n\n**Error 2** (execution 35, building the dataloaders):\n```\nAttributeError: 'FlareDataConfig' object has no attribute 'wave_index_path'\n```\n\nNotice error 2 mentions `FlareDataConfig` \u2014 the *old* class name we renamed away last session. That's the tell: this kernel has been alive since before we did the rename, so `sys.modules['downstream_apps.test.configs']` still holds the **old, pre-rename module object** in memory. `import` in Python only re-reads a module from disk the first time; if it's already in `sys.modules`, later `from X import Y` statements just look up `Y` on the cached (stale) object \u2014 which is why `load_wave_config` isn't found there even though it's on disk.\n\nAnd error 2 confirms this further: `cfg` still holds a `FlareDataConfig` instance left over from an *earlier successful run in this same kernel*, before error 1 ever happened \u2014 cell 32 failed to reassign `cfg`, so the leftover stale global from the previous (pre-rename) run got used instead.\n\n**The fix is not a code change** \u2014 the code on disk is already correct. This is stale kernel state. Let me restart the kernel and re-run from a clean process.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789502682.0991645,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": []
    },
