    {
      "time": 1789506293.2240856,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Understood \u2014 stopping the polling. For the API key, the safe way to do this is to **not** paste it into the notebook or into our chat. Options, best first:\n\n1. **`wandb login` in a terminal** (not in the notebook): open a terminal in Jupyter and run\n   ```bash\n   wandb login\n   ```\n   It'll prompt for the key interactively and store it in `~/.netrc`. Every `WandbLogger()` call in any notebook/kernel then picks it up automatically \u2014 nothing to edit in the notebook itself, and the key never touches a file that could get committed.\n\n2. **Env var, set outside the notebook** \u2014 e.g. `export WANDB_API_KEY=...` in your shell profile before launching Jupyter, or in a `.env` file that's gitignored. Same effect, no code change needed.\n\n3. If you'd rather do it from inside the notebook, add a cell with `wandb.login(key=\"...\")` **yourself** (I'd avoid typing your key into a cell for you, since notebooks are easy to accidentally commit \u2014 and this one is currently untracked in git, `?? 1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb`, so it's one `git add` away from getting picked up).\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: earlier `WANDB_MODE=offline` hung indefinitely (unresponsive to interrupt), while `WANDB_MODE=disabled` did not \u2014 which suggests this environment may not have outbound network access at all. If that's the case, live WandB logging will hang the same way regardless of the key. Do you want to try `wandb login` and see if it actually reaches WandB's servers, or should I first double check whether this machine has network egress at all before you spend time on it?",
      "id": "9f561b3d-2c3a-4ca7-b130-5a013b9edcd9",
      "mentions": [],
      "type": "msg"
    },
