            "raw_input": {
              "content": "# Fix wandb 403 error on the second baseline's `trainer.fit(...)`\n\n## Context\n\nThe two baselines in `1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` now train successfully \u2014 the\n\"flare\" rename is done, WandB is authenticated (`wandb login` stored real credentials in\n`~/.netrc` for `lloverasdiego` / `surya-ws2`), `WANDB_MODE` is back to normal (live)\nlogging, and the `num_workers=0` fix resolved an earlier DataLoader-worker crash. With all\nof that in place, `trainer_constant.fit(...)` (cell `f8a6e448`) completes cleanly and logs\na full WandB run (`wave_classification/j4lxpyb9`, checkpoints on disk).\n\nThe next cell, `trainer_running_diff.fit(...)` (cell `5dd75610-49f1-4810-b2b6-5115164bd2ca`),\nnow fails with:\n\n```\nCommError: Error uploading run: returned error 403: {\"data\":{\"upsertBucket\":null},\n\"errors\":[{\"message\":\"the provided API key cannot access this resource, check\ncredentials\",\"path\":[\"upsertBucket\"]}]}\n```\n\nThe two lines the user quoted (`WARNING The anonymous setting has no effect...` and\n`ERROR Failed to detect the name of this notebook...`) are **harmless, unrelated noise**,\nnot the actual failure:\n- \"anonymous setting has no effect\" \u2014 every `WandbLogger(...)` call passes\n  `anonymous=None` internally (`lightning/pytorch/loggers/wandb.py:345`); wandb warns this\n  is a no-op whenever you're already logged in with real credentials. Cosmetic.\n- \"Failed to detect the name of this notebook\" \u2014 wandb can't infer the `.ipynb` filename\n  under this Jupyter setup, so it skips a \"code saving\" convenience feature. Cosmetic;\n  fixable with `WANDB_NOTEBOOK_NAME` if ever wanted, but not required.\n\nThe real cause of the 403 is the line just above the traceback:\n\n```\nlightning/pytorch/loggers/wandb.py:400: There is a wandb run already in progress and\nnewly created instances of `WandbLogger` will reuse this run. If this is not desired,\ncall `wandb.finish()` before instantiating `WandbLogger`.\n```\n\n`WandbLogger.experiment` is lazy \u2014 `wandb.init()` only actually fires the first time each\ntrainer's `.fit()` accesses it (during Lightning's `_call_setup_hook`). Since\n`trainer_constant.fit(...)` never calls `wandb.finish()` when it's done, its wandb run\n(`j4lxpyb9`) is still \"in progress\" in this kernel process when `trainer_running_diff.fit(...)`\ntries to start a *second* run. wandb's internal service gets into a conflicting state\nbetween the still-open first run and the brand-new `wandb.init()` call, and the second\nrun's upload gets rejected with the 403 \u2014 not truly a credentials problem, despite what\nthe message says.\n\nThis matches the notebook's own stated design intent (see markdown above cell `b008c604`):\n\"one [trainer] per baseline, so each gets its own checkpoint/logger state... directly\ncomparable side by side\" \u2014 i.e. two genuinely separate runs, not one shared run. The fix\nis to explicitly close the first run before the second one starts, exactly as Lightning's\nown warning suggests.\n\n## Change\n\nIn `downstream_apps/test/1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb`:\n\n- Insert a new cell **between** cell `f8a6e448` (`trainer_constant.fit(...)`) and cell\n  `5dd75610-49f1-4810-b2b6-5115164bd2ca` (`trainer_running_diff.fit(...)`) containing:\n  ```python\n  import wandb\n  wandb.finish()  # close the constant-probability baseline's run before starting the next\n  ```\n  (`wandb` isn't imported anywhere yet \u2014 only `WandbLogger` from\n  `lightning.pytorch.loggers` \u2014 so add the import here.)\n\nNo other files need changes; `make_loggers()`, `WandbLogger` construction, and the\n`WANDB_MODE`/auth setup are all already correct.\n\n## Verification\n\n- In the live kernel: run the new `wandb.finish()` cell, then run cell\n  `5dd75610-49f1-4810-b2b6-5115164bd2ca` (`trainer_running_diff.fit(...)`) and confirm it\n  completes both epochs without the `CommError` \u2014 check for a second run directory under\n  `./wandb/wandb_tmp/wave_classification/` and a second `runs/wave_classification_*` CSV\n  log, alongside the first baseline's.\n- Optionally check the WandB web UI (`https://wandb.ai/lloverasdiego/wave_classification`)\n  for two distinct runs, `baseline_constant_prob` and `baseline_running_diff`.\n",
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/i-m-running-a-ipynb-melodic-seal.md"
            },
            "kind": "edit",
            "diffs": [
              {
                "old_text": "# Rename \"Flare\" leftovers to wave classification\n\n## Context\n\n`downstream_apps/test/` was forked from the flare-forecasting template\n(`downstream_apps/template/`). The two trainable baselines (constant-probability +\nrunning-difference) are already implemented and functionally correct for binary wave\nclassification \u2014 confirmed by reading `metrics/template_metrics.py` (BCE-with-logits loss,\n`BinaryAccuracy`/`BinaryAUROC` on raw logits) and cell 23 of\n`1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` (`make_metrics()` already builds the classification\nversion).\n\nWhat's left is naming: several identifiers, docstrings, an error string, and one config\nkey still say \"flare\" even though they now hold/describe wave-classification content. This\nis exactly the checklist ADAPTING.md calls out when forking (\"rename the classes that have\n'Flare' or 'Template' in their names\"). Per `CLAUDE.md`'s priorities (Clarity first), and\nsince `grep -rniE \"flare\"` confirms the spread, this is a straightforward rename-and-reword\npass \u2014 no behavior changes.\n\n**Explicitly out of scope (per your answer):** the `batch[\"forecast\"]` key stays\n`\"forecast\"` everywhere. **Also out of scope:** `3_finetune_template_1D.py` imports from\n`downstream_apps.template.*` (a different, unrelated app) \u2014 a pre-existing bug, untouched\nby this rename since it never touches `downstream_apps.test`. `models/simple_baseline.py`'s\n`RegressionFlareModel` also keeps its name/docstring \u2014 it's a bona fide flare-regression\nmodel kept for reference, not part of the wave-classification path.\n\n## Rename map\n\n| Old | New | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| `FlareDataConfig` | `WaveDataConfig` | `configs.py` |\n| `load_flare_config` | `load_wave_config` | `configs.py` |\n| field `flare_index_path` | `wave_index_path` | `configs.py`, `configs/config_script.yaml` |\n| constructor arg `ds_flare_index_path` | `ds_wave_index_path` | `datasets/wave_dataset.py` |\n| internal column `normalized_intensity` | `label` | `datasets/wave_dataset.py` (2 write sites, 1 read site) |\n| `FlareMetrics` | `WaveMetrics` | `metrics/template_metrics.py` |\n| `FlareLightningModule` | `WaveLightningModule` | `lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py` |\n\nPlus prose fixes (docstrings, comments, one error message) wherever they say \"flare\nintensity\", \"flare catalog\", \"flare forecasting\", etc., reworded for wave classification.\nThe error message in `wave_dataset.py` also currently names the wrong class\n(`\"...for FlareDSDataset\"` when the class is `waveDSDataset`) \u2014 fixed as part of this pass.\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. `test/configs.py`\n- `FlareDataConfig` \u2192 `WaveDataConfig`; `load_flare_config` \u2192 `load_wave_config`; field\n  `flare_index_path` \u2192 `wave_index_path` (update `PATH_FIELDS` tuple too); update module\n  docstring (\"flare-forecasting template app\" \u2192 wave-classification), `__all__`.\n\n### 2. `test/configs/config_script.yaml`\n- Key `flare_index_path` \u2192 `wave_index_path` under `data:`. Reword the two adjacent\n  comments (\"Downstream label alignment (flare catalog \u2192 Surya index)\", the\n  `ds_match_direction` comment) to drop \"flare\".\n\n### 3. `test/datasets/wave_dataset.py`\n- Constructor arg `ds_flare_index_path` \u2192 `ds_wave_index_path`.\n- Internal column `normalized_intensity` \u2192 `label` (the two assignment lines in `__init__`\n  and the read in `__getitem__`); `sample[\"forecast\"] = ...[\"label\"]` \u2014 the *output* key\n  name is unchanged, only the intermediate column.\n- Reword docstrings/comments (\"flare intensity label\" \u2192 \"wave/no-wave label\", \"flare index\"\n  \u2192 \"wave index\", \"flare forecasting supplies its own labels\" \u2192 task-neutral wording).\n- Fix the error message to name `waveDSDataset` (currently wrongly says `FlareDSDataset`).\n\n### 4. `test/metrics/template_metrics.py`\n- `FlareMetrics` \u2192 `WaveMetrics`. No logic changes \u2014 docstrings already describe BCE/\n  accuracy/AUROC correctly.\n\n### 5. `test/lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py`\n- `FlareLightningModule` \u2192 `WaveLightningModule`. Reword module/class docstrings (\"flare\n  prediction training\" \u2192 \"wave classification training\"). Leave `batch[\"forecast\"]` reads\n  in `training_step`/`validation_step` untouched.\n\n### 6. `test/1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb`\n- Update the import cells and instantiations to the new names: `load_wave_config`,\n  `WaveMetrics`, `WaveLightningModule`, `ds_wave_index_path=cfg.data.wave_index_path`.\n- Update the `make_metrics()` cell's docstring and markdown cell 21's prose (\"FlareMetrics\n  has been converted...\" \u2192 \"WaveMetrics...\").\n\n### 7. `test/0_dataset_dataloader_template_diego.ipynb`\n- Same config-identifier updates as above (`load_wave_config`,\n  `ds_wave_index_path=cfg.data.wave_index_path`, both occurrences), since it shares the\n  same config module and would otherwise break after the rename. Reword the intro markdown\n  line describing the dataset as a \"flare forecasting dataset\" example.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `grep -rniE \"flare\" downstream_apps/test --include=\"*.py\" --include=\"*.yaml\"` on the\n  edited files should only still show `models/simple_baseline.py` (kept intentionally) and\n  `3_finetune_template_1D.py` (unrelated pre-existing bug, out of scope).\n- In the `jovyan-Surya_WS` kernel, run `0_dataset_dataloader_template_diego.ipynb` cells\n  one-by-one through the dataset-build cells \u2014 confirms `load_wave_config` /\n  `ds_wave_index_path` work end-to-end.\n- Run `1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` cells one-by-one from the top through the\n  `trainer.fit(...)` cell \u2014 confirms the renamed `WaveMetrics`/`WaveLightningModule`\n  imports resolve and training still completes without error.",
                "new_text": "# Fix wandb 403 error on the second baseline's `trainer.fit(...)`\n\n## Context\n\nThe two baselines in `1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` now train successfully \u2014 the\n\"flare\" rename is done, WandB is authenticated (`wandb login` stored real credentials in\n`~/.netrc` for `lloverasdiego` / `surya-ws2`), `WANDB_MODE` is back to normal (live)\nlogging, and the `num_workers=0` fix resolved an earlier DataLoader-worker crash. With all\nof that in place, `trainer_constant.fit(...)` (cell `f8a6e448`) completes cleanly and logs\na full WandB run (`wave_classification/j4lxpyb9`, checkpoints on disk).\n\nThe next cell, `trainer_running_diff.fit(...)` (cell `5dd75610-49f1-4810-b2b6-5115164bd2ca`),\nnow fails with:\n\n```\nCommError: Error uploading run: returned error 403: {\"data\":{\"upsertBucket\":null},\n\"errors\":[{\"message\":\"the provided API key cannot access this resource, check\ncredentials\",\"path\":[\"upsertBucket\"]}]}\n```\n\nThe two lines the user quoted (`WARNING The anonymous setting has no effect...` and\n`ERROR Failed to detect the name of this notebook...`) are **harmless, unrelated noise**,\nnot the actual failure:\n- \"anonymous setting has no effect\" \u2014 every `WandbLogger(...)` call passes\n  `anonymous=None` internally (`lightning/pytorch/loggers/wandb.py:345`); wandb warns this\n  is a no-op whenever you're already logged in with real credentials. Cosmetic.\n- \"Failed to detect the name of this notebook\" \u2014 wandb can't infer the `.ipynb` filename\n  under this Jupyter setup, so it skips a \"code saving\" convenience feature. Cosmetic;\n  fixable with `WANDB_NOTEBOOK_NAME` if ever wanted, but not required.\n\nThe real cause of the 403 is the line just above the traceback:\n\n```\nlightning/pytorch/loggers/wandb.py:400: There is a wandb run already in progress and\nnewly created instances of `WandbLogger` will reuse this run. If this is not desired,\ncall `wandb.finish()` before instantiating `WandbLogger`.\n```\n\n`WandbLogger.experiment` is lazy \u2014 `wandb.init()` only actually fires the first time each\ntrainer's `.fit()` accesses it (during Lightning's `_call_setup_hook`). Since\n`trainer_constant.fit(...)` never calls `wandb.finish()` when it's done, its wandb run\n(`j4lxpyb9`) is still \"in progress\" in this kernel process when `trainer_running_diff.fit(...)`\ntries to start a *second* run. wandb's internal service gets into a conflicting state\nbetween the still-open first run and the brand-new `wandb.init()` call, and the second\nrun's upload gets rejected with the 403 \u2014 not truly a credentials problem, despite what\nthe message says.\n\nThis matches the notebook's own stated design intent (see markdown above cell `b008c604`):\n\"one [trainer] per baseline, so each gets its own checkpoint/logger state... directly\ncomparable side by side\" \u2014 i.e. two genuinely separate runs, not one shared run. The fix\nis to explicitly close the first run before the second one starts, exactly as Lightning's\nown warning suggests.\n\n## Change\n\nIn `downstream_apps/test/1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb`:\n\n- Insert a new cell **between** cell `f8a6e448` (`trainer_constant.fit(...)`) and cell\n  `5dd75610-49f1-4810-b2b6-5115164bd2ca` (`trainer_running_diff.fit(...)`) containing:\n  ```python\n  import wandb\n  wandb.finish()  # close the constant-probability baseline's run before starting the next\n  ```\n  (`wandb` isn't imported anywhere yet \u2014 only `WandbLogger` from\n  `lightning.pytorch.loggers` \u2014 so add the import here.)\n\nNo other files need changes; `make_loggers()`, `WandbLogger` construction, and the\n`WANDB_MODE`/auth setup are all already correct.\n\n## Verification\n\n- In the live kernel: run the new `wandb.finish()` cell, then run cell\n  `5dd75610-49f1-4810-b2b6-5115164bd2ca` (`trainer_running_diff.fit(...)`) and confirm it\n  completes both epochs without the `CommError` \u2014 check for a second run directory under\n  `./wandb/wandb_tmp/wave_classification/` and a second `runs/wave_classification_*` CSV\n  log, alongside the first baseline's.\n- Optionally check the WandB web UI (`https://wandb.ai/lloverasdiego/wave_classification`)\n  for two distinct runs, `baseline_constant_prob` and `baseline_running_diff`.",
