      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "a6f5c6d9-6f5b-4ed4-bb59-6fefa433d33d",
      "body": "The key itself is very unlikely to be the problem \u2014 a few things already confirm it's authenticating as the right account:\n\n- `wandb.Api().viewer` returned `username: lloverasdiego`, which matches you, not some other account.\n- That same call listed `surya-ws2` among your teams \u2014 a wrong account wouldn't have visibility into a team you're not on.\n- The **first** baseline's run (`j4lxpyb9`) successfully wrote to `surya-ws2` with this exact key. If the key belonged to someone else's account, you wouldn't have write access there even once.\n\nIf it were the wrong key entirely, I'd expect it to fail consistently from the start (including on read, and on that first successful write) \u2014 not succeed once and then fail identically on every write attempt afterward, including a fresh `wandb.teardown()`, a brand-new unrelated `wandb.init()`, and a different team. That \"works once, then blocked on every subsequent write\" pattern is the signature of a quota/rate-limit, not a bad credential.\n\nThat said, it costs nothing to rule it out \u2014 if you want, run `wandb login --relogin` in the terminal again, re-paste the key from https://wandb.ai/authorize (make sure you're logged into the right browser account first), and I'll retry. But I'd bet on the `surya-ws2` team having a per-member run cap rather than a key mismatch. Want me to try the relogin anyway, or just switch the config to your other entity for now?",
      "time": 1789569748.111555,
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
