    {
      "body": "Notebook's clean now: `f8a6e448` (baseline 1 fit) \u2192 `a75db65c` (`wandb.finish()`) \u2192 `ceb1ef4f` (entity switch) \u2192 `5dd75610` (baseline 2 fit) \u2192 conclusion. Both `trainer_constant.fit(...)` and `trainer_running_diff.fit(...)` already completed successfully end-to-end under the new entity (visible in their stored outputs \u2014 no `CommError`), so the original 403 fix is fully verified.\n\n**On your loss question: it's undertraining, not a bug in the implementation or the design.**\n\nEvidence:\n1. **The feature itself is fine.** The AIA193 running-difference input has real, non-degenerate signal (std \u2248 0.058, values ranging -1.68 to 3.46) \u2014 not a zero/constant bug.\n2. **The model can learn it, given enough steps.** I ran the same model on the same 10 training samples with a higher LR (1e-2) in a manual loop, outside Lightning's epoch cap. After only **6 epochs (30 gradient steps)**, loss dropped from ~0.68 (chance level, ln 2) to **0.023**. So the architecture and gradients are correct \u2014 it converges fast once it gets enough updates.\n3. **The real run starves it.** `trainer_running_diff` uses `max_epochs=4`, `batch_size=2`, 10 samples \u2192 **5 batches/epoch \u00d7 4 epochs = only 20 gradient steps total**, at `lr=1e-4` (100x smaller than my test). That's nowhere near enough for Adam to move a freshly-initialized 16,384-parameter linear layer away from its starting point.\n\nSo the stuck-near-0.693 loss you saw in WandB is expected behavior for this config, not a red flag about the baseline's design. If you want to see the running-difference baseline actually separate from the constant baseline, bump `training.max_epochs` and/or `training.learning_rate` in `configs/config_script.yaml` (e.g., more epochs, or `lr: 1e-3`\u2013`1e-2`) \u2014 keeping in mind `max_samples: 10` is explicitly a smoke-test cap, so any resulting accuracy number is just a sanity check, not a meaningful validation score.",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "8a8be56f-1fc3-4f8c-b320-8bd4f9bd041c",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789583634.3457427,
      "mentions": []
    },
