      "body": "Good progress \u2014 most of the fixes landed correctly, but a handful of leftovers will still crash, plus a couple I flagged earlier are still open. Here's the current state, cell by cell.\n\n## Still broken\n\n**Cell `63c1fe60`** \u2014 import fixed, but the *call* wasn't:\n```python\nfrom downstream_apps.test.configs import load_wave_config\ncfg = load_flare_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")   # <- still the old name\n```\n`NameError: name 'load_flare_config' is not defined`. Change the call to `load_wave_config(...)`.\n\n**Cell `34db2cc6`** \u2014 now points at the right app, wrong file:\n```python\nfrom downstream_apps.test.datasets.template_dataset import waveDSDataset\n```\n`downstream_apps/test/datasets/template_dataset.py` exists but only defines `FlareDSDataset` (I checked \u2014 it's a leftover copy of the flare template's dataset, not your wave one). Your actual class is in the sibling file `wave_dataset.py`. Fix:\n```python\nfrom downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset\n```\n\n**Cell `70e9c5b2`** \u2014 the kwargs are now correctly named (`ds_wave_index_path`, `ds_class_column`, etc. \u2014 nice), but it's still missing `label_transform`. I checked `lcpf_aia_waves.csv`: the `class` column holds the string `\"wave\"`, not a 0/1 float. Without a transform, `waveDSDataset.__getitem__` does `self.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx][\"label\"].astype(np.float32)` on that raw string and crashes. Add, as in notebook 1:\n```python\nlabel_transform=lambda s: (s == \"wave\").astype(np.float32),\n```\n\n**Cell `d152b127`** \u2014 imports `WaveMetrics` (capital, correct) but still instantiates the old lowercase name four times:\n```python\ntrain_loss_metrics = waveMetrics(\"train_loss\")\nval_loss_metrics = waveMetrics(\"val_loss\")\ntrain_evaluation_metrics = waveMetrics(\"train_metrics\")\nvalidation_evaluation_metrics = waveMetrics(\"val_metrics\")\n```\nAll four need `WaveMetrics`. Same `NameError` pattern as before.\n\n**Cell `553565e7`** \u2014 same issue, one instance:\n```python\nlit_model = waveLightningModule(model, metrics, lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size)\n```\n\u2192 `WaveLightningModule`.\n\n## Still open from before\n- **`configs/config_script.yaml` line 146**: `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]` \u2014 unchanged. Since this backbone has no `q_proj`/`k_proj`/`v_proj`/`out_proj` (it's fused into `attn.qkv`/`attn.proj`), those four names silently match nothing and LoRA never touches attention. Should be `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`.\n\n## Fixed correctly (confirmed)\nCell `34c502ec` (`WaveMetrics` import), cell `9d730cf3` (`WaveLightningModule` import), cell `70e9c5b2`'s kwarg names, and cell `b008c604`'s `max_epochs = cfg.max_epochs` are all now correct.\n\nWant me to apply these five remaining fixes (four `NameError`s + the YAML) directly?\n\n---\n\nNow, the pipeline inside `workshop_infrastructure/models/` \u2014 how a batch actually becomes a prediction.\n\n## The four files and what each owns\n\n```\nembedding.py          \u2014 turns raw pixels into transformer tokens (and back)\nspectformer.py         \u2014 the \"spectral gating\" block type\ntransformer_ls.py       \u2014 the \"long-short attention\" block type\nflow.py                \u2014 optional learned optical-flow warp between input frames\nhelio_spectformer.py    \u2014 HelioSpectFormer: wires the above into the actual backbone\nfinetune_models.py      \u2014 HelioSpectformer1D/2D: wraps HelioSpectFormer with a task head\n```\n\n`finetune_models.py` is the only one downstream apps import directly. The other four are the vendored Surya internals \u2014 you configure them through `ModelConfig`, but never touch their code.\n\n## Step by step, what happens to your batch\n\n**1. Patch embedding (`embedding.py`).** Your batch's `ts` tensor arrives as `(B, C=13, T, H=4096, W=4096)`. A `Conv2d` at `embedding.patch_embed.proj` slices it into non-overlapping `16\u00d716` patches and projects each patch to `embed_dim=1280`. This turns a `4096\u00d74096` image into a `256\u00d7256` grid of 1280-dim tokens \u2014 that's the \"tokenizer\" CLAUDE.md warns not to accidentally LoRA-adapt (a bare `proj` target would match this Conv2d too).\n\n**2. Time embedding.** `time_embedding.type: linear` (your config) linearly projects the timestamp/lead-time metadata into the same 1280-dim space and adds it to the patch tokens \u2014 this is how the model knows *when* each input frame was relative to the target, without a separate positional-encoding table per timestep. `time_dim` must match how many frames `time_delta_input_minutes` actually supplies (`TrainingConfig.__post_init__` checks this at config-load time).\n\n**3. Optional learned flow (`flow.py`).** Only active if `model.learned_flow: true` (yours is `false`). When on, it estimates an optical-flow field between input frames and warps one onto the other via `F.grid_sample` before the transformer sees them \u2014 this is the op with no deterministic CUDA backward, which is why `learned_flow: true` + `deterministic: true` is rejected by config validation.\n\n**4. The class token (only relevant to your `pooling: class_token`).** `HelioSpectformer1D.forward_with_cls_token` prepends one learnable token (`nglo=1`) to the sequence *before* it enters the backbone blocks. This is the `head_cls_token` you saw in `finetune_models.py` \u2014 it rides alongside the 256\u00d7256 patch tokens through every block and attention layer, accumulating global information, and at the end you read *only* that token as the pooled representation (`agg_tokens = tokens.squeeze(dim=1)`), instead of averaging over 65k patch tokens.\n\n**5. The 10 transformer blocks \u2014 two types, back to back:**\n- **Spectral gating blocks** (`spectral_blocks: 2`, from `spectformer.py`) run first. Each takes the token grid, applies a 2D FFT, multiplies by a *learned complex weight* (`complex_weight` \u2014 the parameter CLAUDE.md says is never LoRA-adapted) in frequency space, then inverse-FFTs back. This is a cheap way to mix global spatial information (every frequency component touches every patch) before the expensive attention blocks run.\n- **Long-short attention blocks** (`depth - spectral_blocks = 8`, from `transformer_ls.py`) run next. Each combines: (a) local windowed attention over `window_size=2` neighboring patches \u2014 cheap, captures fine detail \u2014 with (b) global attention via a learned low-rank projection (`attn.to_dynamic_projection`, rank `dp_rank=4`) that compresses the full 256\u00d7256 token grid down to a handful of \"summary\" tokens that every patch can attend to cheaply. This is what makes attention on a 4096\u00d74096 image tractable at all \u2014 full quadratic attention over 65k tokens would be enormous. `attn.qkv` (the fused q/k/v projection) and `attn.proj` (output projection) live here \u2014 the two module names your LoRA `target_modules` needs to hit.\n\n**6. `finetune=True` strips the pretraining decoder.** `HelioSpectFormer(..., finetune=True)` is the flag `HelioSpectformer1D`/`2D` always pass \u2014 it drops whatever reconstruction/forecasting head Surya used during self-supervised pretraining, since you're replacing it with your own task head anyway. Only the backbone (embedding + time embedding + 10 blocks) survives into `self.backbone`.\n\n**7. Your head, on top (`finetune_models.py`, not vendored \u2014 this is the part written for downstream apps).** After the backbone produces final tokens, `HelioSpectformer1D.forward` does: optional `head_linear` (penultimate `1280\u21921280` projection) \u2192 pooling (`class_token` in your case: just take that one token) \u2192 optional `head_dropout` \u2192 `head_unembed` (`1280\u21921`, your `num_outputs=1`) \u2192 `.squeeze(dim=1)` to get a plain `(B,)` logit. That logit is exactly what `WaveMetrics.train_loss` feeds into `binary_cross_entropy_with_logits`.\n\n**8. Pretrained weights land on `backbone.*` only.** `load_pretrained_weights()` loads the checkpoint (saved with flat keys like `embedding.proj.weight`, `blocks.3.attn.qkv.weight`, etc.) and re-prefixes each with `backbone.` to match where `HelioSpectformer1D` nested it. Your `head_*` layers were never in the checkpoint \u2014 they start randomly initialized, which is exactly why the head must stay trainable (the `head_` / `modules_to_save` mechanism) regardless of which LoRA regime you pick.\n\nSo the full chain for your task is: pixels \u2192 patches \u2192 (+time embedding) \u2192 [2 spectral blocks] \u2192 [8 long-short attention blocks, reading global context through a rank-4 projection] \u2192 class token \u2192 linear \u2192 dropout \u2192 single logit \u2192 BCE. LoRA, when correctly targeted, adds a small trainable delta to `fc1`/`fc2`/`attn.qkv`/`attn.proj` inside every one of those 10 blocks, while everything else in the backbone stays frozen at its pretrained value.",
