              "description": "Find existing baseline training results",
              "prompt": "Search breadth: very thorough.\n\nWorking dir: /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test\n\nI need the ACTUAL NUMBERS from all training runs that have already been executed in this directory, so I can diagnose an overfitting question (train loss decreasing while val loss increases).\n\nPlease find and report:\n\n1. All CSVLogger output directories. Look under `runs/` (e.g. `runs/wave_classification/version_0/metrics.csv`, `version_1`, etc.) and any other `runs*` or `lightning_logs` dirs. For EACH version directory found:\n   - Print the full path\n   - Print the ENTIRE contents of `metrics.csv` (these files are small \u2014 a few dozen rows). I need every row: epoch, step, train_loss, train_loss_bce, train_metric_accuracy, val_loss, val_loss_bce, val_metric_accuracy, val_metric_auroc \u2014 whatever columns exist.\n   - Print the contents of `hparams.yaml` if present.\n   - Report the file modification time (`ls -l` or `stat`) so I can order the runs chronologically.\n\n2. All wandb run directories under `wandb/` (including `wandb/wandb_tmp/`). For each: the run id, run name, and if there is a `files/wandb-summary.json` or `files/config.yaml`, print its contents.\n\n3. Any checkpoint files (`*.ckpt`) anywhere under this directory tree \u2014 report paths, sizes, and modification times. The checkpoint filename usually encodes epoch and val_loss (e.g. `epoch=3-step=20.ckpt`), so report the exact filenames.\n\n4. Report the total count of runs you found and your best guess at which run corresponds to which experiment, based on names like `baseline_constant_prob`, `baseline_running_diff`, `finetune_experiment_1`, and based on the directory names / timestamps.\n\nDo NOT modify anything. Use `cat`, `ls -la`, `find`, `stat`. Be exhaustive \u2014 I need the raw numbers, not a summary. If a metrics.csv is larger than ~200 rows, print the first 60 and last 60 rows plus the row count.",
