              "prompt": "Search breadth: very thorough.\n\nWorking dir: /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test\n\nI need to understand the exact size and class balance of the wave-classification dataset, because I'm planning a time-budgeted training schedule and need to know how many samples are actually available.\n\nPlease investigate and report precisely:\n\n1. The wave label catalog at `data/lcpf_aia_waves.csv` (path is relative to the working dir; the config references it as `../data/lcpf_aia_waves.csv` from `configs/config_script.yaml`, so resolve it correctly and report the absolute path you actually read).\n   - Total row count.\n   - The exact set of distinct values in the `class` column and the count of each (use python/pandas or awk \u2014 e.g. `python3 -c \"import pandas as pd; d=pd.read_csv(...); print(d['class'].value_counts())\"`).\n   - The min and max of the `mid_time` and `start_time` columns (the date range).\n   - Confirm the exact column names (print the header only, it's a very wide CSV with many URL columns \u2014 do NOT print full data rows, they are enormous).\n\n2. The Surya index CSVs referenced by the config as `../../../data/indices/surya_aws_s3_train.csv` and `../../../data/indices/surya_aws_s3_val.csv`. Resolve and report absolute paths.\n   - Row count of each.\n   - The header/columns.\n   - The date range of timestamps in each (min/max).\n   - Print just 2-3 sample rows of each so I can see the format (path, timestamp).\n   - Are the paths s3:// URIs? What bucket?\n\n3. THE KEY QUESTION: how many samples actually survive the merge between the Surya index and the wave catalog? Read `datasets/wave_dataset.py` to understand the merge logic (it uses `pd.merge_asof` with `ds_time_tolerance` and `ds_match_direction`, then drops duplicates on `ds_index`, then applies a time tolerance filter). Then REPLICATE that merge logic in a standalone pandas script (read-only, do not import the dataset class since it would try to touch S3) to compute:\n   - How many matched samples exist for the TRAIN index with `ds_time_tolerance: 4d`, `ds_match_direction: forward`, `ds_time_column: mid_time`.\n   - How many for the VAL index with the same settings.\n   - The class balance (wave vs whatever else) within each of those matched sets.\n   - IMPORTANT: also report what happens with a much tighter tolerance like `15min` and `1h` and `12h`, since 4 days is a suspiciously loose match window and I want to know if the labels are meaningfully aligned in time.\n   Note: `valid_indices` in the dataset comes from the base `HelioNetCDFDataset` \u2014 read `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py` (at /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py) to learn how `valid_indices` is derived from the index CSV (which column, what filtering by `time_delta_input_minutes` etc.), and mimic it as closely as you reasonably can. Report exactly what approximation you made.\n\n4. Check whether the catalog contains any negative-class (\"no wave\") examples at all. If the `class` column is entirely \"wave\", say so LOUDLY and explain what that implies \u2014 it would mean the binary classification task has only positive labels via this catalog. Look for any other CSV in the repo that might supply negatives (search for other csv files under `data/` and `downstream_apps/test/data/`).\n\nReport concrete numbers. Do not modify any file.",
