            "raw_input": {
              "description": "Design 18h training plan architecture",
              "subagent_type": "Plan",
              "prompt": "Design the file/script architecture for an 18-hour experiment plan on a Surya heliophysics fine-tuning task. All facts below are MEASURED, not assumed \u2014 do not re-measure, design against them.\n\nREPO: /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026 (branch downstream/wave_classification)\nAPP DIR: /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test\nPYTHON: /home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python (torch 2.9.1+cu128, lightning 2.6.6, peft 0.18.1)\n\n== TASK ==\nBinary classification of EUV wave events from SDO 13-channel 4096x4096 stacks.\nTwo model families to train and compare:\n (A) Simple baselines: ConstantProbabilityModel (1 param) and RunningDifferenceLogisticModel\n     (AIA193 running difference t vs t-12min, 32x32 AvgPool -> 128x128 -> Linear(16384,1) = 16,385 params).\n     Both in downstream_apps/test/models/simple_baseline.py. Need preprocess_fn=partial(destandardize_channels,...).\n (B) Surya LoRA fine-tune: HelioSpectformer1D (366M backbone + head), 3,157,761 trainable with LoRA.\n\n== MEASURED HARDWARE / THROUGHPUT ==\n- 1x L40S, 47.7 GB VRAM, bf16 OK. 16 CPUs, 124 GB RAM. GPU currently idle.\n- Surya fwd+bwd with LoRA + bf16 autocast + grad checkpointing on all 10 layers:\n    batch=1: 3.05 s/step, peak 17.5 GB alloc / 21.1 GB reserved\n    batch=2: 6.23 s/step, peak 33.4 GB alloc / 42.4 GB reserved  (89% of VRAM)\n    batch=4: CUDA OOM\n  => 3.1 s/sample, linear (compute-bound). batch_size=2 is the hard max.\n- Input tensor ts is (B,13,2,4096,4096) float32 = 1.74 GB PER SAMPLE. This drives both VRAM and RAM.\n  RAM risk: num_workers x prefetch_factor x batch_size x 1.74 GB. Must stay well under 124 GB.\n- S3 (public bucket nasa-surya-bench, anon) throughput saturates at ~600 MB/s with >=8 parallel file fetches\n  (~1.0 s per 560 MB file). Serial is 119 MB/s.\n- EFS cache at /home/jovyan/scratch_space/surya_cache/ reads at 585 MB/s single-stream. Space effectively unlimited.\n- Each sample needs 2 NetCDF frames (t-12min, t), ~560 MB each => ~1.1 GB/sample.\n\n== MEASURED DATASET ==\n- Catalog downstream_apps/test/data/lcpf_aia_waves.csv: 1828 rows, EXACTLY 914 \"wave\" / 914 \"no wave\".\n- CRITICAL STRUCTURE: each event contributes a PAIR sharing one start_time. \"wave\" = mid_time = start_time+30min,\n  \"no wave\" = mid_time = start_time-30min. So negatives are the SAME event's pre-onset frame, 1 hour earlier,\n  same active region. Classes are matched on everything except the 1-hour offset.\n- With time_delta_input_minutes=[-12,0] (T=2) and ds_time_tolerance 4d, merged against the day-disjoint Surya indices:\n    train = 1231 samples (612 wave / 619 no wave)\n    val   =   27 samples (13 / 14)   <-- very small, 1 sample = 3.7%\n    test  =   54 samples (25 / 29)\n- Cache coverage of the frames THIS task needs: train 49/2457 (2.0%), val 21/54, test 0/108.\n  => ~1.35 TB must be downloaded fresh. At 600 MB/s that is ~40 min of pure network time for train,\n     and it can be overlapped with GPU compute by the DataLoader workers.\n\n== MEASURED BUGS ALREADY DIAGNOSED (must be respected by the design) ==\n1. Pretrained checkpoint's patch embedding is Conv2d(26,1280,16,16) = 13 channels x 2 timesteps.\n   With the current config (time_delta_input_minutes:[0], time_embedding.time_dim:1) the model builds a\n   13-channel tokenizer and load_pretrained_weights silently SKIPS it: 156/159 tensors load, tokenizer stays RANDOM.\n   With time_dim=2 -> 157/159 load (only the pretraining decoder unembed.* is skipped, which is correct).\n   => time_delta_input_minutes must have >=2 entries and time_dim must be 2. This ALSO gives the\n      running-difference baseline the 2 frames it needs, so both model families can share ONE dataset. \n2. YAML lora_config.target_modules is [q_proj,v_proj,k_proj,out_proj,fc1,fc2]. Surya has NO q_proj/k_proj/v_proj/out_proj\n   (q/k/v are fused as attn.qkv, output is attn.proj). PEFT silently ignores non-matching names, so only fc1/fc2\n   got adapters: 2,666,241 trainable instead of 3,157,761 \u2014 attention was never adapted. A real prior run logged\n   exactly \"2.7 M Trainable params\", confirming this. Correct value: [fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj].\n3. A prior LoRA run at learning_rate 0.01 DIVERGED (losses 10^2..10^4, exact 0.0 bf16 values, val acc/auroc pinned\n   at 0.5). lr=0.01 was tuned for the 16K-param logistic baseline and must NOT be reused for Surya.\n4. A prior baseline run (16,385 params, 10 train samples, lr=0.01, 20 epochs) is textbook overfitting:\n   train_loss 0.814 -> 0.0058 while val_loss rose monotonically 0.760 -> 1.006, val AUROC frozen at exactly 0.4.\n\n== EXISTING CODE TO REUSE (do not reinvent) ==\n- workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py: build_helio_dataloaders(cfg, dataset_cls, scalers=, num_workers=, seed=, **task_kwargs)\n  Note: it sets pin_memory=True, persistent_workers=True, multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\", drop_last=True,\n  and does NOT expose prefetch_factor. batch_size comes from cfg.batch_size.\n- workshop_infrastructure/utils.py: build_scalers, apply_peft_lora, load_pretrained_weights, discover_head_modules,\n  UploadBestCheckpointToS3\n- workshop_infrastructure/models/finetune_models.py: HelioSpectformer1D.from_config(cfg.model, num_outputs=, dtype=, use_latitude_in_learned_flow=)\n- downstream_apps/test/configs.py: WaveDataConfig + load_wave_config (already correct for the wave task)\n- downstream_apps/test/datasets/wave_dataset.py: waveDSDataset. Constructor kwargs: ds_wave_index_path,\n  ds_time_column, ds_class_column, ds_time_tolerance, ds_match_direction, label_transform, return_surya_stack,\n  max_number_of_samples. Requires label_transform=lambda s: (s==\"wave\").astype(np.float32) because the raw column holds strings.\n- downstream_apps/test/metrics/template_metrics.py: WaveMetrics (BCE-with-logits loss; accuracy + AUROC reporting) \u2014 already correct.\n- downstream_apps/test/lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py: WaveLightningModule(model, metrics, lr=, batch_size=, preprocess_fn=)\n- downstream_apps/test/3_finetune_template_1D.py: EXISTS but is a byte-identical, UNADAPTED copy of the flare\n  template (imports downstream_apps.template.*, load_flare_config, FlareDSDataset, FlareMetrics, FlareLightningModule,\n  passes ds_flare_index_path). Its build_trainer() has NO gradient_clip_val, NO accumulate_grad_batches, and there is\n  no --lr CLI flag. It must NOT be modified (user said don't remove anything) \u2014 a NEW adapted script is required.\n\n== USER'S EXPLICIT REQUIREMENTS ==\n1.1 Plot the AIA193 running difference AFTER the 32x32 pooling kernel, for a wave and a no-wave sample, to judge\n    whether wave features are visible at the resolution the baseline actually sees.\n1.2 Explain why train loss decreased while val loss increased in the existing results.\n2.1 Verify 2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb is correct.\n2.2 Create a copy of 3_finetune_template_1D.py adapted to this task (new file).\n2.3 That file must contain all the explorations we want to run.\n2.4 Report whether loss decreases in train and val, and explain.\n- Maximize VRAM use without exceeding VRAM or RAM (exceeding RAM would collapse the shared AWS machine).\n- The SAME training dataset must be used for task 1 and task 2.\n- If time permits, multiple runs with different numbers of images, to see whether val loss improves with more data.\n- Create new files rather than deleting/replacing anything. Save plots BOTH to wandb and to local disk.\n- Total wall clock available: 18 hours, unattended.\n\n== WHAT I WANT FROM YOU ==\nA concrete architecture and schedule, specifically:\n(a) What new files to create (paths + responsibility of each). Consider that the baseline's features are a\n    deterministic 128x128 pooled map (~64 KB/sample) whereas loading a sample costs 1.1 GB \u2014 so a single data pass\n    that CACHES pooled baseline features to disk would let the baseline train in seconds and be studied exhaustively,\n    while simultaneously warming the EFS cache for the Surya runs and producing the 1.1 plots. Evaluate that idea\n    critically, including the wrinkle that phase=\"train\" applies random channel masking and vertical flips\n    (so cached features freeze one augmentation draw).\n(b) The exact DataLoader worker/prefetch configuration that maximizes GPU utilization while keeping RAM safe,\n    given 1.74 GB per sample and that build_helio_dataloaders does not expose prefetch_factor. Say whether\n    a new builder wrapper or an added kwarg is warranted, and how to bound worst-case RAM arithmetically.\n(c) How to get an effective batch size larger than 2 (gradient accumulation) and what value to use.\n(d) A minute-by-minute 18-hour schedule with explicit checkpoints, ordered so that partial completion still yields\n    publishable results, and with the cheap/high-information work first. Include an LR sanity probe for Surya before\n    committing hours, given bug 3.\n(e) How to make the scaling study (different numbers of images) a fair comparison \u2014 fixed epochs vs fixed gradient\n    steps, and how to handle the 27-sample validation set's noise in the comparison.\n(f) Crash/robustness concerns for an unattended 18-hour run: what to checkpoint, how to make each run resumable,\n    how to avoid one failed run taking down the rest, and how to keep wandb runs from colliding\n    (a prior session had WandbLogger reuse one run across two fits because wandb.finish() was never called).\n\nBe concrete and critical. Point out anything in my framing that is wrong or risky. Do not write any files."
            },
