            "raw_input": {
              "content": "# 18-hour plan: wave-event classification baselines + Surya LoRA fine-tune\n\n## Context\n\nYou need publishable results in 18 unattended hours on binary classification of EUV wave\nevents, comparing simple baselines against a Surya LoRA fine-tune. Prior attempts produced\ntwo failed runs (a baseline that overfit 10 samples, and a Surya run that numerically\ndiverged), and the notebook path has silent misconfigurations. Before committing hours of\nGPU time I measured the hardware, the dataset, and the model, because three of those\nmeasurements invalidate the obvious plan. Everything below is measured, not estimated.\n\n---\n\n## The numbers that drive every decision\n\n**Hardware** \u2014 1\u00d7 L40S, 47.7 GB VRAM, bf16 OK; 16 CPUs; 124 GB RAM; GPU idle.\n\n**Surya throughput** (LoRA + bf16 autocast + grad checkpointing on all 10 layers, measured):\n\n| batch | s/step | s/sample | peak alloc | peak reserved |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 3.05 | 3.05 | 17.5 GB | 21.1 GB |\n| 2 | 6.23 | 3.12 | 33.4 GB | **42.4 GB (89% of VRAM)** |\n| 4 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | **CUDA OOM** |\n\n\u2192 **`batch_size: 2` is the hard maximum and already maximizes VRAM.** Throughput is linear\n(compute-bound), so a bigger batch would buy no speed anyway \u2014 larger *effective* batch must\ncome from gradient accumulation, which is free. **Budget 3.1 s per training sample.**\n\n**RAM is the real danger.** `ts` is `(B,13,2,4096,4096)` float32 = **1.74 GB per sample**.\nWorst-case RSS \u2248 `num_workers \u00d7 prefetch_factor \u00d7 batch_size \u00d7 1.74 GB`. At the config's\n`num_workers: 8` with default `prefetch_factor=2` and batch 2 that is **56 GB**, before the\nmain process, pinned-memory copies and page cache. This is the \"collapse the AWS machine\"\nrisk, and it must be bounded explicitly rather than left to defaults.\n\n**Dataset** (measured by replicating the `merge_asof` logic under `[-12, 0]`, T=2):\ntrain **1231** (612 wave / 619 no-wave), val **27** (13/14), test **54** (25/29).\nCatalog is exactly 914/914 balanced.\n\n**The label structure is the most important scientific finding.** Every event contributes a\n*pair* sharing one `start_time`: `wave` = `start_time + 30 min`, `no wave` = `start_time \u2212 30 min`.\nSo the negative is *the same event's own pre-onset frame, one hour earlier, same active region*.\nThe classes are matched by construction on limb position, disk activity and AR presence \u2014 there\nare no cheap shortcuts, and the real task is \"is this frame 30 min after or 30 min before onset\".\nThat is a much finer discrimination than wave-vs-quiet-Sun and it resets what counts as a good\nAUROC here. It also means class balance can be held exact by subsampling at the *event* level.\n\n**The 2 TB S3 cache is nearly useless for this task** \u2014 of the frames this task needs, only\n**49/2457 train (2.0%)**, 21/54 val, 0/108 test are cached. The 3699 cached files are unrelated\ndates. So **~1.35 TB must be downloaded fresh.** Measured S3 throughput saturates at\n**~600 MB/s with \u22658 parallel fetches** (119 MB/s serial), so that is ~40 min of pure network\ntime \u2014 and at 1.1 GB per sample (2 frames) it is ~1.9 s/sample, comfortably hidden behind the\n3.1 s/sample GPU compute *provided enough workers*. EFS re-reads at 585 MB/s. Not a blocker,\nbut it does mean **epoch 1 is I/O-bound and epochs 2+ are not**.\n\n---\n\n## Confirmed bugs (all verified by execution, not inspection)\n\n1. **The pretrained tokenizer is silently discarded.** The checkpoint's patch embedding is\n   `Conv2d(26,1280,16,16)` \u2014 13 channels \u00d7 **2** timesteps. The config's\n   `time_delta_input_minutes: [0]` / `time_embedding.time_dim: 1` builds a 13-channel tokenizer,\n   so `load_pretrained_weights` skips it and it stays **randomly initialized**. Measured:\n   `time_dim=1` \u2192 **156/159** tensors load; `time_dim=2` \u2192 **157/159** (only the pretraining\n   decoder `unembed.*` is skipped, which is correct). Every Surya result so far was produced\n   with a random tokenizer.\n   *Fixing this to `time_dim: 2` also gives the running-difference baseline the 2 frames it\n   needs \u2014 so one dataset serves both tasks, satisfying your same-dataset requirement.*\n\n2. **LoRA never adapted attention.** `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]`\n   \u2014 Surya has none of those names (q/k/v are fused as `attn.qkv`, output is `attn.proj`). PEFT\n   only errors when *nothing* matches, so `fc1`/`fc2` matched and the rest were silently ignored.\n   Measured: buggy targets \u2192 **2,666,241** trainable; correct `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`\n   \u2192 **3,157,761**, exactly the documented figure. Your diverged run logged \"2.7 M\", confirming it.\n\n3. **`learning_rate: 0.01` diverges Surya.** Prior run: losses 10\u00b2\u201310\u2074, exact `0.0` bf16 values,\n   val acc/AUROC pinned at 0.5. That LR was tuned on the 16 K-param logistic baseline.\n\n4. **`max_samples` subsetting is chronologically biased.** `waveDSDataset` head-slices a frame\n   sorted by `ds_index`, so `max_samples: 10` yields the 5 earliest event pairs (all June\u2013July\n   2010). Using it for a data-scaling study would confound \"more data\" with \"different epoch of\n   the solar cycle\". The scaling study must use seeded random **event-level** subsets instead.\n\n5. **`build_helio_dataloaders` sets `drop_last=True` for validation too**, silently discarding\n   1 of 27 val samples (3.7%). It also does not expose `prefetch_factor`, which is exactly the\n   knob needed to bound RAM.\n\n### Task 2.1 \u2014 state of `2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb`\nYour fixes all landed: it now runs top-to-bottom with sequential exec counts, calls\n`load_wave_config`, has `import numpy as np` + `label_transform`, and uses `WaveMetrics` /\n`WaveLightningModule` and `max_epochs = cfg.max_epochs`. What remains: bugs **1, 2, 3** above\nreach it through the shared YAML (so its Surya model has a random tokenizer, starved LoRA, and a\ndivergent LR), `max_number_of_samples=10` is hardcoded rather than `cfg.data.max_samples`, no\n`wandb.finish()` (causes run reuse), and several markdown cells still describe the flare\nregression task \u2014 `FlareDataConfig`/`load_flare_config`, and \"Mean Squared Error\"/\"RRSE\" where the\ncode computes BCE/accuracy/AUROC.\n\n---\n\n## Open decisions \u2014 I need your call on these four\n\nI could not use the interactive picker (disabled this session), so tell me which you want and I\nwill proceed; the plan below assumes the **bold** option.\n\n1. **Validation is only 27 samples** (1 sample = 3.7%), so `val_loss` checkpointing is nearly a\n   coin flip. **(a) Use the 27-sample val for monitoring + evaluate the best checkpoint on the\n   official 54-sample test split for a final, less-noisy number** (81 held out, +60 GB download,\n   no extra training time, official splits intact). (b) Official val only. (c) Re-split from\n   `surya_aws_s3_full_index.csv` for a few-hundred-sample val (recovers the 254 catalog rows in\n   2012/2022 that are missing from *all* official splits, but breaks comparability).\n\n2. **Match tolerance.** 99.2% of matches land within 15 min, but the 10 loosest train matches (up\n   to 1 day off) are 9-to-1 \"no wave\" \u2014 that skew is the whole reason balance is 612/619 rather\n   than 611/611. **(a) Tighten `ds_time_tolerance` to `15min`** (costs 10 train + 2 val samples,\n   restores exact balance, guarantees every label sits within one cadence step of its image).\n   (b) Keep `4d`.\n\n3. **Where corrected settings live.** **(a) Fix the outright bugs (1 and 2) in place in\n   `configs/config_script.yaml` with old values kept as comments \u2014 so your notebooks become\n   correct too \u2014 plus a new config for full-scale run settings.** (b) New config only, leaving\n   `config_script.yaml` untouched (zero risk to notebooks, but the notebook keeps training a\n   random tokenizer).\n\n4. **If time runs short, what to protect.** **(a) The data-scaling curve, since it answers your\n   \"does val loss improve with more images\" question** \u2014 the largest run gets fewer epochs.\n   (b) One full-set run to convergence as the headline. (c) Baselines + diagnosis first.\n\n---\n\n## Plan\n\nOrdered so cheap, high-information work lands first and partial completion still yields results.\nEvery Surya run gets a hard `Trainer(max_time=...)` wall-clock cap so no single run can starve\nthe rest.\n\n### Phase 0 \u2014 Fixes and pre-flight (~30 min, no GPU)\n- Apply bug fixes 1\u20132 per decision 3; set a safe Surya `learning_rate` (1e-4 default, confirmed\n  by Phase 3).\n- Fix notebook 2's remaining items: `cfg.data.max_samples`, add `wandb.finish()`, correct the\n  stale flare markdown (MSE/RRSE \u2192 BCE/accuracy/AUROC, `FlareDataConfig` \u2192 `WaveDataConfig`).\n- Pre-flight assertions in the new script, failing loudly rather than training something wrong:\n  `load_pretrained_weights` reports **157/159**, trainable == **3,157,761** under LoRA, and\n  `ts.shape[2] == 2`.\n\n### Phase 1 \u2014 One data pass: cache warm + baseline features + 1.1 plots (~90 min, no GPU)\nA single pass over train+val(+test) that does three jobs at once. This is the key scheduling\nmove: the baseline's features are a deterministic 128\u00d7128 pooled map (**64 KB/sample**) while\n*loading* a sample costs 1.1 GB, so paying the I/O once buys unlimited cheap baseline work.\n- Warms the EFS cache with all ~1.35 TB the Surya runs will need (so Surya epoch 1 is not I/O-bound).\n- Writes pooled AIA193 running-difference features + labels + timestamps to a small `.npz` (~80 MB).\n- Saves full-resolution and pooled RD arrays for a few **matched pairs** (same `start_time`) for 1.1.\n- Runs at `batch_size=1`, `num_workers=12`, `prefetch_factor=2` \u2192 bounded \u2248 42 GB RAM.\n- *Caveat to record in the output:* `phase=\"train\"` applies random channel masking and vertical\n  flips, so cached train features freeze one augmentation draw. Acceptable for a 16 K-param model\n  and it makes the baseline deterministic; the Surya runs read the live dataset and are unaffected.\n\n### Phase 2 \u2014 Task 1.1 and 1.2 (~20 min, CPU only)\n- **1.1**: for 2\u20133 matched wave/no-wave pairs, plot AIA193 at *t*, the full-res running difference,\n  and the **32\u00d732-pooled 128\u00d7128 map the baseline actually sees**, side by side within each pair.\n  Because the negative is the same event 1 h earlier, this is a controlled comparison and directly\n  answers whether wave signal survives the pooling kernel. Add per-map RD statistics.\n- **1.2**: the existing answer is **overfitting**, and the cached features let me *demonstrate* it\n  rather than assert it. `runs/.../baseline_running_diff/version_3` trained **16,385 parameters on\n  10 samples at lr=0.01**: train loss 0.814\u21920.0058 with train accuracy 1.0 from epoch 1, while val\n  loss rose monotonically 0.760\u21921.006. The tell that it is memorization and not mere miscalibration\n  is that **val AUROC froze at exactly 0.4000000059604645 from epoch 0 through 13** \u2014 the *ranking*\n  of val samples never changed, so the model only ever scaled a decision direction fixed by the\n  first few steps. I will reproduce this and then run a learning curve over\n  N \u2208 {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1231} (event-level stratified, seconds per fit) to show where\n  the train/val gap closes, plus weight-decay and pooling-kernel sweeps. This is the cheapest\n  rigorous result available and it also gives the baseline arm of your \"does val loss improve with\n  more images\" question.\n\n### Phase 3 \u2014 Surya LR probe (~25 min GPU)\nInsurance against repeating bug 3: three short runs at lr \u2208 {3e-5, 1e-4, 3e-4}, ~60 steps at\neffective batch 2 (~6 min each). Keep the largest LR whose loss decreases without spiking. Add\n`gradient_clip_val=1.0` for all real runs.\n\n### Phase 4 \u2014 Surya scaling study (remaining ~13 h GPU)\n**Iso-compute comparison** \u2014 each run gets the same sample budget (~3900 samples \u2248 3.4 h), so\n\"more images\" is tested at equal cost rather than confounded with more compute:\n\n| run | train N (events) | epochs | samples | est. wall |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 154 (77 pairs) | 25 | 3850 | ~3.3 h |\n| B | 462 (231 pairs) | 8 | 3696 | ~3.2 h |\n| C | 1231 (full) | 3 | 3693 | ~3.2 h |\n| D | best config, extended | \u2014 | remainder | ~3 h |\n\nRun in increasing-N order so an overrun still leaves A and B complete. Each: `batch_size=2`,\n`accumulate_grad_batches=4` (effective batch 8), `num_workers=6`, `prefetch_factor=2`\n(\u2248 42 GB RAM worst case), bf16-mixed, `max_time` cap, own wandb run with an explicit\n`wandb.finish()` between runs. Report best val loss vs N, and **2.4** = whether train and val loss\ndecrease, read off these curves.\n\n---\n\n## Files to create (nothing removed or overwritten)\n\n| path | responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `downstream_apps/test/4_finetune_wave_1D.py` | Adapted copy of `3_finetune_template_1D.py` (task 2.2). Wave imports; `--lr`, `--num-workers`, `--prefetch-factor`, `--accum`, `--max-time`, `--train-n`, `--subset-seed`, `--run-name`; builds its own DataLoaders from `build_helio_datasets` so it can set `prefetch_factor`, use `drop_last=False` for val, and take event-level stratified subsets; `gradient_clip_val`; pre-flight assertions; saves plots locally **and** to wandb. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/extract_baseline_features.py` | Phase 1 pass: cache warm + pooled RD `.npz` + pair arrays for 1.1. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_baseline_study.py` | Phase 2: 1.1 plots, 1.2 reproduction, learning curve, sweeps. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_surya_schedule.sh` | Phase 3\u20134 driver: sequential runs with per-run `max_time`, logging, continue-on-failure. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/configs/config_wave_full.yaml` | Full-scale run settings (new file; see decision 3). |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/results/` | Local plots (PNG) + metrics JSON, mirrored to wandb. |\n\nReused as-is: `build_helio_datasets`, `build_scalers`, `apply_peft_lora`, `load_pretrained_weights`,\n`HelioSpectformer1D.from_config`, `WaveMetrics`, `WaveLightningModule`, `waveDSDataset`,\n`load_wave_config`, `destandardize_channels`, `ConstantProbabilityModel`,\n`RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`.\n\n---\n\n## Verification\n\n- **Pre-flight (blocks training on failure):** 157/159 pretrained tensors load; trainable ==\n  3,157,761; `ts.shape == (B,13,2,4096,4096)`; `[LoRA] Adapted modules` list contains `attn.qkv`\n  and `attn.proj`; train/val sample counts == 1231/27 (or 1221/25 under decision 2a).\n- **Resource guards:** log `torch.cuda.max_memory_reserved()` and process RSS every N steps; abort\n  the run if RSS crosses a configured ceiling (default 70 GB) rather than letting the machine die.\n- **Phase 1:** `.npz` has 1231+27 rows with a 612/619 (or 611/611) label split; spot-check that a\n  pooled feature map is finite and non-constant.\n- **Phase 2:** the reproduced 10-sample run must show train loss \u2193 with val loss \u2191 and frozen AUROC,\n  matching `version_3` \u2014 confirming the diagnosis is real and not a logging artifact.\n- **Phase 3:** at least one LR shows monotone decreasing loss with no bf16 saturation.\n- **Phase 4:** each run leaves a `best-{epoch}-{val_loss}.ckpt`, a CSV log, a finished wandb run,\n  and PNGs on disk. Final deliverable: val loss vs N curve (Surya + baseline on the same axes) and\n  a written answer to 2.4.\n- **End-to-end smoke test before the unattended stretch:** run `4_finetune_wave_1D.py` with\n  `--train-n 8 --max-epochs 1` and confirm it completes, logs, checkpoints and finishes wandb cleanly.\n",
