      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "The plan is written. Summary of what the measurements changed:\n\n**Three findings reshape the obvious approach:**\n\n1. **Every Surya result you have so far was produced with a randomly-initialized tokenizer.** The checkpoint's patch embedding is `Conv2d(26,1280,16,16)` \u2014 13 channels \u00d7 **2** timesteps. Your config's `time_dim: 1` builds a 13-channel tokenizer, so it's silently skipped: I measured 156/159 tensors loading at `time_dim=1` vs 157/159 at `time_dim=2`. Fixing it also gives the running-difference baseline the 2 frames it needs, so **one dataset serves both tasks** \u2014 your same-dataset requirement falls out for free rather than needing a compromise.\n\n2. **The 2 TB cache is 98% useless for this task** \u2014 only 49 of 2457 needed train frames are cached. ~1.35 TB must be downloaded. Measured S3 at 600 MB/s so it's ~40 min of network, not a blocker, but it means epoch 1 is I/O-bound.\n\n3. **Data loading and GPU compute are nearly balanced**, not GPU-dominated: 15.2 s CPU per sample vs 3.1 s GPU. That sets `num_workers=8, prefetch_factor=1` (which is both faster *and* 14 GB lighter on RAM than `6\u00d72`) and pushes full-set epochs to ~75-80 min.\n\n**On VRAM maximization:** `batch_size=2` uses 42.4 GB of 47.7 (89%); batch 4 OOMs. That's already maximal, and since throughput is linear there'd be no speed gain anyway \u2014 so effective batch comes from gradient accumulation, free.\n\n**On your two questions:** 1.2 is textbook overfitting (16,385 params on 10 samples at lr=0.01), and the giveaway is that val AUROC froze at *exactly* 0.4 for 14 consecutive epochs \u2014 the ranking never changed, so the model only rescaled a direction fixed in the first few steps. Separately, your Surya run didn't overfit at all, it **numerically diverged** \u2014 same lr=0.01 carried onto 2.7 M LoRA params. And that 2.7 M is itself a bug: `target_modules` names layers Surya doesn't have, so attention got no adapters; correct targets give exactly 3,157,761.\n\nFor 1.1, one structural point matters more than the plot mechanics: your negatives are **the same event's pre-onset frame, 1 hour earlier, same active region** \u2014 not quiet Sun. So I'll plot *matched pairs* rather than two random samples, which makes it a controlled comparison.\n\n**Four decisions I need from you** (the interactive picker is disabled this session, so \u2014 in the plan file, with my recommendation in bold): validation strategy given only 27 val samples; whether to tighten match tolerance from 4d to 15min; whether to fix the shared config in place or add a new one; and what to protect if 18 h runs short.\n\nTell me your answers (or just say \"go with your defaults\") and I'll execute.",
