      "id": "f184b026-8e65-4658-84d7-d8e343f31988",
      "body": "All four of your points check out, and two change the plan materially.\n\n**Cadence \u2014 you're right and we agree.** Modal spacing in the bucket is 12 min (445,606 of ~449k gaps), and the RD pair `[-12, 0]` is exactly one consecutive step. The \"1 hour\" was a different quantity: `wave` sits at `start_time + 30.0 min` and `no wave` at `start_time \u2212 30.0 min` (std = 0 for all 914 events), so the *positive and negative samples* are 60 min apart. Both facts hold at once.\n\n**But your 40-minute figure exposes a real risk.** The positive sample sits at onset **+30 min**, so its running difference spans **+18 \u2192 +30 min**. If waves are only visible ~40 min, we're sampling the faint tail, near the end of the window. That could be the actual reason the RD baseline never generalized \u2014 not just the small sample size. I've made this a first-class experiment in 1.1.\n\n**LoRA rank won't buy batch size** \u2014 measured: at r=8 the adapters are 24 MB of weights+grads+Adam against a 33 GB peak, i.e. 0.07%. Memory is dominated by the 1.74 GB/sample fp32 input and activations. And batch size isn't worth buying anyway: B=3 *does* fit with bf16 inputs (46.5 GB), but it's **18% slower per sample** (3.59 s vs 3.11 s). The GPU is already compute-saturated by one 65,536-token sample \u2014 B=1 is 3.05, B=2 is 3.11, B=3 is 3.59. So maximizing VRAM actively hurts throughput here; rank stays a *capacity* knob (worth ablating r=4 vs 8 on a small train set), not a memory one.\n\n**The head is functionally correct but half-wasted.** Single logit \u2192 `squeeze` \u2192 `(B,)`, BCE-compatible, shapes line up with `WaveMetrics`. However `head_linear` (1280\u21921280) feeds `head_unembed` (1280\u21921) with **no nonlinearity between them** \u2014 at eval that composition is exactly one linear map, so those **1,639,680 params add zero representational capacity** while being 52% of all trainable weights. Setting `penultimate_linear_layer: false` cuts trainable from 3,157,761 to **1,518,081** and removes pure overfitting surface.\n\n**Fewer variables for 1.1: yes, dramatically.** The RD baseline uses only AIA193 (1 of 13 channels). Reading just that variable is **11.8\u00d7 faster** (0.40 s vs 4.69 s) and signum-log drops 4.67 s \u2192 0.39 s, so per-sample CPU falls from **15.2 s to 1.2 s** and RAM from 1.74 GB to 0.134 GB.",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "body": "",
      "id": "3987beef-5ace-4ccc-9f75-208423a45076",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
