      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01BNGjrM7GtDhH7UHrxqehEv",
            "diffs": [
              {
                "old_text": "\n## Context\n\nYou need publishable results in 18 unattended hours on binary classification of EUV wave\nevents, comparing simple baselines against a Surya LoRA fine-tune. Prior attempts produced\ntwo failed runs (a baseline that overfit 10 samples, and a Surya run that numerically\ndiverged), and the notebook path has silent misconfigurations. Before committing hours of\nGPU time I measured the hardware, the dataset, and the model, because three of those\nmeasurements invalidate the obvious plan. Everything below is measured, not estimated.\n\n---\n\n## The numbers that drive every decision\n\n**Hardware** \u2014 1\u00d7 L40S, 47.7 GB VRAM, bf16 OK; 16 CPUs; 124 GB RAM; GPU idle.\n\n**Surya throughput** (LoRA + bf16 autocast + grad checkpointing on all 10 layers, measured):\n\n| batch | s/step | s/sample | peak alloc | peak reserved |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 3.05 | 3.05 | 17.5 GB | 21.1 GB |\n| 2 | 6.23 | 3.12 | 33.4 GB | **42.4 GB (89% of VRAM)** |\n| 4 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | **CUDA OOM** |\n\n\u2192 **`batch_size: 2` is the hard maximum and already maximizes VRAM.** Throughput is linear\n(compute-bound), so a bigger batch would buy no speed anyway \u2014 larger *effective* batch must\ncome from gradient accumulation, which is free. **Budget 3.1 s per training sample.**\n\n**RAM is the real danger.** `ts` is `(B,13,2,4096,4096)` float32 = **1.74 GB per sample**.\nWorst-case RSS \u2248 `num_workers \u00d7 prefetch_factor \u00d7 batch_size \u00d7 1.74 GB`. At the config's\n`num_workers: 8` with default `prefetch_factor=2` and batch 2 that is **56 GB**, before the\nmain process, pinned-memory copies and page cache. This is the \"collapse the AWS machine\"\nrisk, and it must be bounded explicitly rather than left to defaults.\n\n**The pipeline is CPU/IO-balanced against the GPU, not GPU-dominated** \u2014 measured per sample:\n2 \u00d7 4.7 s to read 13 channels from a cached NetCDF (EFS+HDF5 gives ~185 MB/s per stream, well\nbelow `dd`'s 585 MB/s) + 4.7 s signum-log + 0.4 s z-score = **15.2 s of CPU per sample**. To feed\none sample per 3.1 s therefore needs **\u2265 5 workers**, and aggregate EFS demand at 8 workers\n(~1.5 GB/s) may itself throttle. Consequence: **`num_workers=8, prefetch_factor=1` is the right\noperating point** \u2014 more CPU parallelism *and less* memory than `6 \u00d7 2` (27.8 GB vs 41.8 GB) \u2014 and\nepoch time should be budgeted at the data-limited rate (~3.6 s/sample, **~75-80 min for the full\n1231**), not the ideal 3.1. Set `OMP_NUM_THREADS=2` so 8 workers do not oversubscribe 16 CPUs.\n\n**Dataset** (measured by replicating the `merge_asof` logic under `[-12, 0]`, T=2):\ntrain **1231** (612 wave / 619 no-wave), val **27** (13/14), test **54** (25/29).\nCatalog is exactly 914/914 balanced.\n\n**The label structure is the most important scientific finding.** Every event contributes a\n*pair* sharing one `start_time`: `wave` = `start_time + 30 min`, `no wave` = `start_time \u2212 30 min`.\nSo the negative is *the same event's own pre-onset frame, one hour earlier, same active region*.\nThe classes are matched by construction on limb position, disk activity and AR presence \u2014 there\nare no cheap shortcuts, and the real task is \"is this frame 30 min after or 30 min before onset\".\nThat is a much finer discrimination than wave-vs-quiet-Sun and it resets what counts as a good\nAUROC here. It also means class balance can be held exact by subsampling at the *event* level.\n\n**The 2 TB S3 cache is nearly useless for this task** \u2014 of the frames this task needs, only\n**49/2457 train (2.0%)**, 21/54 val, 0/108 test are cached. The 3699 cached files are unrelated\ndates. So **~1.35 TB must be downloaded fresh.** Measured S3 throughput saturates at\n**~600 MB/s with \u22658 parallel fetches** (119 MB/s serial), so that is ~40 min of pure network\ntime \u2014 and at 1.1 GB per sample (2 frames) it is ~1.9 s/sample, comfortably hidden behind the\n3.1 s/sample GPU compute *provided enough workers*. EFS re-reads at 585 MB/s. Not a blocker,\nbut it does mean **epoch 1 is I/O-bound and epochs 2+ are not**.\n\n---\n\n## Confirmed bugs (all verified by execution, not inspection)\n\n1. **The pretrained tokenizer is silently discarded.** The checkpoint's patch embedding is\n   `Conv2d(26,1280,16,16)` \u2014 13 channels \u00d7 **2** timesteps. The config's\n   `time_delta_input_minutes: [0]` / `time_embedding.time_dim: 1` builds a 13-channel tokenizer,\n   so `load_pretrained_weights` skips it and it stays **randomly initialized**. Measured:\n   `time_dim=1` \u2192 **156/159** tensors load; `time_dim=2` \u2192 **157/159** (only the pretraining\n   decoder `unembed.*` is skipped, which is correct). Every Surya result so far was produced\n   with a random tokenizer.\n   *Fixing this to `time_dim: 2` also gives the running-difference baseline the 2 frames it\n   needs \u2014 so one dataset serves both tasks, satisfying your same-dataset requirement.*\n\n2. **LoRA never adapted attention.** `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]`\n   \u2014 Surya has none of those names (q/k/v are fused as `attn.qkv`, output is `attn.proj`). PEFT\n   only errors when *nothing* matches, so `fc1`/`fc2` matched and the rest were silently ignored.\n   Measured: buggy targets \u2192 **2,666,241** trainable; correct `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`\n   \u2192 **3,157,761**, exactly the documented figure. Your diverged run logged \"2.7 M\", confirming it.\n\n3. **`learning_rate: 0.01` diverges Surya.** Prior run: losses 10\u00b2\u201310\u2074, exact `0.0` bf16 values,\n   val acc/AUROC pinned at 0.5. That LR was tuned on the 16 K-param logistic baseline.\n\n4. **`max_samples` subsetting is chronologically biased.** `waveDSDataset` head-slices a frame\n   sorted by `ds_index`, so `max_samples: 10` yields the 5 earliest event pairs (all June\u2013July\n   2010). Using it for a data-scaling study would confound \"more data\" with \"different epoch of\n   the solar cycle\". The scaling study must use seeded random **event-level** subsets instead.\n\n5. **`build_helio_dataloaders` sets `drop_last=True` for validation too**, silently discarding\n   1 of 27 val samples (3.7%). It also does not expose `prefetch_factor`, which is exactly the\n   knob needed to bound RAM.\n\n### Task 2.1 \u2014 state of `2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb`\nYour fixes all landed: it now runs top-to-bottom with sequential exec counts, calls\n`load_wave_config`, has `import numpy as np` + `label_transform`, and uses `WaveMetrics` /\n`WaveLightningModule` and `max_epochs = cfg.max_epochs`. What remains: bugs **1, 2, 3** above\nreach it through the shared YAML (so its Surya model has a random tokenizer, starved LoRA, and a\ndivergent LR), `max_number_of_samples=10` is hardcoded rather than `cfg.data.max_samples`, no\n`wandb.finish()` (causes run reuse), and several markdown cells still describe the flare\nregression task \u2014 `FlareDataConfig`/`load_flare_config`, and \"Mean Squared Error\"/\"RRSE\" where the\ncode computes BCE/accuracy/AUROC.\n\n---\n\n## Open decisions \u2014 I need your call on these four\n\nI could not use the interactive picker (disabled this session), so tell me which you want and I\nwill proceed; the plan below assumes the **bold** option.\n\n1. **Validation is only 27 samples** (1 sample = 3.7%), so `val_loss` checkpointing is nearly a\n   coin flip. **(a) Use the 27-sample val for monitoring + evaluate the best checkpoint on the\n   official 54-sample test split for a final, less-noisy number** (81 held out, +60 GB download,\n   no extra training time, official splits intact). (b) Official val only. (c) Re-split from\n   `surya_aws_s3_full_index.csv` for a few-hundred-sample val (recovers the 254 catalog rows in\n   2012/2022 that are missing from *all* official splits, but breaks comparability).\n\n2. **Match tolerance.** 99.2% of matches land within 15 min, but the 10 loosest train matches (up\n   to 1 day off) are 9-to-1 \"no wave\" \u2014 that skew is the whole reason balance is 612/619 rather\n   than 611/611. **(a) Tighten `ds_time_tolerance` to `15min`** (costs 10 train + 2 val samples,\n   restores exact balance, guarantees every label sits within one cadence step of its image).\n   (b) Keep `4d`.\n\n3. **Where corrected settings live.** **(a) Fix the outright bugs (1 and 2) in place in\n   `configs/config_script.yaml` with old values kept as comments \u2014 so your notebooks become\n   correct too \u2014 plus a new config for full-scale run settings.** (b) New config only, leaving\n   `config_script.yaml` untouched (zero risk to notebooks, but the notebook keeps training a\n   random tokenizer).\n\n4. **If time runs short, what to protect.** **(a) The data-scaling curve, since it answers your\n   \"does val loss improve with more images\" question** \u2014 the largest run gets fewer epochs.\n   (b) One full-set run to convergence as the headline. (c) Baselines + diagnosis first.\n\n---\n\n## Plan\n\nOrdered so cheap, high-information work lands first and partial completion still yields results.\nEvery Surya run gets a hard `Trainer(max_time=...)` wall-clock cap so no single run can starve\nthe rest.\n\n**Budget: 0.5 h fixes + 1.5 h data pass + 0.35 h baseline study + 0.5 h LR probe + 12.1 h Surya\nruns \u2248 15 h, leaving ~3 h slack** for overruns, the test-split evaluation, and writeup. Tasks 1.1,\n1.2 and the complete baseline arm are all done inside the first ~2.5 h, so you have real results\nearly regardless of what happens to the GPU runs overnight.\n\n### Phase 0 \u2014 Fixes and pre-flight (~30 min, no GPU)\n- Apply bug fixes 1\u20132 per decision 3; set a safe Surya `learning_rate` (1e-4 default, confirmed\n  by Phase 3).\n- Fix notebook 2's remaining items: `cfg.data.max_samples`, add `wandb.finish()`, correct the\n  stale flare markdown (MSE/RRSE \u2192 BCE/accuracy/AUROC, `FlareDataConfig` \u2192 `WaveDataConfig`).\n- Pre-flight assertions in the new script, failing loudly rather than training something wrong:\n  `load_pretrained_weights` reports **157/159**, trainable == **3,157,761** under LoRA, and\n  `ts.shape[2] == 2`.\n\n### Phase 1 \u2014 One data pass: cache warm + baseline features + 1.1 plots (~90 min, no GPU)\nA single pass over train+val(+test) that does three jobs at once. This is the key scheduling\nmove: the baseline's features are a deterministic 128\u00d7128 pooled map (**64 KB/sample**) while\n*loading* a sample costs 1.1 GB, so paying the I/O once buys unlimited cheap baseline work.\n- Warms the EFS cache with all ~1.35 TB the Surya runs will need (so Surya epoch 1 is not I/O-bound).\n- Writes pooled AIA193 running-difference features + labels + timestamps to a small `.npz` (~80 MB).\n- Saves full-resolution and pooled RD arrays for a few **matched pairs** (same `start_time`) for 1.1.\n- No GPU contention, so run it wide: `batch_size=1`, `num_workers=12`, `prefetch_factor=1`\n  \u2192 bounded \u2248 21 GB RAM. At 15.2 s CPU/sample and 12 workers this is ~27 min of CPU-limited work\n  for 1258 samples, but S3 fetch of ~1.35 TB at ~600 MB/s is the floor, so budget **~60-90 min**.\n- **Caching is exactly equivalent to the live dataset here** \u2014 I verified the pipeline applies *no*\n  augmentation as configured: `num_mask_aia_channels` defaults to 0 and is not passed by\n  `_base_dataset_kwargs`, `drop_hmi_probability` is 0.0, and `random_vert_flip` defaults to False\n  and is never set. So there is no augmentation draw to freeze, and train/val differ only by index.\n  (Had masking been active this shortcut would have needed a caveat; it does not.)\n\n### Phase 2 \u2014 Task 1.1 and 1.2 (~20 min, CPU only)\n- **1.1**: for 2\u20133 matched wave/no-wave pairs, plot AIA193 at *t*, the full-res running difference,\n  and the **32\u00d732-pooled 128\u00d7128 map the baseline actually sees**, side by side within each pair.\n  Because the negative is the same event 1 h earlier, this is a controlled comparison and directly\n  answers whether wave signal survives the pooling kernel. Add per-map RD statistics.\n  Frame ordering is verified correct for this: `time_delta_input_minutes` is sorted ascending with\n  the offset-0 \"now\" frame always last, so the model's `x[:,-1] - x[:,0]` really is now-minus-12-min\n  and the plotted difference carries the same sign the baseline trains on.\n- **1.2**: the existing answer is **overfitting**, and the cached features let me *demonstrate* it\n  rather than assert it. `runs/.../baseline_running_diff/version_3` trained **16,385 parameters on\n  10 samples at lr=0.01**: train loss 0.814\u21920.0058 with train accuracy 1.0 from epoch 1, while val\n  loss rose monotonically 0.760\u21921.006. The tell that it is memorization and not mere miscalibration\n  is that **val AUROC froze at exactly 0.4000000059604645 from epoch 0 through 13** \u2014 the *ranking*\n  of val samples never changed, so the model only ever scaled a decision direction fixed by the\n  first few steps. I will reproduce this and then run a learning curve over\n  N \u2208 {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1231} (event-level stratified, seconds per fit) to show where\n  the train/val gap closes, plus weight-decay and pooling-kernel sweeps. This is the cheapest\n  rigorous result available and it also gives the baseline arm of your \"does val loss improve with\n  more images\" question.\n\n### Phase 3 \u2014 Surya LR probe (~25 min GPU)\nInsurance against repeating bug 3: three short runs at lr \u2208 {3e-5, 1e-4, 3e-4}, ~60 steps at\neffective batch 2 (~6 min each). Keep the largest LR whose loss decreases without spiking. Add\n`gradient_clip_val=1.0` for all real runs.\n\n### Phase 4 \u2014 Surya scaling study (remaining ~13 h GPU)\n**Iso-compute comparison** \u2014 each run gets the same sample budget (~3900 samples \u2248 3.4 h), so\n\"more images\" is tested at equal cost rather than confounded with more compute:\n\n| run | train N (events) | epochs | samples | est. wall (@3.6 s/sample) | `max_time` cap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 154 (77 pairs) | 12 | 1848 | ~1.9 h | 2.5 h |\n| B | 462 (231 pairs) | 6 | 2772 | ~2.8 h | 3.5 h |\n| C | 1231 (full) | 4 | 4924 | ~4.9 h | 6.0 h |\n| D | best config, extended | \u2014 | remainder | ~2-3 h | rest |\n\nPrimary comparison is **best val loss vs N under a fixed per-run wall-clock cap** \u2014 each run stops\nat whichever of `max_epochs` / `max_time` comes first, which is both the honest \"does more data\nhelp\" experiment and inherently safe for unattended running. Note explicitly in the writeup that C\ngets more compute than A (more data at comparable epochs *is* more compute); I will also report the\niso-compute slice (val loss at equal samples-seen) from the logged curves so both readings are\navailable without extra runs.\n\nRun in increasing-N order so an overrun still leaves A and B complete. Each: `batch_size=2`,\n`accumulate_grad_batches=4` (effective batch 8), `num_workers=8`, `prefetch_factor=1`\n(\u2248 27.8 GB RAM worst case), `OMP_NUM_THREADS=2`, bf16-mixed, `gradient_clip_val=1.0`, its own\nwandb run with an explicit `wandb.finish()` between runs. Report best val loss vs N, and **2.4** =\nwhether train and val loss decrease, read off these curves.\n\n---\n",
                "path": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/i-m-running-a-ipynb-melodic-seal.md",
                "new_text": "\n## Context\n\nYou need publishable results in 18 unattended hours on binary classification of EUV wave events,\ncomparing simple baselines against a Surya LoRA fine-tune. Two prior attempts failed (a baseline\nthat overfit 10 samples; a Surya run that numerically diverged) and the notebook path has silent\nmisconfigurations. Everything below is **measured**, not estimated \u2014 several measurements\ninvalidate the obvious plan, and one of your own observations exposed a scientific risk that now\ndrives the ordering of the work.\n\n---\n\n## Measurements that drive every decision\n\n**Hardware** \u2014 1\u00d7 L40S, 47.7 GB VRAM, bf16 OK; 16 CPUs; 124 GB RAM; GPU idle.\n\n**Surya throughput / VRAM** (LoRA + bf16 autocast + grad checkpointing on all 10 layers):\n\n| batch | input dtype | s/step | **s/sample** | peak alloc | peak reserved |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | fp32 | 3.05 | **3.05** | 17.5 GB | 18.0 GB |\n| 2 | fp32 | 6.22 | **3.11** | 33.4 GB | 34.6 GB |\n| 3 | fp32 | \u2014 | OOM | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| 2 | bf16 | 6.22 | **3.11** | 31.6 GB | 32.8 GB |\n| 3 | bf16 | 10.76 | **3.59** | 46.5 GB | 47.0 GB |\n| 4 | bf16 | \u2014 | OOM | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n\n**Maximizing VRAM is the wrong objective here.** The GPU is already compute-saturated by a single\n65,536-token sample, so throughput is flat-to-worse in batch size: B=3 fits (with bf16 inputs) but\nis **18% slower per sample**. Use **batch_size 2 + `accumulate_grad_batches` 4** (effective batch 8,\n34.6 GB, near-optimal 3.11 s/sample); B=1 is equally valid and halves both VRAM and worker RAM.\nBudget **3.1 s per training sample**.\n\n**LoRA rank is not a memory lever.** Adapters at r=8 are 1,515,520 params \u2248 **24 MB** of\nweights+grads+Adam, i.e. **0.07%** of the 33 GB peak (r=32 is still only 97 MB). Memory is dominated\nby the 1.74 GB/sample fp32 input plus activations. Rank remains a useful *capacity* knob on a small\ntraining set, so r \u2208 {4, 8} is an ablation, not a memory fix.\n\n**RAM is the real hazard.** `ts` is `(B,13,2,4096,4096)` fp32 = **1.74 GB/sample**. Worst-case RSS \u2248\n`num_workers \u00d7 prefetch_factor \u00d7 batch_size \u00d7 1.74 GB`; the config's `num_workers: 8` with default\n`prefetch_factor=2` at batch 2 is **56 GB** before the main process, pinned copies and page cache.\nMust be bounded explicitly. Chosen: **`num_workers=8, prefetch_factor=1`** \u2192 27.8 GB, which is both\nfaster *and* lighter than `6 \u00d7 2` (41.8 GB). Set `OMP_NUM_THREADS=2` (8 workers on 16 CPUs).\n\n**Per-sample CPU cost, and why task 1 is far cheaper than task 2:**\n\n| read | time | signum-log | total/sample | RAM/sample |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| all 13 channels | 4.69 s \u00d72 frames | 4.67 s | **15.2 s** | 1.74 GB |\n| **aia193 only** | 0.40 s \u00d72 frames | 0.39 s | **1.2 s** | 0.134 GB |\n\nThe RD baseline uses only AIA193, so task 1 runs **12.7\u00d7 cheaper** than task 2. Note the 15.2 s\nfigure means the Surya pipeline is **CPU/IO-balanced against the GPU, not GPU-dominated** \u2014 \u22655\nworkers are needed just to keep it fed, so budget the data-limited rate (~3.6 s/sample) for epochs.\n\n**Cadence and label geometry (both verified):** bucket modal spacing is **12 min** (445,606 of\n~449k gaps), so the RD pair `[-12, 0]` is exactly **one consecutive frame step** \u2014 as you wanted.\nSeparately, the catalog places `wave` at `start_time + 30.0 min` and `no wave` at\n`start_time \u2212 30.0 min` (std = 0 across all 914 events), so the positive and negative *samples* of\none event are 60 min apart. Both statements are true; they describe different things.\n\n**Dataset:** catalog is exactly 914/914 balanced. Matched against the day-disjoint Surya indices\nunder `[-12, 0]`: train **1231**, val **27**, test **54**. Cache coverage of the frames this task\nneeds is only **2%** (49/2457 train), so frames must be fetched; measured S3 saturates at\n**~600 MB/s** with \u22658 parallel fetches. Since you confirmed the dataset may shrink, we use a\n**seeded, event-level stratified subset capped at 384 train** \u2014 822 frames \u2248 460 GB \u2248 13 min of\ndownload \u2014 which buys many more epochs per run and cleaner curves than 4 epochs on 1231.\n\n---\n\n## Confirmed bugs (all verified by execution)\n\n1. **The pretrained tokenizer is silently discarded.** Checkpoint patch embedding is\n   `Conv2d(26,1280,16,16)` = 13 channels \u00d7 **2** timesteps. The config's `time_dim: 1` builds a\n   13-channel tokenizer, so it is skipped and stays **random**. Measured: `time_dim=1` \u2192 **156/159**\n   tensors load; `time_dim=2` \u2192 **157/159** (only the pretraining decoder `unembed.*`, correctly).\n   *Every Surya result so far was produced with a random tokenizer.* Fixing it also gives the RD\n   baseline its 2 frames, so one dataset serves both tasks.\n2. **LoRA never adapted attention.** `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]`\n   \u2014 Surya has none of those (q/k/v fused as `attn.qkv`, output `attn.proj`). PEFT only errors when\n   *nothing* matches, so only `fc1`/`fc2` were adapted: **2,666,241** trainable vs **3,157,761**\n   with `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`. Your diverged run logged \"2.7 M\", confirming it.\n3. **`learning_rate: 0.01` diverges Surya** (losses 10\u00b2\u201310\u2074, exact `0.0` bf16 values, val\n   acc/AUROC pinned at 0.5). That LR was tuned on the 16 K-param logistic baseline.\n4. **`max_samples` subsetting is chronologically biased** \u2014 `waveDSDataset` head-slices a frame\n   sorted by `ds_index`, so `max_samples: 10` gives the 5 earliest pairs (all June\u2013July 2010).\n   The scaling study must use seeded random **event-level** subsets instead.\n5. **`build_helio_dataloaders` sets `drop_last=True` for validation too**, silently dropping 1 of\n   27 val samples (3.7%); it also does not expose `prefetch_factor`, the knob needed to bound RAM.\n6. **The head is correct but half-redundant.** `head_unembed` gives a single logit,\n   `squeeze`\u2192`(B,)`, BCE-compatible, shapes match `WaveMetrics` \u2014 functionally fine for\n   classification. But `head_linear` (1280\u21921280) feeds `head_unembed` (1280\u21921) with **no\n   nonlinearity between them**, so at eval it is exactly one linear map: those **1,639,680 params\n   add zero representational capacity** while being 52% of all trainable weights. Measured with\n   `penultimate_linear_layer: false`: head = 2,561 params, total trainable **1,518,081**. Adopt\n   `false` as the default (less overfitting surface on a few-hundred-sample set) and A/B it.\n\n### Task 2.1 \u2014 state of `2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb`\nYour fixes all landed: runs top-to-bottom with sequential exec counts, `load_wave_config`,\n`import numpy as np` + `label_transform`, `WaveMetrics` / `WaveLightningModule`,\n`max_epochs = cfg.max_epochs`. Remaining: bugs **1, 2, 3, 6** reach it via the shared YAML (random\ntokenizer, starved LoRA, divergent LR, redundant head); `max_number_of_samples=10` hardcoded rather\nthan `cfg.data.max_samples`; no `wandb.finish()` (causes run reuse); and several markdown cells\nstill describe the flare regression task (`FlareDataConfig`/`load_flare_config`, \"Mean Squared\nError\"/\"RRSE\" where the code computes BCE/accuracy/AUROC).\n\n---\n\n## Plan\n\nCheap, high-information work first; every Surya run gets a hard `Trainer(max_time=...)` cap so no\nrun can starve the rest.\n\n### Phase 0 \u2014 Fixes and pre-flight (~30 min, no GPU)\nFix bugs 1, 2, 6 in `configs/config_script.yaml` with old values kept as comments (so the notebooks\nbecome correct too), plus a new config for run-scale settings. Fix notebook 2's remaining items.\nPre-flight assertions that fail loudly rather than training something wrong: **157/159** tensors\nload, trainable == **1,518,081** (or 3,157,761 with the penultimate layer on), `ts.shape[2] == 2`,\nand the `[LoRA] Adapted modules` list contains `attn.qkv` and `attn.proj`.\n\n### Phase 1 \u2014 Data pass: cache warm + baseline features + 1.1 arrays (~30-45 min, no GPU)\nOne pass over the 384-train/27-val/54-test subset that does three jobs. Full 13-channel `.nc` files\nare downloaded (Surya needs them later, so pay it once), but **only AIA193 is decoded**, so CPU cost\nis 1.2 s/sample. Writes pooled 128\u00d7128 RD features + labels + timestamps to a small `.npz`\n(~64 KB/sample), and full-resolution arrays for the 1.1 figures. Runs at `num_workers=12,\nprefetch_factor=1` (~2 GB RAM at 1 channel). **Caching is exactly equivalent to the live dataset**:\nI verified no augmentation is active \u2014 `num_mask_aia_channels` defaults to 0 and is never passed,\n`drop_hmi_probability` is 0.0, `random_vert_flip` defaults to False \u2014 so there is no augmentation\ndraw to freeze and train/val differ only by index.\n\n### Phase 2 \u2014 Task 1.1, including the wave-visibility test (~30 min, CPU only)\n- **1.1 as asked:** for 2-3 matched wave/no-wave pairs, plot AIA193 at *t*, the full-resolution\n  running difference, and the **32\u00d732-pooled 128\u00d7128 map the baseline actually sees**, side by side\n  within each pair. Because the negative is the same event 1 h earlier, this is a controlled\n  comparison. Frame ordering is verified correct: `time_delta_input_minutes` is sorted ascending\n  with the offset-0 \"now\" frame last, so the model's `x[:,-1] - x[:,0]` really is\n  now-minus-12-min and the plotted difference carries the sign the baseline trains on.\n- **1.1 extended \u2014 the risk your 40-min figure exposes.** Positives sit at onset **+30 min**, so\n  their RD spans **+18 \u2192 +30 min**, i.e. the faint tail of the visibility window. For ~6 events I\n  will extract the RD at onset **+12, +24, +36, +48 min** and measure RD contrast versus offset.\n  If signal peaks near +12 and has decayed by +30, then the label timing \u2014 not the sample count \u2014 is\n  the primary reason the baseline cannot generalize. Remedy if confirmed: emit a **new** catalog CSV\n  (original untouched) with the positive offset moved to the measured optimum, and re-run. This is\n  cheap and could be the most consequential result of the whole 18 hours, which is why it comes\n  before any GPU time.\n\n### Phase 3 \u2014 Task 1.2 and the baseline arm (~20 min, CPU only)\nThe cached features make the baseline instant, so 1.2 gets demonstrated rather than asserted.\n`runs/.../baseline_running_diff/version_3` trained **16,385 params on 10 samples at lr=0.01**: train\nloss 0.814\u21920.0058, train accuracy 1.0 from epoch 1, val loss rising monotonically 0.760\u21921.006. The\ntell that it is memorization and not miscalibration is that **val AUROC froze at exactly\n0.4000000059604645 from epoch 0 through 13** \u2014 the *ranking* of val samples never changed, so the\nmodel only rescaled a direction fixed in the first few steps. Separately your Surya run did not\noverfit at all, it **diverged** (bug 3). I will reproduce the overfit, then run a learning curve over\nN \u2208 {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 384} (event-level stratified, seconds per fit) plus weight-decay and\npooling-kernel sweeps \u2014 giving the baseline arm of the \"does val loss improve with more images\"\nquestion at negligible cost.\n\n### Phase 4 \u2014 Surya LR probe (~30 min GPU)\nInsurance against repeating bug 3: short runs at lr \u2208 {3e-5, 1e-4, 3e-4}, ~60 steps each. Keep the\nlargest LR whose loss decreases without bf16 saturation. All real runs use `gradient_clip_val=1.0`.\n\n### Phase 5 \u2014 Surya scaling study + ablations (~11 h GPU, remainder as slack)\n\n| run | train N (events) | epochs | est. wall | `max_time` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 48 (24 pairs) | 30 | ~1.3 h | 1.8 h |\n| B | 96 (48 pairs) | 24 | ~2.0 h | 2.5 h |\n| C | 192 (96 pairs) | 18 | ~3.0 h | 3.5 h |\n| D | 384 (192 pairs) | 14 | ~4.7 h | 5.5 h |\n| E | ablations at best N: r=4 vs 8, penultimate head on/off | \u2014 | slack | rest |\n\nIncreasing-N order, so an overrun still leaves the smaller runs complete. Each run: `batch_size=2`,\n`accumulate_grad_batches=4`, `num_workers=8`, `prefetch_factor=1` (\u224827.8 GB RAM),\n`OMP_NUM_THREADS=2`, bf16-mixed, `gradient_clip_val=1.0`, `max_time` cap, its own wandb run with an\nexplicit `wandb.finish()` between runs. Primary result: **best val loss vs N** with the baseline on\nthe same axes; I will also report the iso-compute slice (val loss at equal samples-seen) from the\nlogged curves so both readings are available without extra runs. **2.4** is read off these curves.\n\n---\n"
              },
