              "command": "python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\np = Path(\"2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb\")\nnb = json.loads(p.read_text())\ncells = nb[\"cells\"]\n\ndef replace_in(i, old, new, label):\n    src = \"\".join(cells[i][\"source\"])\n    assert old in src, f\"[{i}] {label}: pattern not found:\\n{old[:160]}\"\n    cells[i][\"source\"] = src.replace(old, new).splitlines(keepends=True)\n    print(f\"  ok  [{i}] {label}\")\n\ndef set_source(i, text, label):\n    cells[i][\"source\"] = text.splitlines(keepends=True)\n    print(f\"  ok  [{i}] {label} (replaced)\")\n\n# --- Cell 9: still describes the flare task's label normalization -------------\nset_source(9, \"\"\"## Define Downstream (DS) datasets\n\nThis child class takes as input all expected HelioFM parameters, plus additonal parameters relevant to the downstream application.  Here we focus in particular to the DS index and parameters necessary to combine it with the HelioFM index.\n\nAnother important component of creating a dataset class for your DS is turning the catalog's label column into something the loss can consume.  Here the catalog's `class` column holds the strings `\"wave\"` / `\"no wave\"`, and `label_transform` maps them to `1.0` / `0.0` for the binary cross-entropy loss.\n\nEach catalog event appears **twice**: once as `wave` at `start_time + 30 min` and once as `no wave` at `start_time - 30 min`.  The two rows share a `start_time`, so they are a matched pair of the same event an hour apart \u2014 which is what makes the classification a controlled comparison rather than a comparison of different active regions.\n\nIn this case we will define both a training and a validation dataset using the indices pointed at in the config\n\n**_Important:  the cap on dataset size comes from `data.max_samples` in the config (10 while exploring), not from a number typed into this notebook.  Keep this in mind in case the database seems smaller than you expect \u2014 and note that the cap head-slices a frame sorted by time, so it gives you the EARLIEST samples, not a random sample._**\n\"\"\", \"cell 9 markdown\")\n\n# --- Cell 11: hardcoded sample cap, and \"flare-specific\" --------------------\nreplace_in(11,\n    \"# It also handles two details that are easy to get wrong by hand: the validation set gets\\n\"\n    \"# phase=\\\"val\\\" (no random channel masking or flips), and only the training loader shuffles.\",\n    \"# It also handles two details that are easy to get wrong by hand: the validation set gets\\n\"\n    \"# phase=\\\"val\\\" (no random channel masking or flips), and only the training loader shuffles.\\n\"\n    \"#\\n\"\n    \"# One thing it does NOT expose is prefetch_factor, and it sets drop_last=True for the\\n\"\n    \"# validation loader too. Neither matters at max_samples: 10, but both do at full scale --\\n\"\n    \"# see experiments/wave_common.py:build_wave_loader() for why and what to do instead.\",\n    \"loader caveats\")\nreplace_in(11,\n    \"# the list you replace when you fork the template.\",\n    \"# the list you replace when you fork the template.\",\n    \"no-op check\")\nreplace_in(11,\n    \"    max_number_of_samples=10,\",\n    \"    # From the config, not a literal: data.max_samples is the one knob for dataset size,\\n\"\n    \"    # so the notebook and 4_finetune_wave_1D.py cannot disagree about how much data ran.\\n\"\n    \"    max_number_of_samples=cfg.data.max_samples,\",\n    \"max_number_of_samples from config\")\nreplace_in(11,\n    \"# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\\n\"\n    \"# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\\n\"\n    \"# like written out. Only the flare-specific arguments are passed here, which is exactly\",\n    \"# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\\n\"\n    \"# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\\n\"\n    \"# like written out. Only the wave-specific arguments are passed here, which is exactly\",\n    \"wave-specific wording\")\n\np.write_text(json.dumps(nb, indent=1) + \"\\n\")\nPY",
              "description": "Fix notebook cells 9 and 11"
            },
            "kind": "execute",
            "title": "python - <<'PY' import json from pathlib import Path p = Path(\"2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb\") nb = json.loads(p.read_text()) cells = nb[\"cells\"] def replace_in(i, old, new, label): src = \"\".join(cells[i][\"source\"]) assert old in src, f\"[{i}] {label}: pattern not found:\\n{old[:160]}\" cells[i][\"source\"] = src.replace(old, new).splitlines(keepends=True) print(f\" ok [{i}] {label}\") def set_source(i, text, label): cells[i][\"source\"] = text.splitlines(keepends=True) print(f\" ok [{i}] {label} (replaced)\") # --- Cell 9: still describes the flare task's label normalization ------------- set_source(9, \"\"\"## Define Downstream (DS) datasets This child class takes as input all expected HelioFM parameters, plus additonal parameters relevant to the downstream application. Here we focus in particular to the DS index and parameters necessary to combine it with the HelioFM index. Another important component of creating a dataset class for your DS is turning the catalog's label column into something the loss can consume. Here the catalog's `class` column holds the strings `\"wave\"` / `\"no wave\"`, and `label_transform` maps them to `1.0` / `0.0` for the binary cross-entropy loss. Each catalog event appears **twice**: once as `wave` at `start_time + 30 min` and once as `no wave` at `start_time - 30 min`. The two rows share a `start_time`, so they are a matched pair of the same event an hour apart \u2014 which is what makes the classification a controlled comparison rather than a comparison of different active regions. In this case we will define both a training and a validation dataset using the indices pointed at in the config **_Important: the cap on dataset size comes from `data.max_samples` in the config (10 while exploring), not from a number typed into this notebook. Keep this in mind in case the database seems smaller than you expect \u2014 and note that the cap head-slices a frame sorted by time, so it gives you the EARLIEST samples, not a random sample._** \"\"\", \"cell 9 markdown\") # --- Cell 11: hardcoded sample cap, and \"flare-specific\" -------------------- replace_in(11, \"# It also handles two details that are easy to get wrong by hand: the validation set gets\\n\" \"# phase=\\\"val\\\" (no random channel masking or flips), and only the training loader shuffles.\", \"# It also handles two details that are easy to get wrong by hand: the validation set gets\\n\" \"# phase=\\\"val\\\" (no random channel masking or flips), and only the training loader shuffles.\\n\" \"#\\n\" \"# One thing it does NOT expose is prefetch_factor, and it sets drop_last=True for the\\n\" \"# validation loader too. Neither matters at max_samples: 10, but both do at full scale --\\n\" \"# see experiments/wave_common.py:build_wave_loader() for why and what to do instead.\", \"loader caveats\") replace_in(11, \"# the list you replace when you fork the template.\", \"# the list you replace when you fork the template.\", \"no-op check\") replace_in(11, \" max_number_of_samples=10,\", \" # From the config, not a literal: data.max_samples is the one knob for dataset size,\\n\" \" # so the notebook and 4_finetune_wave_1D.py cannot disagree about how much data ran.\\n\" \" max_number_of_samples=cfg.data.max_samples,\", \"max_number_of_samples from config\") replace_in(11, \"# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\\n\" \"# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\\n\" \"# like written out. Only the flare-specific arguments are passed here, which is exactly\", \"# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\\n\" \"# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\\n\" \"# like written out. Only the wave-specific arguments are passed here, which is exactly\", \"wave-specific wording\") p.write_text(json.dumps(nb, indent=1) + \"\\n\") PY",
            "raw_output": "  ok  [9] cell 9 markdown (replaced)\n  ok  [11] loader caveats\n  ok  [11] no-op check\n  ok  [11] max_number_of_samples from config\n  ok  [11] wave-specific wording",
