              "command": "python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"configs/config_wave_full.yaml\")\ns = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"  # 8 rather than the benchmark default of 4: measured S3 throughput on this machine\n  # saturates around 600 MB/s only once >= 8 fetches are in flight, and with 8 DataLoader\n  # workers each running its own multipart download that is 64 connections in flight.\n  s3_boto3_max_concurrency: 8\n\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"  # 8 parts per frame, measured. What matters more is the number of *frames* in flight,\n  # which is num_workers (each worker fetches serially), and that has a clear optimum:\n  # mimicking the DataLoader's pattern with P processes x 8 parts gave\n  #     P=4  352 MB/s     P=6  269 MB/s     P=8  380 MB/s     P=12  (see below)\n  # Raising parts per frame instead is worse: P=4 x 16 parts gave 294 MB/s. Going wide on\n  # frames is what oversubscribes: 12 workers x batch 4 = 48 samples = 96 frames in flight\n  # collapsed the observed rate to 175 MB/s, while a *separate* process on the same machine\n  # was still getting 424 MB/s at the same moment. So this is a concurrency knee, not a\n  # bandwidth ceiling, and num_workers x batch_size is the thing to keep modest.\n  s3_boto3_max_concurrency: 8\n\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old) == 1\ns = s.replace(old, new)\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"config annotated with the measurement\")\nPY\ngrep -vE \"Warning|warn\" /tmp/bench_pattern.log | tail -3"
            },
            "title": "python - <<'PY' from pathlib import Path p = Path(\"configs/config_wave_full.yaml\") s = p.read_text() old = \"\"\" # 8 rather than the benchmark default of 4: measured S3 throughput on this machine # saturates around 600 MB/s only once >= 8 fetches are in flight, and with 8 DataLoader # workers each running its own multipart download that is 64 connections in flight. s3_boto3_max_concurrency: 8 \"\"\" new = \"\"\" # 8 parts per frame, measured. What matters more is the number of *frames* in flight, # which is num_workers (each worker fetches serially), and that has a clear optimum: # mimicking the DataLoader's pattern with P processes x 8 parts gave # P=4 352 MB/s P=6 269 MB/s P=8 380 MB/s P=12 (see below) # Raising parts per frame instead is worse: P=4 x 16 parts gave 294 MB/s. Going wide on # frames is what oversubscribes: 12 workers x batch 4 = 48 samples = 96 frames in flight # collapsed the observed rate to 175 MB/s, while a *separate* process on the same machine # was still getting 424 MB/s at the same moment. So this is a concurrency knee, not a # bandwidth ceiling, and num_workers x batch_size is the thing to keep modest. s3_boto3_max_concurrency: 8 \"\"\" assert s.count(old) == 1 s = s.replace(old, new) p.write_text(s) print(\"config annotated with the measurement\") PY grep -vE \"Warning|warn\" bench_pattern.log | tail -3",
