          {
            "raw_input": {
              "description": "Save durable environment facts to memory",
              "command": "M=/home/jovyan/.claude/projects/-home-jovyan-surya-workshop2026/memory\ncat > $M/surya-ws-python-interpreter.md <<'MD'\n---\nname: surya-ws-python-interpreter\ndescription: The runnable interpreter for this repo is /home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python, not the default `notebook` conda env\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nRun everything in this repo with `/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python` (Python 3.12,\ntorch 2.9.1+cu128, lightning 2.6.6, peft 0.18.1, wandb 0.23.1, sunpy 7.1.2).\n\nThe shell's default `python` is `/srv/conda/envs/notebook/bin/python` (3.11) and it is\n**missing lightning, peft, wandb, einops, torchmetrics and hdf5plugin** \u2014 so any training or\nnotebook code fails on import there. There is also only one registered Jupyter kernelspec\n(`python3`, pointing at the `notebook` env), so a notebook opened in JupyterLab is not\nnecessarily using the right interpreter either. `environment.yml` in the repo describes an\nenv named `surya_ws`, which is not what exists on this machine.\n\n**Why:** the mismatch is invisible until an import fails several minutes into a run, and the\nrepo gives no hint that the default interpreter is the wrong one.\n\n**How to apply:** set `PY=/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python` and use `$PY -m\n<module.path>` from the repo root. See [[surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow]].\nMD\ncat > $M/surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow.md <<'MD'\n---\nname: surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow\ndescription: /home/jovyan/scratch_space is EFS and writes at ~9 MB/s; never use it as the Surya S3 frame cache\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\n`/home/jovyan/scratch_space` is an EFS mount (`fs-03a4e5d8dfd36f570`, whole filesystem root)\nand is unusable as a data cache: writing a 0.59 GB SDO frame there measured **8.9 MB/s at\n8-way concurrency and 5.9 MB/s at 16-way** \u2014 it gets *worse* with concurrency, because EFS\nbursting mode allots ~50 KB/s per GB stored (~210 MB/s for the 4.2 TB volume) shared with\nevery client mounting it, and other tenants were consuming almost all of it.\n\nFor comparison on the same machine: S3 \u2192 RAM 292 MB/s single / ~400 MB/s aggregate,\nS3 \u2192 local NVMe (`/home/jovyan`, ~77 GB free) 352 MB/s.\n\n**Why:** several committed configs point `data.s3_cache_dir` at that EFS path, which silently\nturns a GPU-bound job into an I/O-bound one 40x slower \u2014 it looks like the model is slow, not\nthe disk.\n\n**How to apply:** cache on `/home/jovyan` (bounded, it is small) or read S3 objects straight\ninto RAM. Concurrency has a sharp knee at 8 whole-frame fetches in flight (380 MB/s); 12\ncollapses to 166 MB/s. Since each DataLoader worker fetches serially, that means\n`num_workers=8` \u2014 do not raise it to use the other CPUs. See [[surya-ws-python-interpreter]].\nMD\ncat > $M/MEMORY.md <<'MD'\n- [Python interpreter for this repo](surya-ws-python-interpreter.md) \u2014 use /home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python; the default env lacks lightning/peft/wandb\n- [EFS scratch is not a usable cache](surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow.md) \u2014 9 MB/s writes; cache on local NVMe or read S3 into RAM, num_workers=8\nMD\nls $M/; echo \"--- phase1 ---\"; tail -1 /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/experiments/results/logs/phase1_extract.log"
            },
            "raw_output": "MEMORY.md\nsurya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow.md\nsurya-ws-python-interpreter.md\n--- phase1 ---\n[train] 146/384 samples  0.21 samples/s  eta 18.6 min",
            "title": "M=/home/jovyan/.claude/projects/-home-jovyan-surya-workshop2026/memory cat > $M/surya-ws-python-interpreter.md <<'MD' --- name: surya-ws-python-interpreter description: The runnable interpreter for this repo is python, not the default `notebook` conda env metadata: type: project --- Run everything in this repo with `/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python` (Python 3.12, torch 2.9.1+cu128, lightning 2.6.6, peft 0.18.1, wandb 0.23.1, sunpy 7.1.2). The shell's default `python` is `/srv/conda/envs/notebook/bin/python` (3.11) and it is **missing lightning, peft, wandb, einops, torchmetrics and hdf5plugin** \u2014 so any training or notebook code fails on import there. There is also only one registered Jupyter kernelspec (`python3`, pointing at the `notebook` env), so a notebook opened in JupyterLab is not necessarily using the right interpreter either. `environment.yml` in the repo describes an env named `surya_ws`, which is not what exists on this machine. **Why:** the mismatch is invisible until an import fails several minutes into a run, and the repo gives no hint that the default interpreter is the wrong one. **How to apply:** set `PY=/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python` and use `$PY -m <module.path>` from the repo root. See [[surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow]]. MD cat > $M/surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow.md <<'MD' --- name: surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow description: scratch_space is EFS and writes at ~9 MB/s; never use it as the Surya S3 frame cache metadata: type: project --- `/home/jovyan/scratch_space` is an EFS mount (`fs-03a4e5d8dfd36f570`, whole filesystem root) and is unusable as a data cache: writing a 0.59 GB SDO frame there measured **8.9 MB/s at 8-way concurrency and 5.9 MB/s at 16-way** \u2014 it gets *worse* with concurrency, because EFS bursting mode allots ~50 KB/s per GB stored (~210 MB/s for the 4.2 TB volume) shared with every client mounting it, and other tenants were consuming almost all of it. For comparison on the same machine: S3 \u2192 RAM 292 MB/s single / ~400 MB/s aggregate, S3 \u2192 local NVMe (`/home/jovyan`, ~77 GB free) 352 MB/s. **Why:** several committed configs point `data.s3_cache_dir` at that EFS path, which silently turns a GPU-bound job into an I/O-bound one 40x slower \u2014 it looks like the model is slow, not the disk. **How to apply:** cache on `/home/jovyan` (bounded, it is small) or read S3 objects straight into RAM. Concurrency has a sharp knee at 8 whole-frame fetches in flight (380 MB/s); 12 collapses to 166 MB/s. Since each DataLoader worker fetches serially, that means `num_workers=8` \u2014 do not raise it to use the other CPUs. See [[surya-ws-python-interpreter]]. MD cat > $M/MEMORY.md <<'MD' - [Python interpreter for this repo](surya-ws-python-interpreter.md) \u2014 use python; the default env lacks lightning/peft/wandb - [EFS scratch is not a usable cache](surya-ws-efs-cache-is-slow.md) \u2014 9 MB/s writes; cache on local NVMe or read S3 into RAM, num_workers=8 MD ls $M/; echo \"--- phase1 ---\"; tail -1 phase1_extract.log",
