    {
      "time": 1789662202.0142136,
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "Everything is built and pre-validated. Here's where the work stands while Phase 1 finishes.\n\n## Done and verified\n\n**Phase 0 \u2014 all four confirmed bugs fixed, with the old values kept as commented-out lines:**\n\n| bug | fix | verified |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pretrained tokenizer silently random | `time_delta_input_minutes: [-12, 0]`, `time_dim: 2` | **157/159** tensors load (was 156/159); `ts` is `(B,13,2,4096,4096)` end-to-end |\n| LoRA never adapted attention | `target_modules: [fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]` | 36 modules adapted, **16 in attention**; 1,518,081 trainable |\n| Redundant 1.64 M-param head layer | `penultimate_linear_layer: false` | head = 2,561 params; 3,157,761 with it on |\n| Loose match tolerance | `ds_time_tolerance: 15min` | train 606/604, val 25, test 50 |\n\nRestricting to *complete event pairs* then gives exact balance everywhere: **train 596 pairs (1192), val 12 pairs (24), test 24 pairs (48)**, with subsets provably nested across N. Notebook 2's remaining items are all fixed (no `Flare`/`RRSE`/`MSE`/hardcoded-10 references remain; `wandb.finish()` cell added; `gradient_clip_val=1.0`).\n\n**Task 1.1 is complete** \u2014 and it answers its question: the wave **is** visible in the 128\u00d7128 map the baseline actually receives, with contrast ratios of **4.4\u00d7, 8.1\u00d7 and 2.3\u00d7** against each event's own matched control an hour earlier.\n\n## One measurement overturned the plan's resource model\n\nThe configured `s3_cache_dir` is on EFS, which writes at **8.9 MB/s** (and *worse* at higher concurrency \u2014 it's a shared filesystem throughput cap, not a client limit). S3\u2192RAM is **400 MB/s**. So I added an in-memory read path (`datasets/wave_dataset_memory.py`) that never touches disk, and pinned only the validation split \u2014 the one split re-read every epoch \u2014 to local NVMe. Val extraction went from 3.5 min to **0.8 min**.\n\nConcurrency then has a sharp knee, and it's `num_workers` (each worker fetches serially): **8 frames \u2192 380 MB/s, 12 \u2192 166 MB/s**. At 8 workers the data path costs 2.97 s/sample against 3.12 s/sample of GPU \u2014 so the GPU is the limiter and the plan's 11 h budget holds. GPU numbers confirmed directly: B=2 \u2192 3.12 s/sample, 37.2 GiB of 44.4 GiB; B=3 OOMs with fp32 inputs.\n\n## Bugs the smoke test caught before the unattended stretch\n\n- `Trainer(max_time=\"01:50:00\")` is rejected \u2014 Lightning demands `DD:HH:MM:SS`. Every cap in the schedule would have failed one run at a time. Now normalized in-script.\n- `Trainer.log_dir` returns *wandb's* directory when wandb is enabled, so the training-curve figure would have silently come out empty.\n- One hung S3 fetch blocked all 8 workers for 10 minutes \u2014 `make_s3_client` sets no socket timeouts. Added explicit timeouts plus a watchdog.\n\nwandb auth is verified working, and the schedule now carries the LR probe's pick into the scaling phase automatically, so it runs as one unattended command.\n\nTwo things I should flag: **validation is 24 samples**, so one sample is 4.2% and BCE differences below ~0.05 aren't resolvable \u2014 the 48-sample test evaluation is the more trustworthy comparison. And task 1.1 shows the bright running-difference signal is dominated by the **eruption source region**, not the propagating front, so a classifier may be learning \"is there a bright compact eruption\" rather than \"is there a wave\" \u2014 worth stating in the writeup regardless of how the numbers land.\n\nPhase 1's train split has ~12 minutes left; I'll run the visibility scan and baseline study as soon as it lands, then launch the schedule.",
      "id": "11906ceb-873d-439e-a92d-7589b4c38d3a",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
