            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_011JRKH1f2dqJpmUkrSGE8Gm",
            "raw_input": {
              "command": "python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"experiments/wave_visibility_scan.py\")\ns = p.read_text()\nold = '''# Solar radius as a fraction of the frame width. AIA level-1 is ~0.6\"/px and R_sun is ~960\",\n# so the disk radius is ~1600 px in a 4096-pixel frame \u2014 about 0.39 of the width, which\n# matches what the task 1.1 figures show. 0.38 stays just inside the limb: including off-limb\n# pixels would dilute the high quantile the wave front shows up in, and the corners contribute\n# nothing but read noise.\nDISK_FRACTION = 0.38'''\nnew = '''# Analysis radius as a fraction of the frame width. Measured on a real AIA193 frame rather\n# than taken from the plate scale: the radial median brightness rises to a peak of 2.49x the\n# disk median at r = 1600 px (frac 0.391) \u2014 that is limb brightening \u2014 and falls away beyond\n# it, so the limb sits at frac ~0.39.\n#\n# 0.36 stops short of it on purpose. A static limb cancels in a running difference, but the\n# brightness gradient there is so steep that sub-pixel pointing jitter leaves a bright ring in\n# the difference (visible in the task 1.1 figures), and that ring would dominate the 99.9th\n# percentile this script uses to track the wave front. Excluding it keeps 85% of the disk area,\n# which is far more than a wave detection needs.\nDISK_FRACTION = 0.36'''\nassert s.count(old) == 1\np.write_text(s.replace(old, new))\nprint(\"disk fraction set from the measurement\")\nPY\n/home/jovyan/envs/surya_WS/bin/python -m py_compile experiments/wave_visibility_scan.py && echo ok; date; tail -1 /home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/experiments/results/logs/phase1_extract.log",
