      "type": "msg",
      "body": "Everything checks out. Here's the state.\n\n## Status after the restart\n\nIt wasn't the machine \u2014 the **JupyterHub singleuser server restarted ~4 minutes ago** (host uptime is 21 h, but the server process is 4 min old). Everything it had spawned died with it. GPU: **0 MiB used, 0% util, no processes**.\n\n**Did the baseline train? Yes \u2014 the whole baseline arm is done and on disk.** What died was the GPU schedule: the LR probe at 3e-5 got as far as printing its model summary and was killed before a single training step. No probe summary JSON exists, so Phases 4\u20135 (all Surya runs) are entirely unstarted.\n\nPre-flight assertions did fire correctly in that probe log before it died, which is worth noting: **36 LoRA modules adapted, 16 of them in attention**, trainable **1,518,081**, and `batch['ts']` shape **(1, 13, 2, 4096, 4096)** \u2014 so bugs 1, 2 and 6 are genuinely fixed in the running code, not just in the YAML.\n\nDataset as built: train **384** (192 events), val **24** (12 events), test **48** (24 events), `ds_time_tolerance: 15min`, seed 42 \u2014 both open items resolved to the defaults.\n\n## Task 1 plots\n\n**1.1 \u2014 are wave features visible after the kernel?** (shown above, `results/task_1_1_wave_visibility.png`)\n\nYes, in 2 of the 3 matched pairs. Events 2010-06-12T0030 and 2010-08-07T1755 show a compact bipolar signature in the 128\u00b2 pooled map \u2014 the right column, which is *all* the baseline ever sees \u2014 that the same event's no-wave frame an hour earlier does not. The 2010-12-31T0418 pair is the counterexample: its pooled maps carry comparable structure in both classes, and note its colour scale is 4\u00d7 tighter (\u00b10.11 vs \u00b10.4), so this is a genuinely weak event, not a plotting artifact. The signal survives 32\u00d7 mean pooling but is far from a clean separator per-event.\n\n**1.1 extended \u2014 the visibility scan** (`results/wave_visibility_scan.png`)\n\nThis **contradicts the worry that drove the plan's ordering**. Signal is weakest early and strongest late: median pooled SNR runs +12 \u2192 1.49, +24 \u2192 1.89, **+36 \u2192 3.11 (peak)**, +48 \u2192 2.07. The catalog's +30 min sits at **2.50**, on the rising flank, 20% below peak. So +30 min is an acceptable sampling point and **no re-timed catalog is needed** \u2014 the expensive remedy I'd budgeted for is unnecessary. Sample count remains the binding constraint.\n\n**1.2 \u2014 why train loss falls while val loss rises** (`results/baseline_study.png`)\n\nReproduced on the true earliest 10 samples: train 0.359 \u2192 **0.0066** with accuracy 1.0 from epoch 1, val 0.771 \u2192 **0.858**, and val accuracy pinned at exactly 0.5 the whole time. 16,385 parameters on 10 samples at lr=0.01 \u2014 memorization.\n\nOne correction to what I reported before the restart: the script's strict `val_auroc_frozen` flag is **False**, not met. AUROC isn't frozen from epoch 0 as in `version_3`; it climbs 0.333 \u2192 0.444 over epochs 0\u20134, *then* holds at 0.444 for the remaining 11 epochs. Every value is **below chance**. So the train\u2193/val\u2191 signature reproduces cleanly, but the specific \"ranking never changed at all\" tell only holds over the tail \u2014 `signature_matches_version_3: False`.\n\n**And the answer the baseline gives to \"does val loss improve with more images\":**\n\n| N | best val BCE (median) | seed spread | val AUROC |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | 0.605 | 0.554\u20130.705 | 0.681 |\n| 16 | 0.696 | 0.484\u20130.765 | 0.500 |\n| 32 | 0.628 | 0.570\u20130.765 | 0.722 |\n| 64 | 0.641 | 0.573\u20130.766 | 0.757 |\n| 128 | 0.592 | 0.576\u20130.651 | 0.729 |\n| 256 | 0.578 | 0.563\u20130.595 | 0.792 |\n| 384 | **0.561** | 0.559\u20130.564 | **0.813** |\n\nBelow N\u2248128 it's pure seed noise (a 0.28-wide spread straddling chance at N=16). From 128 up it decreases monotonically and the spread collapses to \u00b10.003. Chance is 0.693, so the N=384 baseline is real signal. Weight decay does nothing below 1e-3; pool kernel 16 is marginally best (0.547).\n\n## What's next\n\nRelaunch the GPU schedule \u2014 that's the only thing outstanding. ~11.5 h of work against 18 h available from 18:00. One change I want to make: launch it with `setsid nohup` so it's detached from the Jupyter server's process tree, since that's exactly what killed it.\n\nLet me record the status in the plan and get your go-ahead to resume.",
