                "text": "# API Reference: wave-classification code under `/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/`\n\n---\n\n## 1. `models/simple_baseline.py` (197 lines, git status `M`)\n\nImports: `torch`, `torch.nn as nn`, `from einops import rearrange`, `torch.nn.functional as F`.\n\n### Public API \u2014 4 top-level objects (2 pre-existing, 3 new since HEAD)\n\n| Object | Kind | Signature |\n|---|---|---|\n| `destandardize_channels` | function | `(batch: dict, channel_order: list, scalers: dict) -> dict` |\n| `RegressionFlareModel` | `nn.Module` | `__init__(self, input_dim: int)` |\n| `ConstantProbabilityModel` | `nn.Module` | `__init__(self)` \u2014 **NEW** |\n| `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference` | function | `(ts_signum_log: torch.Tensor, channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int) -> torch.Tensor` \u2014 **NEW** |\n| `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` | `nn.Module` | `__init__(self, channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int = 32)` \u2014 **NEW** |\n\n**IMPORTANT \u2014 the NEW functionality you asked about beyond the three items you named is `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference()`.** It is a module-level free function (not a method), and it is the only place the scalar feature is defined; `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel.forward()` merely calls it. There is no other new symbol in the file.\n\n#### `destandardize_channels(batch, channel_order, scalers) -> dict`\nSummary: returns a **new** batch dict with `'ts'` moved from normalized space to **signum-log** space. Undoes the per-channel z-score ONLY; log compression is deliberately retained, so the result is `sign(x*s) * log1p(|x*s|)`, not raw DN/Gauss. For true physical units use `HelioNetCDFDataset.inverse_transform_data()`.\nReturns: `{**batch, \"ts\": x}` with `x = batch[\"ts\"].clone()`, each channel passed through `scalers[channel].inverse_transform` under `torch.no_grad()`.\n\n#### `RegressionFlareModel(input_dim: int)`\n`self.linear = nn.Linear(input_dim, 1)`. `forward(self, x: dict) -> torch.Tensor`:\n```python\nx = x[\"ts\"]\nx = x.abs().mean(dim=[3, 4])\nx = rearrange(x, \"b c t -> b (c t)\")\nreturn self.linear(x)\n```\nExpects `'ts'` already in signum-log space. Returns `(B, 1)`.\n\n#### `ConstantProbabilityModel()` \u2014 NEW\nDocstring: \"Trainable baseline: a single learned logit, independent of the input. Learns the class base rate (the wave / no-wave prior) and nothing else.\"\n`__init__`: `self.logit = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(1))`. No arguments at all.\n**Full `forward` body:**\n```python\ndef forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    Args:\n        batch (dict): Batch dict; only used to read the batch size from\n            ``batch[\"forecast\"]`` (the target), since the model itself ignores\n            the input entirely.\n    \"\"\"\n    batch_size = batch[\"forecast\"].shape[0]\n    return self.logit.expand(batch_size, 1)\n```\nReturns `(B, 1)` logits. Note it reads `batch[\"forecast\"]`, **not** `batch[\"ts\"]` \u2014 so it works even if `ts` is absent, but requires the target key to be present in the batch handed to the model.\n\n#### `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference(ts_signum_log, channel_index, pool_kernel)` \u2014 NEW\nNo defaults \u2014 all three args positional/required (`pool_kernel` has **no** default here, unlike the model's `pool_kernel: int = 32`).\nSummary: collapses a running-difference image into one scalar per sample: `mean(|pool_K(now - prev)|)`. Pool first, then `abs`, then spatial mean. The docstring is explicit that the order is **not** interchangeable: mean pooling composes (kernel-8 map re-pooled by 4 == kernel-32 of the original), but `abs()` does not, so any code deriving the scalar from a cached map must re-pool to `pool_kernel` first, then `abs`, then mean.\n**Full body:**\n```python\nx = ts_signum_log[:, channel_index]                                   # (B, T, H, W)\ndiff = x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]                                             # (B, H, W)\npooled = F.avg_pool2d(diff.unsqueeze(1), kernel_size=pool_kernel)     # (B, 1, S, S)\nreturn pooled.abs().mean(dim=(-2, -1))                                # (B, 1)\n```\nReturns `(B, 1)` \u2014 shaped ready for `nn.Linear(1, 1)`.\n\n#### `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int = 32)` \u2014 NEW\nClass docstring: logistic regression on ONE scalar per sample \u2014 `mean(|32x32 mean pool of AIA193(now) - AIA193(prev)|)` in signum-log space, `nn.Linear(1, 1)` -> logit, `BCEWithLogitsLoss` supplies the sigmoid. **Two parameters total.** It records that the previous version fed the whole 128x128 pooled map into `nn.Linear(16384, 1)` and memorized its 384 training samples (train loss -> 0.0065, val AUROC frozen at 0.4). Because the readout is monotone in a single feature it is a threshold detector: AUROC is a fixed property of the feature, only loss/calibration are learned.\nNotes in the docstring worth acting on:\n- `'ts'` must be `(B, C, T=2, H, W)` with T ordered `[t_prev, t_now]` (`HelioNetCDFDataset._get_index_data` always puts offset-0 last), so `x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]` is forward in time.\n- `'ts'` must be in signum-log space \u2014 use `destandardize_channels()` via `WaveLightningModule(preprocess_fn=...)`.\n- `channel_index` **differs per config**: 3 in the 13-channel configs, 0 when `data.channels` is narrowed to `[\"aia193\"]`. Pass `cfg.data.channels.index(\"aia193\")`, not a literal.\n- The feature is order 0.1 while the logit needs weight order 10\u2013100, so `learning_rate: 0.01` is too small by a couple of orders of magnitude. Affects loss/threshold only; AUROC unchanged.\n\n`__init__` body: stores `self.channel_index`, `self.pool_kernel`, and `self.linear = nn.Linear(1, 1)`.\n**Full `forward` body:**\n```python\ndef forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    Args:\n        batch (dict): Batch dict with 'ts' of shape (B, C, T=2, H, W) in signum-log space.\n\n    Returns:\n        ``(B, 1)`` logits.\n    \"\"\"\n    feature = mean_abs_pooled_running_difference(\n        batch[\"ts\"], self.channel_index, self.pool_kernel\n    )\n    return self.linear(feature)\n```\n\n### Git diff vs committed version (`009017b..47d2bb0`)\nPurely **additive**, plus one import. Nothing was removed or altered:\n1. `+import torch.nn.functional as F` (line 8).\n2. The committed file ended at `return self.linear(x)` **with no trailing newline**; the diff shows that line replaced by an identical line plus newline, then ~125 new lines appended (the current file also ends without a trailing newline).\n3. Appended, in order: `ConstantProbabilityModel`, `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference`, `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` \u2014 exactly as quoted above.\n\nStyle nits visible in the diff (not bugs): only one blank line before `def mean_abs_pooled_running_difference` and before `class RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` (PEP8 wants two), and the signature's closing paren is indented 4 spaces.\n\nOther files modified but not in this diff: `lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py`, `metrics/template_metrics.py`, `configs.py`, `configs/config_script.yaml`, `3_finetune_template_1D.py`, two notebooks. Untracked (never committed): `datasets/wave_dataset.py`, `datasets/wave_dataset_memory.py`, the whole `experiments/` dir, `4_finetune_wave_1D.py`, `configs/config_wave_full.yaml`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `experiments/wave_common.py` (641 lines, untracked)\n\nModule-level constants: `EVENT_COL = \"start_time\"`, `POSITIVE_CLASS = \"wave\"`, `FRAME_GB = 0.59`, `RESERVE_GB = 15.0`.\n\nModule docstring states the three reasons it exists: event-level stratified subsetting (the catalog stores each event twice \u2014 `wave` at `start+30min`, `no wave` at `start-30min`, so an event is a matched pair; `waveDSDataset.max_number_of_samples` head-slices by `ds_index` and so returns the *earliest* pairs, a chronological bias); pre-flight assertions; and the single definition of the running-difference feature.\n\n### Complete function/class inventory\n\n| Symbol | Signature | One line |\n|---|---|---|\n| `wave_label_transform` | `(class_series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series` | Maps catalog `class` column to float32 1.0/0.0 via `== \"wave\"`; passed as `waveDSDataset(label_transform=...)`. |\n| `SubsetInfo` | `@dataclass(n_samples, n_events, n_positive, n_negative, n_dropped_unpaired, n_events_available)` \u2014 all `int` | **The dataset-statistics reporter**: sample count, event/pair count, class balance, dropped-unpaired count. |\n| `SubsetInfo.describe` | `(self) -> str` | Formats those six numbers into one log line (see below). |\n| `event_stratified_subset` | `(dataset, n_samples: int \\| None = None, seed: int = 42) -> SubsetInfo` | **The subsetter** \u2014 restricts a dataset **in place** to a seeded, event-level, exactly-balanced subset. |\n| `dataset_frame_uris` | `(dataset) -> list[str]` | Distinct NetCDF paths the dataset will read, chronological. |\n| `cache_coverage` | `(dataset) -> tuple[int, int]` | `(n_cached, n_total)` frames, using the dataset's own cache-path scheme. |\n| `plan_pinned_frames` | `(val_dataset, train_dataset, budget_gb: float, train_priority_n: int \\| None = None, subset_seed: int = 42) -> tuple[set[str], dict]` | Chooses which S3 frames to pin on fast local disk under a GB budget; val first, then the nested train prefix. |\n| `pin_frames` | `(dataset, extra_datasets=(), uris: set[str] \\| None = None) -> int` | Sets `cache_paths` on one or more datasets; returns whitelist size (0 if no local cache). |\n| `count_matched_pretrained` | `(model: torch.nn.Module, checkpoint_path: str) -> tuple[int, int]` | Counts bit-identical checkpoint tensors present in the model after loading (tries `key` and `backbone.key`). |\n| `assert_tokenizer_loaded` | `(model: torch.nn.Module, checkpoint_path: str) -> int` | **Assertion helper** \u2014 raises unless `matched >= total - 2`; guards the silent `time_dim`/tokenizer shape mismatch. |\n| `assert_lora_adapted_attention` | `(model: torch.nn.Module) -> list[str]` | **Assertion helper** \u2014 raises unless LoRA reached `attn.qkv` and `attn.proj`; returns adapted module names. |\n| `assert_trainable_count` | `(model: torch.nn.Module, expected: int \\| None = None) -> int` | **Assertion helper** \u2014 prints trainable/total; asserts equality when `expected` given. |\n| `assert_batch_shape` | `(batch: dict, n_channels: int, n_timesteps: int, img_size: int) -> None` | **Assertion helper** \u2014 asserts `batch[\"ts\"].shape[1:] == (C, T, S, S)`. |\n| `pooled_running_difference` | `(ts_signum_log: torch.Tensor, channel_index: int, pool_kernel: int) -> torch.Tensor` | Running difference, mean-pooled, **no abs** \u2014 returns the `(B, H//K, W//K)` map. |\n| `repool` | `(features: np.ndarray, factor: int) -> np.ndarray` | Mean-pool cached `(N, S, S)` maps by a further integer factor. |\n| `build_wave_datasets` | `(cfg, scalers, include_test: bool = False, in_memory: bool = True, local_cache_budget_gb: float = 40.0)` | **The dataset builder** \u2014 train/val(/test) `waveDSDataset`s. |\n| `build_wave_loader` | `(dataset, batch_size: int, num_workers: int, prefetch_factor: int \\| None, seed: int, shuffle: bool, drop_last: bool)` | **The dataloader builder** \u2014 all args required, no defaults. |\n\n`SubsetInfo.describe()` returns exactly:\n```python\nreturn (\n    f\"{self.n_samples} samples / {self.n_events} events \"\n    f\"({self.n_positive} wave, {self.n_negative} no wave) \"\n    f\"from {self.n_events_available} complete pairs available; \"\n    f\"dropped {self.n_dropped_unpaired} unpaired sample(s)\"\n)\n```\nCallers print it as `[DATA] {name}: {info.describe()}` \u2014 see `4_finetune_wave_1D.py:284`, `experiments/extract_baseline_features.py:226`, `experiments/wave_visibility_scan.py:182`.\n\n**Note the two feature functions are NOT interchangeable.** `wave_common.pooled_running_difference()` returns the pooled **map** with no `abs()`; `models.simple_baseline.mean_abs_pooled_running_difference()` returns the `(B, 1)` **scalar** with `abs()` before the spatial mean. The module docstring claims `pooled_running_difference()` is \"the single definition\" both the model and the extractor use, and that `assert_feature_matches_model()` checks they agree \u2014 but `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` actually calls its own local function, and **there is no `assert_feature_matches_model` in this file** (grep finds no such definition). The docstring is stale relative to the current `simple_baseline.py`.\n\n### Full body: `event_stratified_subset` (lines 76\u2013163)\n\nDocstring: keeps only events whose *both* samples survived the Surya-index match, so the result is exactly 50/50 and accuracy 0.5 is exactly chance (on train at `ds_time_tolerance: 15min` that costs 18 of 1210 matched samples). `seed` is fixed across a scaling study so N=48 \u2282 N=96 \u2282 N=192. Raises `ValueError` if `n_samples` is odd or exceeds available paired samples.\n\n```python\n    df = dataset.df_valid_indices\n    if EVENT_COL not in df.columns:\n        raise ValueError(\n            f\"The wave catalog must carry a {EVENT_COL!r} column to subset by event; \"\n            f\"got columns {sorted(df.columns)[:8]}\u2026\"\n        )\n\n    # Positional order of df_valid_indices matches dataset.valid_indices, and\n    # waveDSDataset.__getitem__ indexes both by position. Any mask must be applied to\n    # both, in the same order.\n    labels = df[\"label\"].to_numpy()\n    events = df[EVENT_COL].to_numpy()\n\n    per_event = pd.Series(labels).groupby(events).nunique()\n    complete = np.sort(per_event.index[per_event == 2].to_numpy())\n    n_dropped = int(len(labels) - np.isin(events, complete).sum())\n\n    if n_samples is None:\n        chosen_events = complete\n    else:\n        if n_samples % 2 != 0:\n            raise ValueError(\n                f\"n_samples must be even: events contribute one 'wave' and one 'no wave' \"\n                f\"sample each, so an odd count cannot be class-balanced (got {n_samples}).\"\n            )\n        n_events = n_samples // 2\n        if n_events > len(complete):\n            raise ValueError(\n                f\"Requested {n_samples} samples ({n_events} events) but only \"\n                f\"{len(complete)} complete event pairs ({2 * len(complete)} samples) \"\n                f\"survived the Surya-index match. Loosen data.ds_time_tolerance or \"\n                f\"lower --train-n.\"\n            )\n        # Permute the *sorted* event list so the choice depends only on the seed, not on\n        # dict/CSV ordering. Taking a prefix makes the subsets nested across N, which is\n        # what lets the learning curve attribute a change to sample count alone.\n        order = np.random.default_rng(seed).permutation(len(complete))\n        chosen_events = complete[np.sort(order[:n_events])]\n\n    keep = np.flatnonzero(np.isin(events, chosen_events))\n\n    dataset.df_valid_indices = df.iloc[keep]\n    dataset.valid_indices = [dataset.valid_indices[i] for i in keep]\n    dataset.adjusted_length = len(keep)\n\n    kept_labels = dataset.df_valid_indices[\"label\"].to_numpy()\n    info = SubsetInfo(\n        n_samples=len(keep),\n        n_events=len(chosen_events),\n        n_positive=int((kept_labels == 1).sum()),\n        n_negative=int((kept_labels == 0).sum()),\n        n_dropped_unpaired=n_dropped,\n        n_events_available=len(complete),\n    )\n    if info.n_positive != info.n_negative:\n        raise AssertionError(\n            f\"Event-level subsetting should be exactly balanced but produced \"\n            f\"{info.n_positive} wave / {info.n_negative} no wave. This means an event \"\n            f\"contributed two samples of the same class.\"\n        )\n    return info\n```\nMutates three attributes on the dataset: `df_valid_indices`, `valid_indices`, `adjusted_length`.\n\n### Full body: `build_wave_datasets` (lines 509\u2013585)\n\nDocstring: thin wrapper over `build_helio_datasets()` adding the wave-specific kwargs, the **test** split, and the choice of read path. `in_memory=True` (default) uses `waveDSDatasetInMemory` (400 MB/s vs 9 MB/s, because the configured cache dir is a throughput-capped EFS mount). `build_helio_datasets()` returns exactly two datasets, so the test split is obtained by calling it a second time with `valid_data_path` pointed at the test index and keeping the second dataset \u2014 `phase=\"val\"` is the right phase for a held-out split (no channel masking, no flips). Returns `(train, val)` or `(train, val, test)`.\n\n```python\n    from workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_datasets\n\n    if in_memory:\n        from downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset_memory import (\n            waveDSDatasetInMemory as dataset_cls,\n        )\n        read_kwargs = dict(\n            # Populated after subsetting by pin_frames(): the whitelist has to be the\n            # frames the *subset* reads, which is not known until the subset exists.\n            cache_paths=None,\n            local_cache_dir=cfg.data.s3_cache_dir,\n            local_cache_budget_gb=local_cache_budget_gb,\n        )\n    else:\n        from downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset as dataset_cls\n        read_kwargs = {}\n\n    task_kwargs = dict(\n        **read_kwargs,\n        return_surya_stack=True,\n        # None on purpose: max_number_of_samples head-slices chronologically. Subsetting\n        # is event_stratified_subset()'s job, after the match has happened.\n        max_number_of_samples=None,\n        label_transform=wave_label_transform,\n        ds_wave_index_path=cfg.data.wave_index_path,\n        ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n        ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column,\n        ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n        ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n    )\n\n    train, val = build_helio_datasets(cfg, dataset_cls, scalers=scalers, **task_kwargs)\n    if not include_test:\n        return train, val\n\n    test_index = str(cfg.data.train_data_path).replace(\"_train.csv\", \"_test.csv\")\n    if not os.path.isfile(test_index):\n        raise FileNotFoundError(\n            f\"Test index not found at {test_index}. It is derived from \"\n            f\"data.train_data_path by swapping _train.csv for _test.csv.\"\n        )\n    original = cfg.data.valid_data_path\n    try:\n        cfg.data.valid_data_path = test_index\n        _, test = build_helio_datasets(cfg, dataset_cls, scalers=scalers, **task_kwargs)\n    finally:\n        cfg.data.valid_data_path = original\n    return train, val, test\n```\n\n### Full body: `build_wave_loader` (lines 588\u2013641)\n\nDocstring: exists because `build_helio_dataloaders()` hard-codes `drop_last=True` for **both** loaders and does not expose `prefetch_factor`. `drop_last=True` on an odd-sized val split silently discards a sample (1 of 27 = 3.7% of the checkpoint-selecting metric). `prefetch_factor` is the only bound on worker memory: `ts` is `(B, 13, 2, 4096, 4096)` fp32 = 1.74 GB/sample, so worst case is `num_workers * prefetch_factor * batch_size * 1.74 GB` \u2014 27.8 GB at `8 x 1 x 2`, 55.7 GB at PyTorch's default factor 2. Everything else (spawn \u2014 the dataset holds a boto3 handle that does not survive fork \u2014 persistent workers, explicit generator, per-worker seeding via the deliberately-imported private `_seed_worker`) matches `build_helio_dataloaders()`.\n\n```python\n    from functools import partial\n\n    from torch.utils.data import DataLoader\n    from workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import _seed_worker\n\n    kwargs = dict(\n        batch_size=batch_size,\n        num_workers=num_workers,\n        pin_memory=True,\n        drop_last=drop_last,\n    )\n    if num_workers > 0:\n        kwargs[\"multiprocessing_context\"] = \"spawn\"\n        kwargs[\"persistent_workers\"] = True\n        kwargs[\"worker_init_fn\"] = partial(_seed_worker, base_seed=seed)\n        if prefetch_factor is not None:\n            kwargs[\"prefetch_factor\"] = prefetch_factor\n\n    if shuffle:\n        generator = torch.Generator()\n        generator.manual_seed(seed)\n        kwargs[\"generator\"] = generator\n\n    return DataLoader(dataset, shuffle=shuffle, **kwargs)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. `datasets/wave_dataset.py` vs `datasets/wave_dataset_memory.py`\n\n### `waveDSDataset(HelioNetCDFDataset)` \u2014 `/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/datasets/wave_dataset.py`\n```python\ndef __init__(\n    self,\n    return_surya_stack: bool = True,\n    max_number_of_samples: int | None = None,\n    label_transform: Callable[[pd.Series], pd.Series] | None = None,\n    ds_wave_index_path: str | None = None,\n    ds_time_column: str | None = None,\n    ds_class_column: str | None = None,\n    ds_time_tolerance: str | None = None,\n    ds_match_direction: Literal[\"forward\", \"backward\", \"nearest\"] = \"forward\",\n    **kwargs,   # all HelioNetCDFDataset params: index_path, scalers, channels, s3_*, ...\n):\n```\nSets `kwargs.setdefault(\"load_forecast_frames\", False)` before `super().__init__()`. Merges the wave CSV onto the Surya `valid_indices` with `pd.merge_asof(direction=ds_match_direction)`, dedupes on `ds_index` keeping the smallest `index_delta`, filters by `ds_time_tolerance` (raises `ValueError(\"No intersection between Surya and DS indices\")` if empty), then overwrites `self.valid_indices`, `self.adjusted_length` and sets `df_valid_indices.index = valid_indices`. `max_number_of_samples` head-slices (this is the chronological bias `event_stratified_subset` exists to avoid).\nOther methods: `__len__(self)` -> `self.adjusted_length`; `__getitem__(self, idx: int) -> dict` returns `forecast` (np.float32 label) and `ds_index` (ISO string), plus all `HelioNetCDFDataset.__getitem__` keys when `return_surya_stack=True`.\nRead path: **inherited** \u2014 the stock `HelioNetCDFDataset` `s3_mode` behaviour (`download`/`simplecache` write the 0.59 GB object into `s3_cache_dir` on disk before opening; `stream` does ranged GETs).\n\n### `waveDSDatasetInMemory(waveDSDataset)` \u2014 `/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/datasets/wave_dataset_memory.py`\n```python\ndef __init__(\n    self,\n    cache_paths: set[str] | None = None,\n    local_cache_dir: str | None = None,\n    local_cache_budget_gb: float = 60.0,\n    s3_boto3_pool_size: int = 64,\n    connect_timeout_s: float = 10.0,\n    read_timeout_s: float = 45.0,\n    fetch_timeout_s: float = 180.0,\n    fetch_attempts: int = 4,\n    **kwargs,   # forwarded to waveDSDataset -> HelioNetCDFDataset\n):\n```\nNote `build_wave_datasets` passes `local_cache_budget_gb=40.0` by default, overriding the class default of 60.0.\n\n**Which reads through RAM vs disk:** `waveDSDatasetInMemory` reads **through RAM** \u2014 it overrides `load_nc_data()` to fetch the whole S3 object into an `io.BytesIO` and hand the buffer to `h5netcdf`, never touching disk (except for whitelisted frames, below). `waveDSDataset` uses the inherited **disk-cache** path (`s3_cache_dir`). The module docstring's measurements: S3->memory 292 MB/s, S3->local NVMe 352 MB/s, S3->EFS scratch (the configured cache) 8.9 MB/s at concurrency 8 and 5.9 MB/s at 16 \u2014 raising concurrency made it worse, the signature of a contended filesystem. It is a read-path change only: bytes decoded, channels extracted and normalization are identical, so results are numerically the same as `s3_mode: download`.\n\nDifferences beyond the constructor:\n- Overrides `load_nc_data(self, filepath: str, timestep, channels: list[str]) -> np.ndarray`; falls back to `super()` for non-`s3://` paths. Returns `(C, H, W)`.\n- Hybrid caching: `cache_paths` is a whitelist of S3 URIs that *are* written to `local_cache_dir` (intended for the validation split, re-read every epoch \u2014 48 frames / 28 GB). Everything else is fetched to RAM and discarded, so the train split cannot evict the pinned val frames. `None` disables disk caching entirely. `local_cache_budget_gb` is a ceiling, not a target \u2014 over budget it silently streams instead.\n- `__getstate__` / `__setstate__` drop `_client` and `_client_lock` so the dataset survives the spawn pickle; both are rebuilt lazily per worker.\n- Private helpers: `_get_client()` (deliberately does **not** use `make_s3_client()`, which exposes no socket timeouts; builds `boto3.client(\"s3\", ...)` with explicit connect/read timeouts and `retries={\"max_attempts\": 4, \"mode\": \"adaptive\"}`), `_reset_client()`, `_transfer_config()`, `_local_cache_path(s3_uri)`, `_cache_dir_bytes()`, `_fetch_to_memory(s3_uri) -> io.BytesIO` (per-attempt wall-clock watchdog around boto3, abandons rather than cancels a stalled transfer), `_maybe_write_cache(s3_uri, buf)` (writes to `.{pid}.{uuid}.partial` then `os.replace`, so a losing racer never publishes a truncated file).\n- Consequence documented in the docstring: **nothing is cached by default, so every epoch re-reads from S3** \u2014 ~3.9 s of network vs ~3.1 s of GPU per sample.\n\n---\n\n## 4. `metrics/template_metrics.py` \u2014 `WaveMetrics`\n\nConstructor: `WaveMetrics(mode: str)`. Stores `self.mode = mode` and caches `self._accuracy = tm.classification.BinaryAccuracy()`, `self._auroc = tm.classification.BinaryAUROC()` once rather than per call. No validation of `mode` at construction \u2014 an invalid mode raises only on first `__call__`.\n\nValid mode strings (matched case-insensitively via `self.mode.lower()`), exactly four:\n`\"train_loss\"`, `\"val_loss\"`, `\"train_metrics\"`, `\"val_metrics\"`. Anything else raises `NotImplementedError(f\"{self.mode} is not implemented as a valid metric case.\")`.\n\nEvery method returns `tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]` \u2014 `(metric_dict, weight_list)`:\n\n| mode | dict keys returned | weights | computation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `train_loss` | `{\"bce\"}` | `[1]` | `binary_cross_entropy_with_logits(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))` |\n| `val_loss` | `{\"bce\"}` | `[1]` | delegates to `train_loss` (the override hook for a different validation objective) |\n| `train_metrics` | `{\"accuracy\"}` | `[1]` | `BinaryAccuracy(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1).long())`, under `torch.no_grad()` |\n| `val_metrics` | `{\"accuracy\", \"auroc\"}` | `[1, 1]` | accuracy then `BinaryAUROC`, both under `torch.no_grad()` |\n\n`__call__(self, preds, target)` dispatches with a `match` statement; `train_loss`/`val_loss` are deliberately **not** wrapped in `no_grad` (Lightning already disables grads in validation). Methods are also callable directly: `train_loss`, `val_loss`, `train_metrics`, `val_metrics`, all `(self, preds: torch.Tensor, target: torch.Tensor)`. Private helper `_ensure_device(self, preds) -> None` moves the two torchmetrics modules onto `preds.device`.\n\nShape contract (from the module comment): preds arrive `(B,)` from `HelioSpectformer1D` or `(B, 1)` from the linear baseline, targets always `(B, 1)`; everything uses `reshape(-1)` rather than `squeeze(-1)` because squeeze collapses a batch of one to a 0-d scalar. All preds are **raw logits** \u2014 torchmetrics' `Binary*` metrics auto-apply sigmoid when inputs fall outside `[0, 1]`, so no explicit `.sigmoid()` is needed.\n\n---\n\n## 5. `lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py` \u2014 `WaveLightningModule`\n\n```python\ndef __init__(\n    self,\n    model: torch.nn.Module,\n    metrics: Dict[str, Callable[..., Tuple[Dict[str, torch.Tensor], Weights]]],\n    lr: float,                                              # required, no default\n    batch_size: Optional[int] = None,\n    preprocess_fn: Optional[Callable[[Dict], Dict]] = None,\n):\n```\n`metrics` required keys: `\"train_loss\"`, `\"train_metrics\"`, `\"val_metrics\"`. Optional key `\"val_loss\"` \u2014 `self.validation_loss = metrics.get(\"val_loss\", metrics[\"train_loss\"])`, so older dicts keep working. Assigns `self.batch_size`, `self.model`, `self.preprocess_fn`, `self.training_loss`, `self.validation_loss`, `self.training_evaluation`, `self.validation_evaluation`, `self.lr`. It does **not** call `save_hyperparameters()`.\n\nOther members: `_combine_losses(loss_dict, weights) -> torch.Tensor` (staticmethod; weighted sum in `loss_dict.keys()` order, raises `ValueError` on an empty dict), `forward(self, batch: dict) -> torch.Tensor` (returns `self.model(batch)` \u2014 the **whole batch dict** is passed to the model, not `batch[\"ts\"]`, despite what the class docstring says), `configure_optimizers(self) -> torch.optim.Optimizer` (`torch.optim.Adam(self.parameters(), lr=self.lr)`).\n\nBoth steps do `target = batch[\"forecast\"].unsqueeze(1).float()` **before** `preprocess_fn`, then `batch = self.preprocess_fn(batch)` if set, then `output = self(batch)`.\n\n### Exact `self.log()` names\n\n`training_step(self, batch: Dict[str, Any], batch_idx: int) -> torch.Tensor` logs, in order:\n1. `\"train_loss\"` \u2014 `prog_bar=True`\n2. `f\"train_loss_{key}\"` for each key in the train-loss dict \u2014 `prog_bar=False`\n3. `f\"train_metric_{key}\"` for each key in the train-metrics dict, **only if** `len(training_evaluation_weights) > 0` \u2014 `prog_bar=False`\n\n`validation_step(self, batch: Dict[str, Any], batch_idx: int) -> None` logs, in order:\n1. `\"val_loss\"` \u2014 `prog_bar=True` (this is what `ModelCheckpoint` monitors)\n2. `f\"val_loss_{key}\"` for each key in the val-loss dict \u2014 `prog_bar=False`\n3. `f\"val_metric_{key}\"` for each key in the val-metrics dict, **only if** `len(val_evaluation_weights) > 0` \u2014 `prog_bar=False`\n\nEvery call passes `batch_size=self.batch_size, sync_dist=True`.\n\n**Concrete names when paired with `WaveMetrics`:** training logs `train_loss`, `train_loss_bce`, `train_metric_accuracy`. Validation logs `val_loss`, `val_loss_bce`, `val_metric_accuracy`, `val_metric_auroc`. (So the AUROC key in a CSV logger / WandB is `val_metric_auroc`, not `auroc` or `val_auroc`.)",
                "type": "text"
              }
