              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/downstream_apps/test/experiments/wave_common.py"
            },
            "raw_output": "1\t\"\"\"\n2\tShared helpers for the wave-classification experiments.\n3\t\n4\tThree things are needed by more than one script in this directory, so they live here\n5\tonce rather than being copied (and drifting) across the feature extractor, the baseline\n6\tstudy and the fine-tuning entry point:\n7\t\n8\t1. **Event-level stratified subsetting.** The catalog stores each event twice \u2014 once as\n9\t   ``wave`` at ``start_time + 30 min`` and once as ``no wave`` at ``start_time - 30 min``\n10\t   \u2014 so an event is a *matched pair* of samples. Subsetting by event therefore gives\n11\t   exact class balance for free, and never puts the two halves of one event on opposite\n12\t   sides of a split boundary. ``waveDSDataset``'s own ``max_number_of_samples`` cannot do\n13\t   this: it head-slices a frame sorted by ``ds_index``, so ``max_number_of_samples=10``\n14\t   returns the 5 *earliest* pairs (all June-July 2010), which is a chronological bias, not\n15\t   a sample.\n16\t\n17\t2. **Pre-flight assertions.** Every failure mode this task has actually hit is silent:\n18\t   a tokenizer that stays random because its shape disagreed with the checkpoint, LoRA\n19\t   target names that match nothing, a head frozen at its initialization. Each one still\n20\t   produces a plausible-looking loss curve. These helpers turn them into startup errors.\n21\t\n22\t3. **The running-difference feature.** ``RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`` computes it on\n23\t   the GPU during training; ``extract_baseline_features.py`` computes it once and caches\n24\t   it. ``pooled_running_difference()`` is the single definition both use, and\n25\t   ``assert_feature_matches_model()`` checks they agree numerically.\n26\t\"\"\"\n27\t\n28\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n29\t\n30\timport os\n31\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n32\t\n33\timport numpy as np\n34\timport pandas as pd\n35\timport torch\n36\t\n37\t# Column in the wave catalog that identifies an *event*. Both the \"wave\" row\n38\t# (mid_time = start_time + 30 min) and the \"no wave\" row (mid_time - 30 min) of one\n39\t# event share it, which is what makes pair-level subsetting possible.\n40\tEVENT_COL = \"start_time\"\n41\tPOSITIVE_CLASS = \"wave\"\n42\t\n43\t\n44\tdef wave_label_transform(class_series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n45\t    \"\"\"Map the catalog's ``class`` column to float32 1.0 / 0.0.\n46\t\n47\t    Passed to ``waveDSDataset(label_transform=...)``. Kept here so the notebooks, the\n48\t    feature extractor and the training script cannot disagree on which class is positive.\n49\t    \"\"\"\n50\t    return (class_series == POSITIVE_CLASS).astype(np.float32)\n51\t\n52\t\n53\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n54\t# Event-level subsetting\n55\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n56\t\n57\t@dataclass\n58\tclass SubsetInfo:\n59\t    \"\"\"What a subsetting call actually did, for logging and assertions.\"\"\"\n60\t    n_samples: int\n61\t    n_events: int\n62\t    n_positive: int\n63\t    n_negative: int\n64\t    n_dropped_unpaired: int\n65\t    n_events_available: int\n66\t\n67\t    def describe(self) -> str:\n68\t        return (\n69\t            f\"{self.n_samples} samples / {self.n_events} events \"\n70\t            f\"({self.n_positive} wave, {self.n_negative} no wave) \"\n71\t            f\"from {self.n_events_available} complete pairs available; \"\n72\t            f\"dropped {self.n_dropped_unpaired} unpaired sample(s)\"\n73\t        )\n74\t\n75\t\n76\tdef event_stratified_subset(\n77\t    dataset,\n78\t    n_samples: int | None = None,\n79\t    seed: int = 42,\n80\t) -> SubsetInfo:\n81\t    \"\"\"Restrict ``dataset`` in place to a seeded, event-level stratified subset.\n82\t\n83\t    Only events whose *both* samples survived the Surya-index match are kept, so the\n84\t    result is exactly 50/50 by construction and accuracy of 0.5 is exactly chance. On the\n85\t    train split at ``ds_time_tolerance: 15min`` that costs 18 of 1210 matched samples and\n86\t    buys perfect balance.\n87\t\n88\t    Args:\n89\t        dataset: A ``waveDSDataset`` (already constructed, so the Surya-index match has\n90\t            happened). Modified in place.\n91\t        n_samples: Number of samples to keep \u2014 must be even, since events come in pairs.\n92\t            ``None`` keeps every complete pair.\n93\t        seed: Selects which events. Fixed across the runs of a scaling study so that the\n94\t            N=48 subset is a subset of the N=96 subset is a subset of N=192, \u2026; the\n95\t            learning curve then isolates sample count from which events were drawn.\n96\t\n97\t    Returns:\n98\t        A ``SubsetInfo`` describing the selection.\n99\t\n100\t    Raises:\n101\t        ValueError: if ``n_samples`` is odd, or larger than the available paired samples.\n102\t    \"\"\"\n103\t    df = dataset.df_valid_indices\n104\t    if EVENT_COL not in df.columns:\n105\t        raise ValueError(\n106\t            f\"The wave catalog must carry a {EVENT_COL!r} column to subset by event; \"\n107\t            f\"got columns {sorted(df.columns)[:8]}\u2026\"\n108\t        )\n109\t\n110\t    # Positional order of df_valid_indices matches dataset.valid_indices, and\n111\t    # waveDSDataset.__getitem__ indexes both by position. Any mask must be applied to\n112\t    # both, in the same order.\n113\t    labels = df[\"label\"].to_numpy()\n114\t    events = df[EVENT_COL].to_numpy()\n115\t\n116\t    per_event = pd.Series(labels).groupby(events).nunique()\n117\t    complete = np.sort(per_event.index[per_event == 2].to_numpy())\n118\t    n_dropped = int(len(labels) - np.isin(events, complete).sum())\n119\t\n120\t    if n_samples is None:\n121\t        chosen_events = complete\n122\t    else:\n123\t        if n_samples % 2 != 0:\n124\t            raise ValueError(\n125\t                f\"n_samples must be even: events contribute one 'wave' and one 'no wave' \"\n126\t                f\"sample each, so an odd count cannot be class-balanced (got {n_samples}).\"\n127\t            )\n128\t        n_events = n_samples // 2\n129\t        if n_events > len(complete):\n130\t            raise ValueError(\n131\t                f\"Requested {n_samples} samples ({n_events} events) but only \"\n132\t                f\"{len(complete)} complete event pairs ({2 * len(complete)} samples) \"\n133\t                f\"survived the Surya-index match. Loosen data.ds_time_tolerance or \"\n134\t                f\"lower --train-n.\"\n135\t            )\n136\t        # Permute the *sorted* event list so the choice depends only on the seed, not on\n137\t        # dict/CSV ordering. Taking a prefix makes the subsets nested across N, which is\n138\t        # what lets the learning curve attribute a change to sample count alone.\n139\t        order = np.random.default_rng(seed).permutation(len(complete))\n140\t        chosen_events = complete[np.sort(order[:n_events])]\n141\t\n142\t    keep = np.flatnonzero(np.isin(events, chosen_events))\n143\t\n144\t    dataset.df_valid_indices = df.iloc[keep]\n145\t    dataset.valid_indices = [dataset.valid_indices[i] for i in keep]\n146\t    dataset.adjusted_length = len(keep)\n147\t\n148\t    kept_labels = dataset.df_valid_indices[\"label\"].to_numpy()\n149\t    info = SubsetInfo(\n150\t        n_samples=len(keep),\n151\t        n_events=len(chosen_events),\n152\t        n_positive=int((kept_labels == 1).sum()),\n153\t        n_negative=int((kept_labels == 0).sum()),\n154\t        n_dropped_unpaired=n_dropped,\n155\t        n_events_available=len(complete),\n156\t    )\n157\t    if info.n_positive != info.n_negative:\n158\t        raise AssertionError(\n159\t            f\"Event-level subsetting should be exactly balanced but produced \"\n160\t            f\"{info.n_positive} wave / {info.n_negative} no wave. This means an event \"\n161\t            f\"contributed two samples of the same class.\"\n162\t        )\n163\t    return info\n164\t\n165\t\n166\tdef dataset_frame_uris(dataset) -> list[str]:\n167\t    \"\"\"Return the distinct NetCDF paths a dataset will read, in chronological order.\n168\t\n169\t    Used to report how much of the S3 cache is already warm before a run starts, and to\n170\t    size the download.\n171\t    \"\"\"\n172\t    offsets = dataset.time_delta_input_minutes\n173\t    stamps = sorted({ts + dt for ts in dataset.valid_indices for dt in offsets})\n174\t    return [dataset.index.loc[ts, \"path\"] for ts in stamps]\n175\t\n176\t\n177\tdef cache_coverage(dataset) -> tuple[int, int]:\n178\t    \"\"\"Return ``(n_cached, n_total)`` for the frames ``dataset`` will read.\n179\t\n180\t    Uses the dataset's own cache-path scheme, so the answer reflects what the training\n181\t    run will actually find rather than a guess at the naming.\n182\t    \"\"\"\n183\t    uris = dataset_frame_uris(dataset)\n184\t    # The in-memory dataset consults local_cache_dir, not s3_cache_dir, and only for the\n185\t    # frames on its whitelist. Ask it where it would look rather than assuming.\n186\t    resolve = getattr(dataset, \"_local_cache_path\", None) or getattr(\n187\t        dataset, \"_s3_cache_path\", None)\n188\t    root = getattr(dataset, \"local_cache_dir\", None) or dataset.s3_cache_dir\n189\t    if not root or resolve is None:\n190\t        return 0, len(uris)\n191\t    cached = sum(1 for u in uris if u.startswith(\"s3://\") and os.path.exists(resolve(u)))\n192\t    return cached, len(uris)\n193\t\n194\t\n195\t# Frames per gigabyte of cache, for turning a byte budget into a frame count. One\n196\t# 13-channel 4096x4096 SDO frame is ~0.59 GB on disk.\n197\tFRAME_GB = 0.59\n198\t\n199\t# Space to keep free on the cache filesystem. A Lightning checkpoint of this model is ~1.45 GB\n200\t# (the state dict carries the frozen backbone, not only the adapters), and a full schedule\n201\t# writes one per run.\n202\tRESERVE_GB = 15.0\n203\t\n204\t\n205\tdef plan_pinned_frames(\n206\t    val_dataset,\n207\t    train_dataset,\n208\t    budget_gb: float,\n209\t    train_priority_n: int | None = None,\n210\t    subset_seed: int = 42,\n211\t) -> tuple[set[str], dict]:\n212\t    \"\"\"Choose which frames to keep on fast local storage, given a byte budget.\n213\t\n214\t    The S3 read path sustains ~175 MB/s inside the DataLoader, against ~3.1 s of GPU per\n215\t    sample, so a run that streams every frame is **data-bound at roughly twice the GPU's\n216\t    cost**. Local disk is 350 MB/s but only ~66 GB (\u2248110 frames) of it is free, against a\n217\t    481 GB working set. So the budget has to be spent where it buys the most epochs.\n218\t\n219\t    Two facts make the choice easy:\n220\t\n221\t    * The **validation split is read on every epoch** and is small (48 frames, 28 GB). It is\n222\t      pinned first, unconditionally: over a 30-epoch run those 48 frames would otherwise be\n223\t      fetched 1,440 times.\n224\t    * The event-level subsets are **nested** \u2014 the N=48 subset's events are a subset of\n225\t      N=96's, which are a subset of N=192's. So pinning the frames of the *smallest*\n226\t      training subset makes those frames local for every larger run too. The small runs\n227\t      become GPU-bound, and the large ones still get a partial hit.\n228\t\n229\t    Args:\n230\t        val_dataset: The already-subset validation dataset. Pinned first.\n231\t        train_dataset: The already-subset training dataset, at its full N. Its\n232\t            ``train_priority_n``-sample nested prefix is pinned with whatever budget remains.\n233\t        budget_gb: Total bytes to spend, in GB. Should be below the free space on the cache\n234\t            filesystem, since the frames land there.\n235\t        train_priority_n: Which nested prefix of the training subset to prioritize. Defaults\n236\t            to the smallest scaling run (48). ``None`` disables train pinning.\n237\t        subset_seed: Must match the seed the runs use, or the \"nested prefix\" is a different\n238\t            set of events.\n239\t\n240\t    Returns:\n241\t        ``(uris, report)`` \u2014 the whitelist, and a dict describing how the budget was spent.\n242\t    \"\"\"\n243\t    import copy\n244\t    import shutil\n245\t\n246\t    # Clamp to what the cache filesystem can actually give. Lightning checkpoints hold the\n247\t    # frozen backbone as well as the adapters (~1.45 GB each), and a schedule of six runs\n248\t    # therefore needs ~9 GB that must not be eaten by cached frames.\n249\t    cache_root = getattr(val_dataset, \"local_cache_dir\", None)\n250\t    if cache_root and os.path.isdir(cache_root):\n251\t        free_gb = shutil.disk_usage(cache_root).free / 2**30\n252\t        already_gb = sum(\n253\t            e.stat().st_size for e in os.scandir(cache_root) if e.is_file()) / 2**30\n254\t        allowed = already_gb + max(free_gb - RESERVE_GB, 0.0)\n255\t        if budget_gb > allowed:\n256\t            print(f\"[cache] budget {budget_gb:.0f} GB exceeds what {cache_root} can give \"\n257\t                  f\"({free_gb:.0f} GB free, {already_gb:.0f} GB already cached, \"\n258\t                  f\"{RESERVE_GB:.0f} GB reserved for checkpoints); using {allowed:.0f} GB\")\n259\t            budget_gb = allowed\n260\t\n261\t    val_uris = [u for u in dataset_frame_uris(val_dataset) if u.startswith(\"s3://\")]\n262\t    budget_frames = int(budget_gb / FRAME_GB)\n263\t    chosen = list(val_uris[:budget_frames])\n264\t    report = {\n265\t        \"budget_gb\": budget_gb,\n266\t        \"budget_frames\": budget_frames,\n267\t        \"val_frames_pinned\": len(chosen),\n268\t        \"val_frames_total\": len(val_uris),\n269\t        \"train_frames_pinned\": 0,\n270\t        \"train_priority_n\": train_priority_n,\n271\t    }\n272\t\n273\t    remaining = budget_frames - len(chosen)\n274\t    if train_priority_n and remaining > 0:\n275\t        # A shallow copy shares the dataframe and index but gets its own valid_indices, so\n276\t        # subsetting the copy does not disturb the dataset that is about to be trained on.\n277\t        probe = copy.copy(train_dataset)\n278\t        probe.valid_indices = list(train_dataset.valid_indices)\n279\t        probe.df_valid_indices = train_dataset.df_valid_indices\n280\t        try:\n281\t            event_stratified_subset(probe, n_samples=train_priority_n, seed=subset_seed)\n282\t        except ValueError as exc:\n283\t            report[\"train_pin_skipped\"] = str(exc)\n284\t        else:\n285\t            train_uris = [u for u in dataset_frame_uris(probe) if u.startswith(\"s3://\")]\n286\t            take = train_uris[:remaining]\n287\t            chosen.extend(take)\n288\t            report[\"train_frames_pinned\"] = len(take)\n289\t            report[\"train_frames_priority_total\"] = len(train_uris)\n290\t\n291\t    uris = set(chosen)\n292\t    report[\"total_frames_pinned\"] = len(uris)\n293\t    report[\"estimated_gb\"] = round(len(uris) * FRAME_GB, 1)\n294\t    return uris, report\n295\t\n296\t\n297\tdef pin_frames(dataset, extra_datasets=(), uris: set[str] | None = None) -> int:\n298\t    \"\"\"Apply a local-disk cache whitelist to one or more datasets, and return its size.\n299\t\n300\t    Only meaningful for ``waveDSDatasetInMemory``, whose ``cache_paths`` attribute this\n301\t    sets. Call it after subsetting, and before the DataLoaders are built: the loaders use\n302\t    spawn, so the attribute is pickled to the workers as it stands at that moment.\n303\t\n304\t    Args:\n305\t        dataset: Dataset whose frames are pinned when ``uris`` is not given.\n306\t        extra_datasets: Other datasets that should honour the same whitelist. They share the\n307\t            cache directory, so listing them lets them *read* the pinned frames \u2014 without\n308\t            this, a train dataset would re-fetch a frame the val dataset had already cached.\n309\t        uris: An explicit whitelist, e.g. from ``plan_pinned_frames()``. When ``None``, all\n310\t            of ``dataset``'s own frames are used.\n311\t\n312\t    Returns:\n313\t        Number of frames whitelisted, or 0 if these datasets have no local cache.\n314\t    \"\"\"\n315\t    if not hasattr(dataset, \"cache_paths\"):\n316\t        return 0\n317\t    if uris is None:\n318\t        uris = {u for u in dataset_frame_uris(dataset) if u.startswith(\"s3://\")}\n319\t    for ds in (dataset, *extra_datasets):\n320\t        if hasattr(ds, \"cache_paths\"):\n321\t            ds.cache_paths = set(uris)\n322\t    return len(uris)\n323\t\n324\t\n325\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n326\t# Pre-flight assertions\n327\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n328\t\n329\tdef count_matched_pretrained(model: torch.nn.Module, checkpoint_path: str) -> tuple[int, int]:\n330\t    \"\"\"Count how many checkpoint tensors are present in ``model`` *after* loading.\n331\t\n332\t    Call this after ``load_pretrained_weights()``. For each checkpoint tensor it looks for\n333\t    a model tensor \u2014 under the checkpoint's own key or under ``backbone.<key>``, the two\n334\t    spellings the loader tries \u2014 that is bit-identical to it.\n335\t\n336\t    This is deliberately a check on the *outcome* rather than a second copy of the\n337\t    loader's matching rule: a tensor whose shape disagreed was skipped and still holds its\n338\t    random initialization, so it fails the comparison, which is exactly the question\n339\t    (\"did the pretrained tokenizer actually land?\") that bug 1 was about.\n340\t\n341\t    Returns:\n342\t        ``(n_matched, n_checkpoint_tensors)``. For Surya 366M the ceiling is 157/159: the\n343\t        two ``unembed.unembed.0.*`` tensors are the pretraining decoder, which the\n344\t        fine-tuning wrapper builds with ``finetune=True`` and therefore does not have.\n345\t    \"\"\"\n346\t    ckpt = torch.load(checkpoint_path, weights_only=True, map_location=\"cpu\")\n347\t    state = model.state_dict()\n348\t    matched = 0\n349\t    for key, value in ckpt.items():\n350\t        for candidate in (key, f\"backbone.{key}\"):\n351\t            have = state.get(candidate)\n352\t            if have is not None and have.shape == value.shape:\n353\t                if torch.equal(have.detach().cpu(), value):\n354\t                    matched += 1\n355\t                break\n356\t    return matched, len(ckpt)\n357\t\n358\t\n359\tdef assert_tokenizer_loaded(model: torch.nn.Module, checkpoint_path: str) -> int:\n360\t    \"\"\"Assert the pretrained patch embedding actually landed, and return the match count.\n361\t\n362\t    The failure this guards is silent and total: with ``time_dim: 1`` the model builds a\n363\t    13-channel ``Conv2d`` while the checkpoint's is ``Conv2d(26, 1280, 16, 16)``\n364\t    (13 channels x 2 timesteps), the shapes disagree, ``load_pretrained_weights()`` skips\n365\t    the key, and every image entering the transformer is tokenized by *random* weights.\n366\t    The loss still falls.\n367\t    \"\"\"\n368\t    matched, total = count_matched_pretrained(model, checkpoint_path)\n369\t    print(f\"[PREFLIGHT] pretrained tensors matched: {matched} / {total}\")\n370\t    if matched < total - 2:\n371\t        raise AssertionError(\n372\t            f\"Only {matched} of {total} pretrained tensors are present in the model. \"\n373\t            f\"Expected {total - 2} (everything but the two pretraining-decoder tensors, \"\n374\t            f\"unembed.unembed.0.weight/bias, which finetune=True does not build).\\n\"\n375\t            f\"The usual cause is model.time_embedding.time_dim disagreeing with the \"\n376\t            f\"checkpoint's tokenizer: it is Conv2d(in_channels * time_dim, ...), and the \"\n377\t            f\"checkpoint was trained with time_dim=2 (26 input channels).\"\n378\t        )\n379\t    return matched\n380\t\n381\t\n382\tdef assert_lora_adapted_attention(model: torch.nn.Module) -> list[str]:\n383\t    \"\"\"Assert LoRA adapters reached attention, not just the feed-forward layers.\n384\t\n385\t    PEFT raises only when *no* ``target_modules`` entry matches anything, so a list of\n386\t    plausible-but-wrong names (``q_proj``, ``k_proj``, ``v_proj``, ``out_proj`` \u2014 none of\n387\t    which exist in Surya) is silently reduced to whatever else happened to match. That is\n388\t    how a run trained ``fc1``/``fc2`` alone and reported 2,666,241 trainable parameters\n389\t    where 3,157,761 was intended.\n390\t    \"\"\"\n391\t    adapted = sorted({\n392\t        name.split(\".lora_A\")[0].replace(\"base_model.model.\", \"\")\n393\t        for name, _ in model.named_parameters()\n394\t        if \".lora_A\" in name\n395\t    })\n396\t    missing = [\n397\t        needle for needle in (\"attn.qkv\", \"attn.proj\")\n398\t        if not any(a.endswith(needle) for a in adapted)\n399\t    ]\n400\t    if missing:\n401\t        raise AssertionError(\n402\t            f\"LoRA did not adapt {', '.join(missing)}. Surya fuses q/k/v into attn.qkv and \"\n403\t            f\"names the output projection attn.proj; PEFT silently ignores target_modules \"\n404\t            f\"entries that match nothing.\\nAdapted modules were: {adapted}\"\n405\t        )\n406\t    n_attn = sum(1 for a in adapted if \".attn.\" in a)\n407\t    print(f\"[PREFLIGHT] LoRA adapted {len(adapted)} modules, {n_attn} of them in attention\")\n408\t    return adapted\n409\t\n410\t\n411\tdef assert_trainable_count(model: torch.nn.Module, expected: int | None = None) -> int:\n412\t    \"\"\"Print (and optionally assert) the trainable parameter count.\n413\t\n414\t    Reference values for this task's config, pinned by ``tests/test_lora_setup.py``:\n415\t\n416\t    ==============================================  =========\n417\t    regime                                          trainable\n418\t    ==============================================  =========\n419\t    LoRA r=8, penultimate_linear_layer: false       1,518,081\n420\t    LoRA r=8, penultimate_linear_layer: true        3,157,761\n421\t    linear probe (head only), penultimate false         2,561\n422\t    ==============================================  =========\n423\t    \"\"\"\n424\t    trainable = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\n425\t    total = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())\n426\t    print(f\"[PREFLIGHT] trainable: {trainable:,} / {total:,} ({100 * trainable / total:.3f}%)\")\n427\t    if expected is not None and trainable != expected:\n428\t        raise AssertionError(\n429\t            f\"Expected {expected:,} trainable parameters, got {trainable:,}. Check \"\n430\t            f\"model.use_lora, model.lora_config.r, model.penultimate_linear_layer and the \"\n431\t            f\"head_* modules apply_peft_lora() reported as modules_to_save.\"\n432\t        )\n433\t    return trainable\n434\t\n435\t\n436\tdef assert_batch_shape(batch: dict, n_channels: int, n_timesteps: int, img_size: int) -> None:\n437\t    \"\"\"Assert the input stack has the shape the backbone's tokenizer was trained for.\"\"\"\n438\t    ts = batch[\"ts\"]\n439\t    expected = (n_channels, n_timesteps, img_size, img_size)\n440\t    if tuple(ts.shape[1:]) != expected:\n441\t        raise AssertionError(\n442\t            f\"batch['ts'] has shape {tuple(ts.shape)}; expected (B, {', '.join(map(str, expected))}). \"\n443\t            f\"ts.shape[2] is the number of input timesteps and must be 2 for this \"\n444\t            f\"checkpoint \u2014 it comes from model.time_embedding.time_dim and \"\n445\t            f\"data.time_delta_input_minutes.\"\n446\t        )\n447\t    print(f\"[PREFLIGHT] batch['ts'] shape {tuple(ts.shape)}, dtype {ts.dtype}\")\n448\t\n449\t\n450\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n451\t# The running-difference feature\n452\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n453\t\n454\tdef pooled_running_difference(\n455\t    ts_signum_log: torch.Tensor,\n456\t    channel_index: int,\n457\t    pool_kernel: int,\n458\t) -> torch.Tensor:\n459\t    \"\"\"AIA193 running difference (now - previous), mean-pooled.\n460\t\n461\t    This is the single definition of the baseline's feature.\n462\t    ``RunningDifferenceLogisticModel.forward()`` computes it per training step;\n463\t    ``extract_baseline_features.py`` computes it once and caches the result.\n464\t\n465\t    Mean pooling composes: pooling a ``pool_kernel=8`` map by a further factor of 4 is\n466\t    identical to pooling the original by 32, because every block is the same size. That\n467\t    is what lets the cache be written once at kernel 8 (512x512, 1 MB/sample) and still\n468\t    serve the kernel-16/32/64/128 sweep exactly.\n469\t\n470\t    Args:\n471\t        ts_signum_log: ``(B, C, T, H, W)`` in **signum-log** space \u2014 z-score undone,\n472\t            log compression retained. Use ``destandardize_channels()`` to get there from\n473\t            what the dataset returns.\n474\t        channel_index: Index of AIA193 within the C dimension.\n475\t        pool_kernel: Mean-pool kernel size (and stride).\n476\t\n477\t    Returns:\n478\t        ``(B, H // pool_kernel, W // pool_kernel)``.\n479\t    \"\"\"\n480\t    x = ts_signum_log[:, channel_index, :, :, :]           # (B, T, H, W)\n481\t    diff = x[:, -1] - x[:, 0]                              # now - previous\n482\t    pooled = torch.nn.functional.avg_pool2d(diff.unsqueeze(1), kernel_size=pool_kernel)\n483\t    return pooled.squeeze(1)\n484\t\n485\t\n486\tdef repool(features: np.ndarray, factor: int) -> np.ndarray:\n487\t    \"\"\"Mean-pool cached feature maps by a further integer ``factor``.\n488\t\n489\t    Args:\n490\t        features: ``(N, S, S)`` cached at some base kernel.\n491\t        factor: Additional pooling factor; must divide ``S``.\n492\t\n493\t    Returns:\n494\t        ``(N, S // factor, S // factor)``.\n495\t    \"\"\"\n496\t    if factor == 1:\n497\t        return features\n498\t    n, s, _ = features.shape\n499\t    if s % factor:\n500\t        raise ValueError(f\"repool factor {factor} does not divide the {s}x{s} cached map.\")\n501\t    out = features.reshape(n, s // factor, factor, s // factor, factor)\n502\t    return out.mean(axis=(2, 4))\n503\t\n504\t\n505\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n506\t# Datasets and DataLoaders\n507\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n508\t\n509\tdef build_wave_datasets(\n510\t    cfg,\n511\t    scalers,\n512\t    include_test: bool = False,\n513\t    in_memory: bool = True,\n514\t    local_cache_budget_gb: float = 40.0,\n515\t):\n516\t    \"\"\"Build the train / val (/ test) ``waveDSDataset`` objects described by ``cfg``.\n517\t\n518\t    Thin wrapper over ``build_helio_datasets()`` that adds three things this task needs:\n519\t    the wave-specific keyword arguments in one place, the **test** split, and the choice of\n520\t    read path.\n521\t\n522\t    ``in_memory=True`` (the default) uses ``waveDSDatasetInMemory``, which fetches each S3\n523\t    object into RAM and hands the buffer to h5netcdf rather than writing 0.59 GB to disk\n524\t    first. On this machine that is 400 MB/s against 9 MB/s, because the configured cache\n525\t    directory is on a throughput-capped EFS mount \u2014 see that class's module docstring for\n526\t    the measurements. Set ``in_memory=False`` for the stock ``s3_mode``-driven path.\n527\t\n528\t    ``build_helio_datasets()`` returns exactly two datasets \u2014 one from\n529\t    ``data.train_data_path`` with ``phase=\"train\"`` and one from ``data.valid_data_path``\n530\t    with ``phase=\"val\"``. To get the third split we call it a second time with\n531\t    ``valid_data_path`` pointed at the test index and keep the second dataset. That is the\n532\t    right ``phase`` for a held-out split (no channel masking, no flips), and it means the\n533\t    ~20 generic dataset arguments still come from the one place that owns them.\n534\t\n535\t    Returns:\n536\t        ``(train, val)`` or ``(train, val, test)``.\n537\t    \"\"\"\n538\t    from workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_datasets\n539\t\n540\t    if in_memory:\n541\t        from downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset_memory import (\n542\t            waveDSDatasetInMemory as dataset_cls,\n543\t        )\n544\t        read_kwargs = dict(\n545\t            # Populated after subsetting by pin_frames(): the whitelist has to be the\n546\t            # frames the *subset* reads, which is not known until the subset exists.\n547\t            cache_paths=None,\n548\t            local_cache_dir=cfg.data.s3_cache_dir,\n549\t            local_cache_budget_gb=local_cache_budget_gb,\n550\t        )\n551\t    else:\n552\t        from downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset as dataset_cls\n553\t        read_kwargs = {}\n554\t\n555\t    task_kwargs = dict(\n556\t        **read_kwargs,\n557\t        return_surya_stack=True,\n558\t        # None on purpose: max_number_of_samples head-slices chronologically. Subsetting\n559\t        # is event_stratified_subset()'s job, after the match has happened.\n560\t        max_number_of_samples=None,\n561\t        label_transform=wave_label_transform,\n562\t        ds_wave_index_path=cfg.data.wave_index_path,\n563\t        ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n564\t        ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column,\n565\t        ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n566\t        ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n567\t    )\n568\t\n569\t    train, val = build_helio_datasets(cfg, dataset_cls, scalers=scalers, **task_kwargs)\n570\t    if not include_test:\n571\t        return train, val\n572\t\n573\t    test_index = str(cfg.data.train_data_path).replace(\"_train.csv\", \"_test.csv\")\n574\t    if not os.path.isfile(test_index):\n575\t        raise FileNotFoundError(\n576\t            f\"Test index not found at {test_index}. It is derived from \"\n577\t            f\"data.train_data_path by swapping _train.csv for _test.csv.\"\n578\t        )\n579\t    original = cfg.data.valid_data_path\n580\t    try:\n581\t        cfg.data.valid_data_path = test_index\n582\t        _, test = build_helio_datasets(cfg, dataset_cls, scalers=scalers, **task_kwargs)\n583\t    finally:\n584\t        cfg.data.valid_data_path = original\n585\t    return train, val, test\n586\t\n587\t\n588\tdef build_wave_loader(\n589\t    dataset,\n590\t    batch_size: int,\n591\t    num_workers: int,\n592\t    prefetch_factor: int | None,\n593\t    seed: int,\n594\t    shuffle: bool,\n595\t    drop_last: bool,\n596\t):\n597\t    \"\"\"Build a DataLoader with the two knobs ``build_helio_dataloaders()`` does not expose.\n598\t\n599\t    ``build_helio_dataloaders()`` is the right entry point for a normal run, but it hard-codes\n600\t    ``drop_last=True`` for *both* loaders and does not expose ``prefetch_factor``. Both matter\n601\t    here:\n602\t\n603\t    * ``drop_last=True`` on a 24-sample validation set at batch 2 is survivable, but on any\n604\t      odd-sized split it silently discards a sample \u2014 1 of 27 is 3.7% of the metric that\n605\t      selects checkpoints.\n606\t    * ``prefetch_factor`` is the only bound on worker memory. ``ts`` is\n607\t      ``(B, 13, 2, 4096, 4096)`` fp32 = 1.74 GB per sample, so worst-case resident set is\n608\t      ``num_workers * prefetch_factor * batch_size * 1.74 GB``: 27.8 GB at\n609\t      ``8 x 1 x 2``, but 55.7 GB at PyTorch's default factor of 2.\n610\t\n611\t    Everything else \u2014 spawn (the dataset holds a boto3 handle that does not survive fork),\n612\t    persistent workers, the explicit generator and the per-worker seeding \u2014 matches\n613\t    ``build_helio_dataloaders()`` exactly, by reusing its ``_seed_worker``. That function is\n614\t    private but importing it is deliberate: its whole purpose is to displace Lightning's\n615\t    auto-injected worker init, which derives worker seeds from ambient global RNG state, and\n616\t    a second copy of it here could drift out of step with the datasets it seeds.\n617\t    \"\"\"\n618\t    from functools import partial\n619\t\n620\t    from torch.utils.data import DataLoader\n621\t    from workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import _seed_worker\n622\t\n623\t    kwargs = dict(\n624\t        batch_size=batch_size,\n625\t        num_workers=num_workers,\n626\t        pin_memory=True,\n627\t        drop_last=drop_last,\n628\t    )\n629\t    if num_workers > 0:\n630\t        kwargs[\"multiprocessing_context\"] = \"spawn\"\n631\t        kwargs[\"persistent_workers\"] = True\n632\t        kwargs[\"worker_init_fn\"] = partial(_seed_worker, base_seed=seed)\n633\t        if prefetch_factor is not None:\n634\t            kwargs[\"prefetch_factor\"] = prefetch_factor\n635\t\n636\t    if shuffle:\n637\t        generator = torch.Generator()\n638\t        generator.manual_seed(seed)\n639\t        kwargs[\"generator\"] = generator\n640\t\n641\t    return DataLoader(dataset, shuffle=shuffle, **kwargs)\n642\t",
