          {
            "raw_output": "1\t\"\"\"\n2\tAn in-memory-reading ``waveDSDataset``, and why this task needs one.\n3\t\n4\t``HelioNetCDFDataset``'s three S3 modes all assume the local filesystem is fast:\n5\t``download`` and ``simplecache`` write the whole 0.59 GB object into ``s3_cache_dir``\n6\tbefore opening it, and ``stream`` serves HDF5's random access as many small ranged GETs\n7\t(measured ~9x slower than fetching the object whole). That assumption does not hold on\n8\tthis machine. Measured, one 13-channel SDO frame:\n9\t\n10\t===========================================  ==========\n11\tdestination                                  throughput\n12\t===========================================  ==========\n13\tS3 -> memory (BytesIO), concurrency 16          292 MB/s\n14\tS3 -> local NVMe (/home/jovyan), conc. 16       352 MB/s\n15\tS3 -> EFS scratch (the configured cache), 8     8.9 MB/s\n16\tS3 -> EFS scratch, concurrency 16               5.9 MB/s\n17\t===========================================  ==========\n18\t\n19\tThe configured ``s3_cache_dir`` is on an EFS mount whose throughput is a property of the\n20\t*filesystem*, not of this process: EFS bursting mode allots roughly 50 KB/s per GB stored,\n21\twhich for the 4.2 TB filesystem is ~210 MB/s shared by every client mounting it, and\n22\tanother tenant was consuming almost all of it. Raising concurrency made it *worse*, which\n23\tis the signature of a contended filesystem rather than a slow client. The local NVMe is\n24\tfast but only has 77 GB free, against a ~500 GB working set.\n25\t\n26\tSo the fix is to stop writing the frame down at all: fetch the whole object into RAM and\n27\thand the buffer to ``h5netcdf``, which gives HDF5 the random access it needs without\n28\ttouching a disk. One in-flight frame costs 0.59 GB of RAM, so eight workers holding two\n29\tframes each is ~9.4 GB \u2014 affordable on 124 GB, and the network path is 30-40x faster than\n30\tthe one it replaces.\n31\t\n32\tTwo consequences worth being explicit about:\n33\t\n34\t* **Nothing is cached, so every epoch re-reads from S3.** At ~1.18 GB per sample (two\n35\t  frames) and ~300 MB/s that is ~3.9 s of network per sample against ~3.1 s of GPU, so the\n36\t  data pipeline becomes the (mild) limiter rather than the GPU. ``cache_paths`` exists for\n37\t  the one case where that matters: the validation split is read every single epoch, and it\n38\t  is small enough (48 frames, 28 GB) to pin on the local NVMe.\n39\t* **This is a read-path change only.** The bytes decoded, the channels extracted and the\n40\t  normalization applied are identical to the parent class's, so a run using this dataset is\n41\t  numerically the same as one using ``s3_mode: download`` \u2014 just faster here.\n42\t\"\"\"\n43\t\n44\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n45\t\n46\timport io\n47\timport os\n48\timport threading\n49\tfrom uuid import uuid4\n50\t\n51\timport numpy as np\n52\timport xarray as xr\n53\t\n54\tfrom downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset\n55\tfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import make_s3_client, parse_s3_uri\n56\t\n57\ttry:\n58\t    from boto3.s3.transfer import TransferConfig\n59\texcept Exception:  # pragma: no cover\n60\t    TransferConfig = None\n61\t\n62\t\n63\tclass waveDSDatasetInMemory(waveDSDataset):\n64\t    \"\"\"``waveDSDataset`` that fetches S3 objects into RAM instead of onto disk.\n65\t\n66\t    Overrides the public ``load_nc_data()`` only. Local (non-``s3://``) paths and every\n67\t    other part of the pipeline \u2014 frame sampling, channel masking, signum-log\n68\t    normalization, the label merge \u2014 are the parent classes' unchanged.\n69\t\n70\t    Additional Args:\n71\t        cache_paths: S3 URIs that *should* be written to ``local_cache_dir`` after being\n72\t            fetched, because they will be read again many times. Pass the validation\n73\t            split's frames here: they are re-read every epoch, and pinning 48 of them costs\n74\t            28 GB. Everything not listed is fetched to memory and discarded, so the train\n75\t            split cannot evict them. ``None`` disables disk caching entirely.\n76\t        local_cache_dir: Where cached frames go. Must be on *fast* local storage \u2014 the\n77\t            point of this class is that the configured ``s3_cache_dir`` is not.\n78\t        local_cache_budget_gb: Refuse to write more than this into ``local_cache_dir``,\n79\t            so a mis-sized ``cache_paths`` cannot fill the root filesystem.\n80\t        connect_timeout_s, read_timeout_s: Socket timeouts for the S3 client. Set explicitly\n81\t            because the shared ``make_s3_client()`` helper exposes none, and its defaults let\n82\t            a stalled connection retry for minutes.\n83\t        fetch_timeout_s: Wall-clock deadline for one whole-object fetch, including boto3's\n84\t            internal retries. A healthy fetch is 1.4-3.3 s.\n85\t        fetch_attempts: How many times to retry a failed or timed-out fetch before giving up\n86\t            and failing the sample (which fails the run, deliberately: a silently skipped\n87\t            frame would change the dataset without changing any reported count).\n88\t    \"\"\"\n89\t\n90\t    def __init__(\n91\t        self,\n92\t        cache_paths: set[str] | None = None,\n93\t        local_cache_dir: str | None = None,\n94\t        local_cache_budget_gb: float = 60.0,\n95\t        s3_boto3_pool_size: int = 64,\n96\t        connect_timeout_s: float = 10.0,\n97\t        read_timeout_s: float = 45.0,\n98\t        fetch_timeout_s: float = 180.0,\n99\t        fetch_attempts: int = 4,\n100\t        **kwargs,\n101\t    ):\n102\t        super().__init__(**kwargs)\n103\t        self.cache_paths = set(cache_paths) if cache_paths else set()\n104\t        self.local_cache_dir = local_cache_dir\n105\t        self.local_cache_budget_bytes = int(local_cache_budget_gb * 2**30)\n106\t        self.s3_boto3_pool_size = s3_boto3_pool_size\n107\t        # A healthy 0.59 GB fetch takes 1.4-3.3 s, so 180 s is ~50x the expected time: long\n108\t        # enough that a merely slow transfer is never abandoned, short enough that a hang\n109\t        # costs one frame rather than the run.\n110\t        self.connect_timeout_s = connect_timeout_s\n111\t        self.read_timeout_s = read_timeout_s\n112\t        self.fetch_timeout_s = fetch_timeout_s\n113\t        self.fetch_attempts = fetch_attempts\n114\t        # boto3 clients are not fork-safe and the loaders use spawn, so the client is\n115\t        # built lazily, once per worker process, and kept for the process's lifetime\n116\t        # (the parent class builds a fresh client per download, which costs a TLS\n117\t        # handshake on every frame).\n118\t        self._client = None\n119\t        self._client_lock = threading.Lock()\n120\t        if self.local_cache_dir:\n121\t            os.makedirs(self.local_cache_dir, exist_ok=True)\n122\t\n123\t    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n124\t    # Pickling\n125\t    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n126\t\n127\t    def __getstate__(self):\n128\t        \"\"\"Drop the client and its lock so the dataset can cross a spawn boundary.\n129\t\n130\t        DataLoader workers are started with ``multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"``, which\n131\t        pickles the dataset. Neither a boto3 client nor a ``threading.Lock`` is picklable,\n132\t        and neither *should* cross the boundary: a client carries connection state that is\n133\t        only valid in the process that opened it. Both are rebuilt on first use in the\n134\t        worker.\n135\t        \"\"\"\n136\t        state = self.__dict__.copy()\n137\t        state[\"_client\"] = None\n138\t        state[\"_client_lock\"] = None\n139\t        return state\n140\t\n141\t    def __setstate__(self, state):\n142\t        self.__dict__.update(state)\n143\t        self._client_lock = threading.Lock()\n144\t\n145\t    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n146\t    # S3 client\n147\t    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n148\t\n149\t    def _get_client(self):\n150\t        \"\"\"Return this process's S3 client, building it on first use.\n151\t\n152\t        Deliberately does **not** use ``workshop_infrastructure.utils.make_s3_client``, which\n153\t        is otherwise the right helper: it exposes no socket timeouts, and botocore's defaults\n154\t        combined with ``mode=\"adaptive\"`` and ``max_attempts=10`` can leave a single stalled\n155\t        connection retrying for many minutes. That is survivable interactively and fatal for\n156\t        an unattended schedule \u2014 observed here, one hung fetch blocked all eight DataLoader\n157\t        workers for ten minutes, because with ``prefetch_factor=1`` the main process consumes\n158\t        batches in order and every other worker was left waiting on the stalled one. Short\n159\t        timeouts and fewer attempts turn a hang into a fast retry.\n160\t        \"\"\"\n161\t        if self._client is None:\n162\t            if self._client_lock is None:  # unpickled without __setstate__ running\n163\t                self._client_lock = threading.Lock()\n164\t            with self._client_lock:\n165\t                if self._client is None:\n166\t                    import boto3\n167\t                    from botocore import UNSIGNED\n168\t                    from botocore.config import Config as BotoConfig\n169\t\n170\t                    from workshop_infrastructure.utils import detect_ec2_region\n171\t                    anon = bool(self.s3_storage_options.get(\"anon\")\n172\t                                or self.s3fs_kwargs.get(\"anon\"))\n173\t                    region = (os.environ.get(\"AWS_REGION\")\n174\t                              or os.environ.get(\"AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\")\n175\t                              or detect_ec2_region())\n176\t                    kwargs = dict(\n177\t                        max_pool_connections=max(self.s3_boto3_pool_size,\n178\t                                                 self.s3_boto3_max_concurrency * 2),\n179\t                        connect_timeout=self.connect_timeout_s,\n180\t                        read_timeout=self.read_timeout_s,\n181\t                        retries={\"max_attempts\": 4, \"mode\": \"adaptive\"},\n182\t                    )\n183\t                    if anon:\n184\t                        kwargs[\"signature_version\"] = UNSIGNED\n185\t                    self._client = boto3.client(\"s3\", region_name=region,\n186\t                                                config=BotoConfig(**kwargs))\n187\t        return self._client\n188\t\n189\t    def _reset_client(self) -> None:\n190\t        \"\"\"Discard the client, tearing down its connection pool.\n191\t\n192\t        Called after a stalled fetch. Closing the pool is what makes an abandoned download\n193\t        thread fail instead of holding its socket, and the next call builds a fresh client.\n194\t        \"\"\"\n195\t        client, self._client = self._client, None\n196\t        if client is not None:\n197\t            try:\n198\t                client.close()\n199\t            except Exception:\n200\t                pass\n201\t\n202\t    def _transfer_config(self):\n203\t        part = self.s3_boto3_part_size_mb * 1024 * 1024\n204\t        return TransferConfig(\n205\t            multipart_threshold=part,\n206\t            multipart_chunksize=part,\n207\t            max_concurrency=self.s3_boto3_max_concurrency,\n208\t            use_threads=True,\n209\t            io_chunksize=1024 * 1024,\n210\t        )\n211\t\n212\t    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n213\t    # Reading\n214\t    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n215\t\n216\t    def _local_cache_path(self, s3_uri: str) -> str:\n217\t        \"\"\"Local path for a cached frame, using the parent's naming so it is recognizable.\"\"\"\n218\t        return os.path.join(self.local_cache_dir, os.path.basename(self._s3_cache_path(s3_uri)))\n219\t\n220\t    def _cache_dir_bytes(self) -> int:\n221\t        try:\n222\t            with os.scandir(self.local_cache_dir) as it:\n223\t                return sum(e.stat().st_size for e in it if e.is_file())\n224\t        except FileNotFoundError:\n225\t            return 0\n226\t\n227\t    def _fetch_to_memory(self, s3_uri: str) -> io.BytesIO:\n228\t        \"\"\"Fetch a whole S3 object into a ``BytesIO``, under a wall-clock deadline per attempt.\n229\t\n230\t        The deadline is a watchdog *around* boto3, not a replacement for the socket timeouts\n231\t        set in ``_get_client()``. Both are needed: the socket timeouts bound an individual\n232\t        request, and this bounds the whole multipart transfer, including boto3's own internal\n233\t        retry loop. Without the outer bound one unlucky object can stall a worker \u2014 and with\n234\t        ``prefetch_factor=1`` a stalled worker stalls the run.\n235\t\n236\t        A timed-out attempt is abandoned rather than cancelled (boto3 offers no cancellation):\n237\t        the buffer is closed and the client's connection pool torn down, which makes the\n238\t        orphaned daemon thread fail on its next write instead of lingering with a socket and a\n239\t        0.59 GB buffer.\n240\t        \"\"\"\n241\t        if TransferConfig is None:\n242\t            raise ImportError(\"boto3 is required for the in-memory read path.\")\n243\t        bucket, key = parse_s3_uri(s3_uri)\n244\t\n245\t        last_error: BaseException | None = None\n246\t        for attempt in range(1, self.fetch_attempts + 1):\n247\t            buf = io.BytesIO()\n248\t            outcome: dict = {}\n249\t\n250\t            def _download() -> None:\n251\t                try:\n252\t                    self._get_client().download_fileobj(\n253\t                        bucket, key, buf, Config=self._transfer_config())\n254\t                    outcome[\"ok\"] = True\n255\t                except BaseException as exc:  # reported to the caller below\n256\t                    outcome[\"error\"] = exc\n257\t\n258\t            thread = threading.Thread(target=_download, daemon=True)\n259\t            thread.start()\n260\t            thread.join(self.fetch_timeout_s)\n261\t\n262\t            if outcome.get(\"ok\"):\n263\t                buf.seek(0)\n264\t                return buf\n265\t\n266\t            if thread.is_alive():\n267\t                last_error = TimeoutError(\n268\t                    f\"{s3_uri} did not finish within {self.fetch_timeout_s}s \"\n269\t                    f\"(attempt {attempt}/{self.fetch_attempts})\")\n270\t            else:\n271\t                last_error = outcome.get(\"error\", RuntimeError(f\"unknown failure on {s3_uri}\"))\n272\t            self.logger.warning(f\"Fetch failed, retrying: {last_error}\")\n273\t            print(f\"[S3] retry {attempt}/{self.fetch_attempts}: {last_error}\", flush=True)\n274\t            self._reset_client()\n275\t            buf.close()\n276\t\n277\t        raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to fetch {s3_uri} after \"\n278\t                           f\"{self.fetch_attempts} attempts\") from last_error\n279\t\n280\t    def _maybe_write_cache(self, s3_uri: str, buf: io.BytesIO) -> None:\n281\t        \"\"\"Persist a whitelisted frame to fast local storage, budget permitting.\"\"\"\n282\t        if not self.local_cache_dir or s3_uri not in self.cache_paths:\n283\t            return\n284\t        path = self._local_cache_path(s3_uri)\n285\t        if os.path.exists(path):\n286\t            return\n287\t        size = buf.getbuffer().nbytes\n288\t        if self._cache_dir_bytes() + size > self.local_cache_budget_bytes:\n289\t            return  # silently stream instead; the budget is a ceiling, not a target\n290\t        tmp = f\"{path}.{os.getpid()}.{uuid4().hex}.partial\"\n291\t        try:\n292\t            with open(tmp, \"wb\") as fh:\n293\t                fh.write(buf.getbuffer())\n294\t            os.replace(tmp, path)  # atomic: a losing racer never publishes a truncated file\n295\t        except BaseException:\n296\t            if os.path.exists(tmp):\n297\t                os.remove(tmp)\n298\t            raise\n299\t\n300\t    def load_nc_data(self, filepath: str, timestep, channels: list[str]) -> np.ndarray:\n301\t        \"\"\"Return ``(C, H, W)`` for ``channels``, reading S3 objects through RAM.\n302\t\n303\t        Falls back to the parent implementation for local paths, so an index of local\n304\t        NetCDF files behaves exactly as before.\n305\t        \"\"\"\n306\t        self._ensure_logger()\n307\t\n308\t        if not self._is_s3_path(filepath):\n309\t            return super().load_nc_data(filepath, timestep, channels)\n310\t\n311\t        cached = self._local_cache_path(filepath) if self.local_cache_dir else None\n312\t        if cached and os.path.exists(cached):\n313\t            self.logger.info(f\"Reading local cache {cached}.\")\n314\t            with xr.open_dataset(cached, engine=\"h5netcdf\", chunks=None, cache=False) as ds:\n315\t                return ds[channels].to_array().load().to_numpy()\n316\t\n317\t        self.logger.info(f\"Fetching {filepath} into memory.\")\n318\t        buf = self._fetch_to_memory(filepath)\n319\t        try:\n320\t            self._maybe_write_cache(filepath, buf)\n321\t            buf.seek(0)\n322\t            with xr.open_dataset(buf, engine=\"h5netcdf\", chunks=None, cache=False) as ds:\n323\t                return ds[channels].to_array().load().to_numpy()\n324\t        finally:\n325\t            buf.close()  # release the 0.59 GB before the next frame is fetched\n326\t",
            "kind": "read",
            "locations": [
