            "raw_output": "1\t\"\"\"\n2\tDataset and DataLoader construction shared by every Surya downstream app.\n3\t\n4\t``HelioNetCDFDataset`` takes ~20 constructor arguments, all of which come straight from\n5\tthe ``data:`` and ``training:`` sections of the config. Spelling that block out at every\n6\tentry point means the same 20 lines get copied into every notebook and every training\n7\tscript, and they drift: one copy forgets ``phase=\"val\"``, another hardcodes a flag the\n8\tYAML was supposed to control.\n9\t\n10\tThese builders own that block once. A downstream app supplies its dataset subclass and\n11\twhatever task-specific keyword arguments that subclass adds::\n12\t\n13\t    train_loader, val_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\n14\t        cfg,\n15\t        FlareDSDataset,\n16\t        ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path,\n17\t        label_transform=my_label_transform,\n18\t    )\n19\t\"\"\"\n20\t\n21\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n22\t\n23\timport random\n24\tfrom functools import partial\n25\tfrom typing import Any, Tuple, Type\n26\t\n27\timport numpy as np\n28\timport torch\n29\tfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader, Dataset\n30\t\n31\tfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers\n32\t\n33\t\n34\tdef _seed_worker(worker_id: int, base_seed: int) -> None:\n35\t    \"\"\"Seed every RNG a DataLoader worker might draw from.\n36\t\n37\t    Seeding torch alone is not enough: ``RandomChannelMaskerTransform`` in\n38\t    ``workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`` draws from Python's ``random``\n39\t    (``random.sample`` / ``random.random``), and downstream datasets commonly use NumPy.\n40\t\n41\t    Supplying this deliberately displaces Lightning's auto-injected\n42\t    ``pl_worker_init_function``, which derives worker seeds from ``torch.initial_seed()``\n43\t    \u2014 i.e. from the ambient global RNG state, which is exactly the coupling we are\n44\t    removing here.\n45\t    \"\"\"\n46\t    seed = (base_seed + worker_id) % (2 ** 32)\n47\t    random.seed(seed)\n48\t    np.random.seed(seed)\n49\t    torch.manual_seed(seed)\n50\t\n51\t\n52\tdef _base_dataset_kwargs(cfg, scalers) -> dict:\n53\t    \"\"\"Map a TrainingConfig onto the HelioNetCDFDataset constructor arguments.\n54\t\n55\t    This is the single place where config field names meet dataset parameter names.\n56\t    \"\"\"\n57\t    return dict(\n58\t        # Temporal sampling\n59\t        time_delta_input_minutes=cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes,\n60\t        time_delta_target_minutes=cfg.data.time_delta_target_minutes,\n61\t        n_input_timestamps=cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim,\n62\t        rollout_steps=cfg.rollout_steps,\n63\t        # Channels and normalization\n64\t        channels=cfg.data.channels,\n65\t        scalers=scalers,\n66\t        # Augmentation\n67\t        drop_hmi_probability=cfg.drop_hmi_probability,\n68\t        use_latitude_in_learned_flow=cfg.use_latitude_in_learned_flow,\n69\t        # Storage: local root, and how s3:// paths in the index are read\n70\t        sdo_data_root_path=cfg.data.sdo_data_root_path,\n71\t        s3_mode=cfg.data.s3_mode,\n72\t        s3_storage_options={\"anon\": cfg.data.s3_anon},\n73\t        s3_cache_dir=cfg.data.s3_cache_dir,\n74\t        s3_boto3_max_concurrency=cfg.data.s3_boto3_max_concurrency,\n75\t        s3_boto3_part_size_mb=cfg.data.s3_boto3_part_size_mb,\n76\t    )\n77\t\n78\t\n79\tdef build_helio_datasets(\n80\t    cfg,\n81\t    dataset_cls: Type[Dataset],\n82\t    scalers: Any = None,\n83\t    **task_kwargs,\n84\t) -> Tuple[Dataset, Dataset]:\n85\t    \"\"\"Build the train and validation datasets described by ``cfg``.\n86\t\n87\t    Args:\n88\t        cfg: A ``TrainingConfig`` from ``load_config()``.\n89\t        dataset_cls: ``HelioNetCDFDataset`` or a subclass of it.\n90\t        scalers: Normalization statistics. Built from ``cfg.data.scalers_path`` if omitted;\n91\t            pass an existing dict to avoid re-reading the YAML.\n92\t        **task_kwargs: Extra keyword arguments forwarded to ``dataset_cls`` \u2014 the\n93\t            task-specific parameters your subclass adds.\n94\t\n95\t    Returns:\n96\t        ``(train_dataset, val_dataset)``. They differ only in the index they read and in\n97\t        ``phase``: the validation set uses ``phase=\"val\"``, which disables the random\n98\t        channel masking and vertical flips that are applied during training.\n99\t    \"\"\"\n100\t    if scalers is None:\n101\t        scalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\n102\t\n103\t    common = {**_base_dataset_kwargs(cfg, scalers), **task_kwargs}\n104\t\n105\t    train_dataset = dataset_cls(index_path=cfg.data.train_data_path, phase=\"train\", **common)\n106\t    val_dataset = dataset_cls(index_path=cfg.data.valid_data_path, phase=\"val\", **common)\n107\t    return train_dataset, val_dataset\n108\t\n109\t\n110\tdef build_helio_dataloaders(\n111\t    cfg,\n112\t    dataset_cls: Type[Dataset],\n113\t    scalers: Any = None,\n114\t    num_workers: int | None = None,\n115\t    seed: int | None = None,\n116\t    **task_kwargs,\n117\t) -> Tuple[DataLoader, DataLoader]:\n118\t    \"\"\"Build the train and validation DataLoaders described by ``cfg``.\n119\t\n120\t    Args:\n121\t        cfg: A ``TrainingConfig`` from ``load_config()``.\n122\t        dataset_cls: ``HelioNetCDFDataset`` or a subclass of it.\n123\t        scalers: Normalization statistics; built from the config if omitted.\n124\t        num_workers: Override for ``cfg.num_workers`` (useful in notebooks, where fewer\n125\t            workers start faster).\n126\t        seed: Seed for the shuffle order and the per-worker RNGs. Defaults to ``cfg.seed``.\n127\t            Pinning it explicitly is what makes the epoch order depend only on the config:\n128\t            a bare ``shuffle=True`` seeds its sampler from whatever the global torch RNG\n129\t            state happens to be when the iterator is created, so anything that consumes\n130\t            RNG earlier in the program silently reshuffles the data.\n131\t        **task_kwargs: Extra keyword arguments forwarded to ``dataset_cls``.\n132\t\n133\t    Returns:\n134\t        ``(train_loader, val_loader)``. Only the training loader shuffles.\n135\t    \"\"\"\n136\t    train_dataset, val_dataset = build_helio_datasets(\n137\t        cfg, dataset_cls, scalers=scalers, **task_kwargs\n138\t    )\n139\t\n140\t    workers = cfg.num_workers if num_workers is None else num_workers\n141\t    base_seed = cfg.seed if seed is None else seed\n142\t\n143\t    loader_kwargs = dict(\n144\t        batch_size=cfg.batch_size,\n145\t        num_workers=workers,\n146\t        pin_memory=True,\n147\t        drop_last=True,\n148\t    )\n149\t    if workers > 0:\n150\t        # \"spawn\": the dataset holds an s3fs/boto3 handle that does not survive fork.\n151\t        # Both of these are rejected outright when num_workers == 0.\n152\t        loader_kwargs[\"multiprocessing_context\"] = \"spawn\"\n153\t        loader_kwargs[\"persistent_workers\"] = True\n154\t        loader_kwargs[\"worker_init_fn\"] = partial(_seed_worker, base_seed=base_seed)\n155\t\n156\t    # An explicit generator makes the shuffle a function of the seed alone, rather than\n157\t    # of the global RNG state at the moment the iterator happens to be created.\n158\t    shuffle_generator = torch.Generator()\n159\t    shuffle_generator.manual_seed(base_seed)\n160\t\n161\t    train_loader = DataLoader(\n162\t        train_dataset, shuffle=True, generator=shuffle_generator, **loader_kwargs\n163\t    )\n164\t    # No generator for validation: it is not shuffled, so there is nothing to seed.\n165\t    val_loader = DataLoader(val_dataset, shuffle=False, **loader_kwargs)\n166\t    return train_loader, val_loader\n167\t",
            "status": "completed",
            "raw_input": {
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop2026/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py"
            },
            "kind": "read",
            "title": "Read builders.py"
          },
          {
            "title": "python -c \" import json nb=json.load(open('2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb')) for i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']): src=''.join(c['source']) print('='*70) print('IDX',i,'|',c['cell_type'],'| exec_count=',c.get('execution_count')) if c['cell_type']=='code': print(src) else: print('\\n'.join(src.split('\\n')[:2])) \"",
            "status": "completed",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01C65Greah9axsFSDRhduvBn",
            "raw_output": "======================================================================\nIDX 0 | markdown | exec_count= None\n# PyTorch Lightning fine-tuning template\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 1 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Set your cuda visible device\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 2 | code | exec_count= 1\nimport os\nos.environ[\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = \"0\"\n======================================================================\nIDX 3 | code | exec_count= 2\n# Must be set BEFORE torch is imported: cuBLAS reads this once, when it initializes, so\n# setting it later has no effect. It is what lets training.deterministic work without a\n# cuBLAS warning on every run. (Restart the kernel if torch was already imported.)\nimport os\nos.environ.setdefault(\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\", \":4096:8\")\n\nimport sys\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\n\nimport torch\nimport yaml\n\nimport lightning as L\nfrom lightning.pytorch.callbacks import ModelCheckpoint\nfrom lightning.pytorch.loggers import CSVLogger, WandbLogger\n\n# Append base path.  May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to the wokshop_infrastructure folder.\nsys.path.append(\"../../\")\n \n# Append Surya path. May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to surya's release code.\n\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers  # Data scaling utilities for Surya stacks\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import apply_peft_lora\ntorch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')\n\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 4 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Load configuration\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 5 | code | exec_count= 3\n# The config is the single source of truth. load_wave_config() parses it into a typed\n# object, exactly as the training script 4_finetune_wave_1D.py does, so the same YAML\n# behaves identically here and in production. Notebook 0 walks through what it contains.\nfrom downstream_apps.test.configs import load_wave_config\n\ncfg = load_wave_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")\nprint(f\"Loaded config for job: {cfg.job_id}\")\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 6 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Download assets\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 7 | code | exec_count= 4\n# One implementation, shared by the notebooks, the training script and the\n# download_*.sh wrappers: workshop_infrastructure/assets.py.\n# Fine-tuning needs the pretrained backbone as well (~1.8 GB).\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.assets import ensure_assets\n\nensure_assets(cfg, which=[\"scalers\", \"weights\"])\n\n# Now that scalers.yaml is guaranteed to be on disk, load it. build_scalers()\n# accepts the resolved path directly.\nscalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\nprint(f\"Loaded scalers for {len(scalers)} channels.\")\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 8 | markdown | exec_count= None\n### Typed configuration, and how to extend it for your own task\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 9 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Define Downstream (DS) datasets\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 10 | code | exec_count= 5\nfrom downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset\n======================================================================\nIDX 11 | code | exec_count= 6\n# build_helio_dataloaders() constructs the train and validation datasets and wraps them\n# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\n# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\n# like written out. Only the wave-specific arguments are passed here, which is exactly\n# the list you replace when you fork the template.\n#\n# It also handles two details that are easy to get wrong by hand: the validation set gets\n# phase=\"val\" (no random channel masking or flips), and only the training loader shuffles.\n#\n# One thing it does NOT expose is prefetch_factor, and it sets drop_last=True for the\n# validation loader too. Neither matters at max_samples: 10, but both do at full scale --\n# see experiments/wave_common.py:build_wave_loader() for why and what to do instead.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_dataloaders\nimport numpy as np\ntrain_data_loader, val_data_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\n    cfg,\n    waveDSDataset,\n    scalers=scalers,\n    num_workers=4,          # fewer workers than the script: notebooks start faster\n    #### Downstream (DS) specific parameters\n    return_surya_stack=True,\n    # From the config, not a literal: data.max_samples is the one knob for dataset size,\n    # so the notebook and 4_finetune_wave_1D.py cannot disagree about how much data ran.\n    max_number_of_samples=cfg.data.max_samples,\n    ds_wave_index_path=cfg.data.wave_index_path,\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n    ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column,\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n    label_transform=lambda s: (s == \"wave\").astype(np.float32),\n)\n\nbatch_size = cfg.batch_size\nprint(f\"train: {len(train_data_loader.dataset)} samples | \"\n      f\"val: {len(val_data_loader.dataset)} samples | batch_size: {batch_size}\")\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 12 | markdown | exec_count= None\nTraining and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the index they read and in `phase`: `phase=\"val\"` turns off the random channel masking and vertical flips used for training augmentation.\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 13 | code | exec_count= 7\n# Inspect a single batch to confirm shapes before building the model.\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\nprint({k: (tuple(v.shape) if hasattr(v, \"shape\") else type(v).__name__) for k, v in batch.items()})\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 14 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Initialize the HelioSpectformer model\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 15 | code | exec_count= 8\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.models.finetune_models import HelioSpectformer1D\n======================================================================\nIDX 16 | markdown | exec_count= None\nNow the config file really comes into bear. The Spectformer has a metric ton of hyperparameters\n======================================================================\nIDX 17 | code | exec_count= 9\n# HelioSpectformer1D has a long list of architecture arguments, and all of them come\n# straight from the model: section of the config. from_config() does that mapping, so the\n# backbone can never drift out of sync with the checkpoint it is about to load.\n#\n# Arguments that are not part of ModelConfig (dtype, and anything from the training:\n# section) are passed as explicit overrides.\nmodel = HelioSpectformer1D.from_config(\n    cfg.model,\n    num_outputs=1,\n    dtype=cfg.dtype,\n    use_latitude_in_learned_flow=cfg.use_latitude_in_learned_flow,\n)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 18 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Load model weights\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 19 | code | exec_count= 10\n# The checkpoint was saved from HelioSpectFormer directly, so its keys are flat\n# (e.g. \"embedding.proj.weight\"), while the fine-tuning model nests the backbone under\n# \"backbone.*\". load_pretrained_weights() tries both spellings and reports how many\n# tensors matched \u2014 a low count means the architecture does not match the checkpoint.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import load_pretrained_weights\n\nload_pretrained_weights(model, cfg.model.pretrained_path)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 20 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## To LoRA or not to Lora\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 21 | code | exec_count= 11\n# Three fine-tuning regimes, all selected from the model: section of the config:\n#\n#   use_lora: true                          -> LoRA adapters + the whole head (default)\n#   use_lora: false, freeze_backbone: true  -> linear probe: only the head trains\n#   use_lora: false, freeze_backbone: false -> full fine-tuning of all 366M parameters\n#\n# freeze_backbone is ignored when use_lora is true: PEFT freezes everything, then\n# re-enables the adapters and every head_* module.\n#\n# 3_finetune_template_1D.py applies exactly this logic in build_model().\nif cfg.model.freeze_backbone:\n    for name, param in model.named_parameters():\n        if name.startswith(\"backbone.\"):\n            param.requires_grad = False\n\nif cfg.model.use_lora:\n    # Prints the adapted modules and the trainable head modules it discovered.\n    model = apply_peft_lora(model, cfg.model.lora_config)\n\ntrainable = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\ntotal = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())\nprint(f\"Trainable parameters: {trainable:,} / {total:,} ({100 * trainable / total:.2f}%)\")\n======================================================================\nIDX 22 | markdown | exec_count= None\nWe can now test that this model manipulates a batch as expected and returns one number per sample, as we did for the simple baseline.\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 23 | code | exec_count= 12\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\noutput = model.forward(batch)  # Get rid of singleton dimension\noutput\n======================================================================\nIDX 24 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Define your metrics\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 25 | code | exec_count= 13\nfrom downstream_apps.test.metrics.template_metrics import WaveMetrics\n======================================================================\nIDX 26 | code | exec_count= 14\ntrain_loss_metrics = WaveMetrics(\"train_loss\")\n# val_loss is the quantity logged as \"val_loss\" and used to pick the best checkpoint.\n# It defaults to the same BCE-with-logits as train_loss \u2014 override WaveMetrics.val_loss\n# to monitor something else.\nval_loss_metrics = WaveMetrics(\"val_loss\")\ntrain_evaluation_metrics = WaveMetrics(\"train_metrics\")\n# Reported only: val_metrics do NOT influence checkpoint selection.\nvalidation_evaluation_metrics = WaveMetrics(\"val_metrics\")\n======================================================================\nIDX 27 | markdown | exec_count= None\nNow they can be evaluated on our model's output and our ground truth.   First the loss that actually will backpropagate, in this case **binary cross-entropy with logits**.\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 28 | code | exec_count= 15\ntrain_loss_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])\n======================================================================\nIDX 29 | markdown | exec_count= None\nThen a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we can keep an eye on. Note that reporting lots of metrics during training will slow the training process.  I'm including it her as an example, but oftentimes is better to put the diagnostics only in the validation evaluation metrics.\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 30 | code | exec_count= 16\ntrain_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])\n======================================================================\nIDX 31 | markdown | exec_count= None\nIn the validation evaluation metrics we report both accuracy and **AUROC**.\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 32 | code | exec_count= 17\nvalidation_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])\n======================================================================\nIDX 33 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Define your PyTorch ligthning module\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 34 | code | exec_count= 18\nfrom downstream_apps.test.lightning_modules.pl_simple_baseline import WaveLightningModule\n======================================================================\nIDX 35 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Set your global seeds\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 36 | code | exec_count= 19\nL.seed_everything(42, workers=True)\n======================================================================\nIDX 37 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Intialize Lightning module\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 38 | code | exec_count= 20\nmetrics = {\n    'train_loss': train_loss_metrics,\n    'val_loss': val_loss_metrics,\n    'train_metrics': train_evaluation_metrics,\n    'val_metrics': validation_evaluation_metrics,\n}\n\nlit_model = WaveLightningModule(model, metrics, lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 39 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Logging\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 40 | code | exec_count= 21\nproject_name = cfg.wandb_project\nrun_name = \"finetune_experiment_1\"  # give your run a descriptive name\n\nwandb_logger = WandbLogger(\n    entity=cfg.wandb_entity,  # set wandb_entity in the config; null = personal account\n    project=project_name,\n    name=run_name,\n    log_model=False,\n    save_dir=\"./wandb/wandb_tmp\",\n)\n\ncsv_logger = CSVLogger(\"runs\", name=project_name)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 41 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Initialize trainer\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 42 | code | exec_count= 22\n#max_epochs = 4\nmax_epochs = cfg.max_epochs\n\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Trainer\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntrainer = L.Trainer(\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\n    accelerator=\"auto\",\n    devices=\"auto\",\n    precision=\"bf16-mixed\",\n    # Surya diverged outright once at lr=0.01 (losses of 10^2-10^4, exact 0.0 values under\n    # bf16, val accuracy pinned at 0.5). Clipping costs nothing when gradients are small and\n    # stops one bad batch taking the run with it. 4_finetune_wave_1D.py sets the same value.\n    gradient_clip_val=1.0,\n    logger=[wandb_logger, csv_logger],\n    callbacks=[\n        ModelCheckpoint(\n            monitor=\"val_loss\",\n            mode=\"min\",\n            save_top_k=1,\n        )\n    ],\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\n)\n======================================================================\nIDX 43 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Fit the model\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 44 | code | exec_count= None\ntrainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\n======================================================================\nIDX 45 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Close the WandB run\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 46 | code | exec_count= None\nimport wandb\n\nif wandb.run is not None:\n    wandb.finish()\n    print(\"wandb run closed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"no active wandb run\")\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 47 | markdown | exec_count= None\n## Conclusion",
            "kind": "execute",
            "raw_input": {
              "command": "python -c \"\nimport json\nnb=json.load(open('2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb'))\nfor i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']):\n    src=''.join(c['source'])\n    print('='*70)\n    print('IDX',i,'|',c['cell_type'],'| exec_count=',c.get('execution_count'))\n    if c['cell_type']=='code':\n        print(src)\n    else:\n        print('\\n'.join(src.split('\\n')[:2]))\n\"",
              "description": "Dump notebook 2 structure"
            }
          },
          {
            "raw_output": "======================================================================\nIDX 0 | markdown | exec= None\n# Two trainable baselines for wave classification\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 1 | markdown | exec= None\n## Set your cuda visible device\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 2 | code | exec= 1\nimport os\nos.environ[\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = \"0\"\n======================================================================\nIDX 3 | code | exec= 2\n# Must be set BEFORE torch is imported: cuBLAS reads this once, when it initializes, so\n# setting it later has no effect. It is what lets training.deterministic work without a\n# cuBLAS warning on every run. (Restart the kernel if torch was already imported.)\nimport os\nos.environ.setdefault(\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\", \":4096:8\")\n\nimport sys\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nimport yaml\n\nimport lightning as L\nfrom lightning.pytorch.callbacks import ModelCheckpoint\nfrom lightning.pytorch.loggers import CSVLogger, WandbLogger\n\n# Append base path.  May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to the wokshop_infrastructure folder.\nsys.path.append(\"../../\")\n \n# Append Surya path. May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to surya's release code.\n\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers  # Data scaling utilities for Surya stacks\n\ntorch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 4 | markdown | exec= None\n## Load configuration\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 5 | code | exec= 30\n# The config is the single source of truth. load_wave_config() parses it into a typed\n# object, exactly as the training script 3_finetune_template_1D.py does, so the same YAML\n# behaves identically here and in production. Notebook 0 walks through what it contains.\nfrom downstream_apps.test.configs import load_wave_config\n\ncfg = load_wave_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")\nprint(f\"Loaded config for job: {cfg.job_id}\")\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 6 | markdown | exec= None\n## Download assets\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 7 | code | exec= 5\n# One implementation, shared by the notebooks, the training script and the\n# download_*.sh wrappers: workshop_infrastructure/assets.py.\n# The linear baseline needs no backbone, so skip the 1.8 GB weights.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.assets import ensure_assets\n\nensure_assets(cfg, which=[\"scalers\"])\n\n# Now that scalers.yaml is guaranteed to be on disk, load it. build_scalers()\n# accepts the resolved path directly.\nscalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\nprint(f\"Loaded scalers for {len(scalers)} channels.\")\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 8 | markdown | exec= None\n## Define Downstream (DS) dataset\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 9 | code | exec= 6\nfrom downstream_apps.test.datasets.wave_dataset import waveDSDataset\n======================================================================\nIDX 10 | code | exec= 7\n# build_helio_dataloaders() constructs the train and validation datasets and wraps them\n# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\n# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\n# like written out. Only the wave-specific arguments are passed here.\n#\n# time_delta_input_minutes / n_input_timestamps are overridden here to 2 timesteps\n# (t_prev, t_now) for the running-difference baseline below. task_kwargs override the\n# config-derived defaults inside build_helio_dataloaders(), so this only affects this\n# dataset build \u2014 cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim stays 1 in the YAML, unaffected, for\n# the later full-Surya fine-tuning run that shares this same config file.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_dataloaders\n\ntrain_data_loader, val_data_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\n    cfg,\n    waveDSDataset,\n    scalers=scalers,\n    # num_workers=0: any num_workers > 0 makes build_helio_dataloaders use\n    # multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\" (needed because the dataset's S3 client doesn't\n    # survive fork). Spawned workers need to re-import a __main__ script, which doesn't\n    # exist cleanly under a Jupyter kernel, so they crash on startup here (\"DataLoader\n    # worker exited unexpectedly\"). 0 keeps loading in-process \u2014 fine for 10 samples.\n    num_workers=0,\n    #### Downstream (DS) specific parameters\n    return_surya_stack=True,\n    max_number_of_samples=cfg.data.max_samples,\n    time_delta_input_minutes=[-12, 0],   # t_prev (one AIA cadence step earlier), t_now\n    n_input_timestamps=2,\n    ds_class_column=cfg.data.ds_class_column,\n    label_transform=lambda s: (s == \"wave\").astype(np.float32),\n    ds_wave_index_path=cfg.data.wave_index_path,\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n)\n\nbatch_size = cfg.batch_size\nprint(f\"train: {len(train_data_loader.dataset)} samples | \"\n      f\"val: {len(val_data_loader.dataset)} samples | batch_size: {batch_size}\")\n======================================================================\nIDX 11 | markdown | exec= None\nTraining and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the index they read and in `phase`: `phase=\"val\"` turns off the random channel masking and vertical flips used for training augmentation.\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 12 | code | exec= 8\nprint(\"kernel alive check\")\n======================================================================\nIDX 13 | code | exec= 9\n# Inspect a single batch to confirm shapes before training.\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\nprint({k: (tuple(v.shape) if hasattr(v, \"shape\") else type(v).__name__) for k, v in batch.items()})\n\n# Derive model dimensions directly from the batch/config rather than hardcoding them.\nn_channels = len(cfg.data.channels)\naia193_index = cfg.data.channels.index(\"aia193\")\nimg_size = batch[\"ts\"].shape[-1]\nprint(f\"n_channels={n_channels} | aia193_index={aia193_index} | img_size={img_size}\")\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 14 | markdown | exec= None\n## Define simple baseline models\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 15 | code | exec= 10\nfrom downstream_apps.test.models.simple_baseline import (\n    ConstantProbabilityModel,\n    RunningDifferenceLogisticModel,\n    destandardize_channels,\n)\n======================================================================\nIDX 16 | markdown | exec= None\nWe can now test that both models manipulate a batch as expected and return a wave\nprobability logit.\n======================================================================\nIDX 17 | code | exec= 32\n# Baseline 1: a single learned logit, independent of the input.\nmodel_constant = ConstantProbabilityModel()\n\n# Baseline 2: AIA193 running difference, 32x32 mean-pooled, then logistic regression.\n# It expects 'ts' in signum-log space \u2014 use destandardize_channels() before calling forward().\nmodel_running_diff = RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(channel_index=aia193_index, img_size=img_size)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 18 | markdown | exec= None\nNow we can pass a batch to each model to confirm it returns one logit per sample. Note\nthat our outputs have the size of our batch.\n======================================================================\nIDX 19 | code | exec= 12\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\n\n# RunningDifferenceLogisticModel works in signum-log space, not normalized space.\n# destandardize_channels undoes the per-channel z-score but KEEPS the log compression \u2014\n# raw DN values span too many orders of magnitude to be good features for one linear layer.\n# (For true physical units, use train_dataset.inverse_transform_data() instead. See the\n#  \"THE THREE SPACES\" block in workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py.)\nbatch_logspace = destandardize_channels(batch, channel_order=cfg.data.channels, scalers=scalers)\n\noutput_constant = model_constant.forward(batch)[:, 0]              # ignores 'ts' entirely\noutput_running_diff = model_running_diff.forward(batch_logspace)[:, 0]\n\nprint(\"constant baseline output (logits):\", output_constant)\nprint(\"running-difference baseline output (logits):\", output_running_diff)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 20 | markdown | exec= None\n## Define your metrics\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 21 | code | exec= 13\nfrom downstream_apps.test.metrics.template_metrics import WaveMetrics\n======================================================================\nIDX 22 | code | exec= 14\ndef make_metrics() -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Build a fresh dict of WaveMetrics instances.\n\n    Each of the two baselines below needs its own dict: the accuracy/AUROC torchmetrics\n    objects inside WaveMetrics accumulate internal state across calls, so sharing one\n    instance between two independently-trained models would mix their statistics.\n    \"\"\"\n    return {\n        'train_loss': WaveMetrics(\"train_loss\"),\n        # val_loss is the quantity logged as \"val_loss\" and used to pick the best checkpoint.\n        # It defaults to the same BCE as train_loss \u2014 override WaveMetrics.val_loss to change it.\n        'val_loss': WaveMetrics(\"val_loss\"),\n        'train_metrics': WaveMetrics(\"train_metrics\"),\n        # Reported only: val_metrics do NOT influence checkpoint selection.\n        'val_metrics': WaveMetrics(\"val_metrics\"),\n    }\n\ndemo_metrics = make_metrics()\n======================================================================\nIDX 23 | markdown | exec= None\nNow they can be evaluated on a model's output and our ground truth. First the loss that\nactually will backpropagate \u2014 binary cross-entropy with logits \u2014 demonstrated here on the\n======================================================================\nIDX 24 | code | exec= 15\ndemo_metrics[\"train_loss\"](output_running_diff, batch[\"forecast\"])\n======================================================================\nIDX 25 | markdown | exec= None\nThen a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we\ncan keep an eye on. Note that reporting lots of metrics during training will slow the\n======================================================================\nIDX 26 | code | exec= 16\ndemo_metrics[\"train_metrics\"](output_running_diff, batch[\"forecast\"])\n======================================================================\nIDX 27 | markdown | exec= None\nIn the validation evaluation metrics we report both accuracy and AUROC.\n======================================================================\nIDX 28 | code | exec= 17\ndemo_metrics[\"val_metrics\"](output_running_diff, batch[\"forecast\"])\n======================================================================\nIDX 29 | markdown | exec= None\n## Define your PyTorch ligthning module\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 30 | code | exec= 18\nfrom downstream_apps.test.lightning_modules.pl_simple_baseline import WaveLightningModule\n======================================================================\nIDX 31 | markdown | exec= None\n## Set your global seeds\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 32 | code | exec= 31\nL.seed_everything(42, workers=True)\n======================================================================\nIDX 33 | markdown | exec= None\n## Intialize Lightning modules\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 34 | code | exec= 33\nfrom functools import partial\n\n# Baseline 1: constant probability \u2014 never touches 'ts', so no preprocess_fn needed.\nlit_model_constant = WaveLightningModule(\n    model_constant, make_metrics(), lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size,\n)\n\n# Baseline 2: running difference \u2014 needs the signum-log-space inverse transform wired up\n# so WaveLightningModule applies it before every model call.\npreprocess_fn = partial(\n    destandardize_channels,\n    channel_order=cfg.data.channels,\n    scalers=scalers,\n)\nlit_model_running_diff = WaveLightningModule(\n    model_running_diff, make_metrics(), lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size,\n    preprocess_fn=preprocess_fn,\n)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 35 | markdown | exec= None\n## Logging\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 36 | code | exec= 25\n# WandB is now authenticated via `wandb login` (stored in ~/.netrc as lloverasdiego /\n# surya-ws2), so WandbLogger below logs live \u2014 no offline/disabled override needed.\n======================================================================\nIDX 37 | code | exec= 21\nproject_name = cfg.wandb_project\ndef make_loggers(run_name: str) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Build a fresh WandB + CSV logger pair for one baseline's run.\"\"\"\n    wandb_logger = WandbLogger(\n        entity=cfg.wandb_entity,  # set wandb_entity in the config; null = personal account\n        project=project_name,\n        name=run_name,\n        log_model=False,\n        save_dir=\"./wandb/wandb_tmp\",\n    )\n    csv_logger = CSVLogger(\"runs\", name=f\"{project_name}_{run_name}\")\n    return [wandb_logger, csv_logger]\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 38 | markdown | exec= None\n## Initialize trainer\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 39 | code | exec= 34\n#max_epochs = 4  # previous value: hardcoded, ignored cfg.max_epochs entirely\nmax_epochs = cfg.max_epochs  # now driven by training.max_epochs in config_script.yaml\n\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Trainer \u2014 one per baseline, so each gets its own checkpoint/logger state and their\n# validation metrics are directly comparable side by side.\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntrainer_constant = L.Trainer(\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\n    accelerator=\"auto\",\n    devices=\"auto\",\n    logger=make_loggers(\"baseline_constant_prob\"),\n    callbacks=[ModelCheckpoint(monitor=\"val_loss\", mode=\"min\", save_top_k=1)],\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\n)\n\ntrainer_running_diff = L.Trainer(\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\n    accelerator=\"auto\",\n    devices=\"auto\",\n    logger=make_loggers(\"baseline_running_diff\"),\n    callbacks=[ModelCheckpoint(monitor=\"val_loss\", mode=\"min\", save_top_k=1)],\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\n)\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 40 | markdown | exec= None\n## Fit the models\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 41 | code | exec= 38\n#trainer_constant.fit(lit_model_constant, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\n\n\n======================================================================\nIDX 42 | code | exec= 29\nwandb.finish()\n======================================================================\nIDX 43 | code | exec= 23\ncfg.wandb_entity = \"lloverasdiego-grupo-de-estudios-en-heliofisica-de-mendoza\"\nprint(f\"wandb_entity now: {cfg.wandb_entity}\")\n======================================================================\nIDX 44 | code | exec= 24\ntrainer_running_diff.fit(lit_model_running_diff, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\n======================================================================\nIDX 45 | markdown | exec= None\n## Conclusion",
            "status": "completed",
            "raw_input": {
              "description": "Dump notebook 1 structure",
              "command": "python -c \"\nimport json\nnb=json.load(open('1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb'))\nfor i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']):\n    src=''.join(c['source'])\n    print('='*70)\n    print('IDX',i,'|',c['cell_type'],'| exec=',c.get('execution_count'))\n    if c['cell_type']=='code':\n        print(src)\n    else:\n        print('\\n'.join(src.split('\\n')[:2]))\n\""
            },
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_015Us6Bp4fRMfp3oGdqcvAnA",
            "title": "python -c \" import json nb=json.load(open('1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb')) for i,c in enumerate(nb['cells']): src=''.join(c['source']) print('='*70) print('IDX',i,'|',c['cell_type'],'| exec=',c.get('execution_count')) if c['cell_type']=='code': print(src) else: print('\\n'.join(src.split('\\n')[:2])) \"",
            "kind": "execute"
          },
          {
            "raw_input": {
              "description": "Look at experiments and configs dirs",
              "command": "ls experiments configs && echo \"=== wave_common ===\" && cat experiments/wave_common.py 2>/dev/null | head -120"
            },
            "raw_output": "configs:\nconfig_script.yaml\nconfig_wave_full.yaml\n\nexperiments:\nREADME.md\n__init__.py\n__pycache__\nextract_baseline_features.py\nplot_task_1_1.py\nresults\nrun_baseline_study.py\nrun_surya_schedule.sh\nsummarize_results.py\nuntitled.chat\nwave_common.py\nwave_visibility_scan.py\n=== wave_common ===\n\"\"\"\nShared helpers for the wave-classification experiments.\n\nThree things are needed by more than one script in this directory, so they live here\nonce rather than being copied (and drifting) across the feature extractor, the baseline\nstudy and the fine-tuning entry point:\n\n1. **Event-level stratified subsetting.** The catalog stores each event twice \u2014 once as\n   ``wave`` at ``start_time + 30 min`` and once as ``no wave`` at ``start_time - 30 min``\n   \u2014 so an event is a *matched pair* of samples. Subsetting by event therefore gives\n   exact class balance for free, and never puts the two halves of one event on opposite\n   sides of a split boundary. ``waveDSDataset``'s own ``max_number_of_samples`` cannot do\n   this: it head-slices a frame sorted by ``ds_index``, so ``max_number_of_samples=10``\n   returns the 5 *earliest* pairs (all June-July 2010), which is a chronological bias, not\n   a sample.\n\n2. **Pre-flight assertions.** Every failure mode this task has actually hit is silent:\n   a tokenizer that stays random because its shape disagreed with the checkpoint, LoRA\n   target names that match nothing, a head frozen at its initialization. Each one still\n   produces a plausible-looking loss curve. These helpers turn them into startup errors.\n\n3. **The running-difference feature.** ``RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`` computes it on\n   the GPU during training; ``extract_baseline_features.py`` computes it once and caches\n   it. ``pooled_running_difference()`` is the single definition both use, and\n   ``assert_feature_matches_model()`` checks they agree numerically.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport torch\n\n# Column in the wave catalog that identifies an *event*. Both the \"wave\" row\n# (mid_time = start_time + 30 min) and the \"no wave\" row (mid_time - 30 min) of one\n# event share it, which is what makes pair-level subsetting possible.\nEVENT_COL = \"start_time\"\nPOSITIVE_CLASS = \"wave\"\n\n\ndef wave_label_transform(class_series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    \"\"\"Map the catalog's ``class`` column to float32 1.0 / 0.0.\n\n    Passed to ``waveDSDataset(label_transform=...)``. Kept here so the notebooks, the\n    feature extractor and the training script cannot disagree on which class is positive.\n    \"\"\"\n    return (class_series == POSITIVE_CLASS).astype(np.float32)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Event-level subsetting\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n@dataclass\nclass SubsetInfo:\n    \"\"\"What a subsetting call actually did, for logging and assertions.\"\"\"\n    n_samples: int\n    n_events: int\n    n_positive: int\n    n_negative: int\n    n_dropped_unpaired: int\n    n_events_available: int\n\n    def describe(self) -> str:\n        return (\n            f\"{self.n_samples} samples / {self.n_events} events \"\n            f\"({self.n_positive} wave, {self.n_negative} no wave) \"\n            f\"from {self.n_events_available} complete pairs available; \"\n            f\"dropped {self.n_dropped_unpaired} unpaired sample(s)\"\n        )\n\n\ndef event_stratified_subset(\n    dataset,\n    n_samples: int | None = None,\n    seed: int = 42,\n) -> SubsetInfo:\n    \"\"\"Restrict ``dataset`` in place to a seeded, event-level stratified subset.\n\n    Only events whose *both* samples survived the Surya-index match are kept, so the\n    result is exactly 50/50 by construction and accuracy of 0.5 is exactly chance. On the\n    train split at ``ds_time_tolerance: 15min`` that costs 18 of 1210 matched samples and\n    buys perfect balance.\n\n    Args:\n        dataset: A ``waveDSDataset`` (already constructed, so the Surya-index match has\n            happened). Modified in place.\n        n_samples: Number of samples to keep \u2014 must be even, since events come in pairs.\n            ``None`` keeps every complete pair.\n        seed: Selects which events. Fixed across the runs of a scaling study so that the\n            N=48 subset is a subset of the N=96 subset is a subset of N=192, \u2026; the\n            learning curve then isolates sample count from which events were drawn.\n\n    Returns:\n        A ``SubsetInfo`` describing the selection.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: if ``n_samples`` is odd, or larger than the available paired samples.\n    \"\"\"\n    df = dataset.df_valid_indices\n    if EVENT_COL not in df.columns:\n        raise ValueError(\n            f\"The wave catalog must carry a {EVENT_COL!r} column to subset by event; \"\n            f\"got columns {sorted(df.columns)[:8]}\u2026\"\n        )\n\n    # Positional order of df_valid_indices matches dataset.valid_indices, and\n    # waveDSDataset.__getitem__ indexes both by position. Any mask must be applied to\n    # both, in the same order.\n    labels = df[\"label\"].to_numpy()\n    events = df[EVENT_COL].to_numpy()\n\n    per_event = pd.Series(labels).groupby(events).nunique()\n    complete = np.sort(per_event.index[per_event == 2].to_numpy())\n    n_dropped = int(len(labels) - np.isin(events, complete).sum())\n\n    if n_samples is None:",
            "title": "ls experiments configs && echo \"=== wave_common ===\" && cat experiments/wave_common.py 2>/dev/null | head -120",
