      "body": "Plan written. The headline findings, since two of them change the shape of the work:\n\n**Your rewrite of `simple_baseline.py` broke two callers.** `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel` no longer takes `img_size`, so notebook 1 cell 17 fails, and in `4_finetune_wave_1D.py:355` the construction is commented out \u2014 leaving `model` undefined, so the script's `--train_baseline` path raises `NameError`. That's why I'll fix the script alongside the notebook.\n\n**The dataset problem isn't that the two notebooks differ \u2014 it's that they're identically wrong.** Recon shows nb1 cell 10 and nb2 cell 11 are argument-for-argument equivalent apart from `num_workers`. But both inherit four defects from `build_helio_dataloaders`: `max_samples=10` head-slices chronologically (you get the 5 earliest pairs, all June\u2013July 2010, not a random sample); `drop_last=True` applies to the **validation** loader and silently drops a sample from the metric `ModelCheckpoint` selects on; `prefetch_factor` isn't exposed \u2014 the one knob that bounds worker RAM; and there's no test split. All four are already solved in `experiments/wave_common.py` (`build_wave_datasets`, `event_stratified_subset`, `build_wave_loader`) \u2014 the same functions `4_finetune_wave_1D.py` uses. So the fix is to put those in cell 10, which makes the notebook and script agree and lets nb2 use the identical cell. `config_script.yaml` needs no edit.\n\nA side benefit: the current `label_transform=lambda \u2026` can't survive `spawn`, so nb2's `num_workers=4` is latently broken. `wave_common.wave_label_transform` is a module-level function and pickles \u2014 that's what makes workers usable in a notebook at all.\n\n**On the earlier crash:** my \"Jupyter restarted\" explanation was wrong. `baseline_rd_n10` and `probe_lr3e-5` died identically, before their first training step. The likely cause is RAM \u2014 my own budget of `8 \u00d7 1 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 1.74 GB = 27.8 GB` counted only the decoded tensor, not the ~1.18 GB `BytesIO` each worker holds *while* decoding, which puts the real figure near 46 GB. I couldn't confirm it (the `cgroup`/`ps` reads were declined), so the notebook path with few workers is the prudent instrument.\n\n**Five decisions, defaults in the plan** \u2014 the first dominates the runtime:\n\n1. Load **only AIA193** in nb1 (same events, same split, 12.7\u00d7 faster \u2014 2 min/epoch vs 26 at N=384) or all 13 channels for byte-identical tensors. The new `channel_index` docstring is written for exactly this choice.\n2. `N = 384` (full matched train split).\n3. `baseline_lr = 1.0` \u2014 your own docstring says 0.01 is two orders too small now (feature ~0.1, needed weight ~10\u2013100).\n4. Train **both** baselines, each in its own wandb run.\n5. `num_workers=4, prefetch_factor=1`.\n\nI'll also move `plot_run()` out of `4_finetune_wave_1D.py` (unimportable \u2014 the module name starts with a digit) into `wave_common.py` so notebook and script share one plotting function rather than duplicating it.",
