              "plan": "# 18-hour plan: wave-event classification baselines + Surya LoRA fine-tune\n\n## Context\n\nYou need publishable results in 18 unattended hours on binary classification of EUV wave events,\ncomparing simple baselines against a Surya LoRA fine-tune. Two prior attempts failed (a baseline\nthat overfit 10 samples; a Surya run that numerically diverged) and the notebook path has silent\nmisconfigurations. Everything below is **measured**, not estimated \u2014 several measurements\ninvalidate the obvious plan, and one of your own observations exposed a scientific risk that now\ndrives the ordering of the work.\n\n---\n\n## STATUS \u2014 2026-09-17 18:00 (Phases 0-3 COMPLETE, Phases 4-5 to run)\n\nThe JupyterHub singleuser server restarted at ~17:57 and killed every process it had spawned\n(host uptime 21 h, server process 4 min). GPU is idle, 0 MiB. **All CPU work survived on disk;\nall GPU work was lost.**\n\n**Done and verified on disk:**\n- Phase 0 \u2014 fixes landed. The probe log confirms them in the *running* code, not just the YAML:\n  36 LoRA modules adapted (16 in attention), trainable **1,518,081**, `ts` shape\n  `(1,13,2,4096,4096)`. Bugs 1, 2, 6 fixed.\n- Phase 1 \u2014 data pass complete. train **384** / val **24** / test **48**, exact class balance,\n  `ds_time_tolerance: 15min`, seed 42. Both open items resolved to their defaults.\n- Phase 2 \u2014 **the plan's central worry is refuted.** Median pooled SNR: +12 \u2192 1.49, +24 \u2192 1.89,\n  **+36 \u2192 3.11 (peak)**, +48 \u2192 2.07; catalog's +30 min \u2192 **2.50** on the rising flank. +30 min is\n  an acceptable sampling point. **The re-timed-catalog remedy is dropped \u2014 not needed.**\n- Phase 3 \u2014 baseline arm complete. 1.2 reproduced (train 0.359\u21920.0066, acc 1.0 from epoch 1;\n  val 0.771\u21920.858; val acc pinned at 0.500; AUROC below chance throughout). Caveat: the strict\n  `val_auroc_frozen` check is **False** \u2014 AUROC rises 0.333\u21920.444 over epochs 0-4 then holds for\n  11 epochs, so it is frozen over the tail only, not from epoch 0 as in `version_3`.\n  Baseline learning curve (chance = 0.693): N=8 0.605, 16 0.696, 32 0.628, 64 0.641, 128 0.592,\n  256 0.578, **384 0.561** (AUROC 0.813); seed spread collapses from 0.28 wide at N=16 to 0.005 at\n  N=384. Monotone from N=128 up. Weight decay inert below 1e-3; pool kernel 16 marginally best.\n- Smoke test `smoke_n8` passed at 16:30.\n\n**Not done:** Phase 4 and Phase 5 in full. The `probe_lr3e-5` run printed its model summary and was\nkilled before any training step; no `probe_lr*_summary.json` exists.\n\n**DEFECT \u2014 Phases 1-3 never logged to wandb.** `run_baseline_study.py`, `plot_task_1_1.py`,\n`wave_visibility_scan.py` and `extract_baseline_features.py` contain **no wandb calls at all**; they\nwrite PNG + JSON to `experiments/results/` only. This violates the standing instruction \"save plots\nin wandb and also in the machine\" \u2014 the whole baseline arm is invisible on wandb, which is how it was\nnoticed. Only the GPU entry point `4_finetune_wave_1D.py` logs correctly. Fix: add wandb logging to\n`run_baseline_study.py` (per-epoch series for the 1.2 reproduction and the learning curve, not just\nimage attachments) and re-run it \u2014 CPU-only on cached features, a few minutes, no S3. Upload the\ntask 1.1 and visibility figures as media. Phase 1 extraction does **not** need re-running.\n\n**Correction to the \"reused as-is\" claim.** `run_baseline_study.py` does not import\n`RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`; it reimplements it on the cached features\n(`torch.nn.Linear(n_features,1)` + BCEWithLogits over a 32x32 mean-pooled AIA193 RD). The\ncomputation is equivalent \u2014 the cache holds an 8x mean pool (4096->512) and the study re-pools by 4\n(512->128), and successive uniform non-overlapping means compose into a single 32x pool, matching the\nclass's `pool_kernel=32`, uniform 1/1024 weights, signum-log space, `T = [t_prev, t_now]` \u2014 but it is\na reimplementation and should be described as one.\n\n**Resume action:** relaunch `run_surya_schedule.sh` from the `probe` phase (smoke already passed),\n**detached via `setsid nohup`** so a Jupyter-server restart cannot kill it again. ~11.5 h of\nschedule against 18 h available from 18:00. No script changes needed; the driver is already\ncontinue-on-failure with per-run `max_time` caps and per-run logs/summaries.\n\n---\n\n## CURRENT TASK \u2014 notebook 1: fix the train/val datasets, train the new 2-param baseline in wandb\n\n### Context\n\nThree things changed since everything below was written, and together they move the work back into\nthe notebook.\n\n1. **`models/simple_baseline.py` was rewritten (by you, after 18:50).** `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`\n   is now `(channel_index, pool_kernel=32)` \u2014 **`img_size` is gone** \u2014 and it is a **2-parameter**\n   logistic regression on one scalar per sample, `mean(|pool\u2083\u2082(now \u2212 prev)|)`, computed by a new\n   module-level `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference()`. `ConstantProbabilityModel` (a single learned\n   logit) is also new. The 16,385-param version that memorized 384 samples is gone, and with it the\n   memorization failure mode: with two parameters the model is a *threshold detector*, so AUROC is a\n   fixed property of the feature and only loss/calibration are learned.\n2. **Two callers still pass the removed `img_size` and are broken.** Notebook 1 cell 17, and\n   `4_finetune_wave_1D.py:355\u2013358` where the construction is commented out \u2014 leaving `model`\n   undefined, so line 363 raises `NameError`. The script's `--train_baseline` path cannot run at all\n   right now.\n3. **The scripted run crashed.** `baseline_rd_n10` died at 18:50:12 before its first training step,\n   silently (no traceback, no CSV row, 7-byte wandb history) \u2014 a SIGKILL, almost certainly RAM at the\n   first batch: 8 workers \u00d7 prefetch 1 \u00d7 batch 2 each hold a decoded 1.74 GB sample *plus* the\n   ~1.18 GB `BytesIO` it was decoded from, \u224846 GB materializing at once, not the 27.8 GB this plan\n   budgeted. `probe_lr3e-5` died identically, so my earlier \"the Jupyter server restarted\" explanation\n   was wrong. Unconfirmed: the two reads that would settle it (`/sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events`, `ps`)\n   were declined.\n\nThe notebook is the safer instrument for the baseline: it runs the data path in-process or with few\nworkers, so the RAM cliff is avoided while the crash cause is still open.\n\n### The dataset fix \u2014 the reusable part\n\nBoth notebooks currently call `build_helio_dataloaders(cfg, waveDSDataset, \u2026)` (nb1 cell 10,\nnb2 cell 11) and the recon confirms their datasets are already argument-for-argument equivalent\napart from `num_workers` (0 vs 4). So \"the same dataset\" is not the problem \u2014 **the shared\nconstruction is wrong in four ways, identically in both**:\n\n| defect | consequence | fix (existing code, no new logic) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `max_number_of_samples=cfg.data.max_samples` (=10) | head-slices by `ds_index`, so you get the **5 earliest pairs, all June\u2013July 2010** \u2014 a chronological sample, not a random one | `wave_common.event_stratified_subset(ds, n_samples=N, seed=42)` \u2014 seeded, **event-level**, exactly 50/50, nested across N |\n| `drop_last=True` on the **val** loader | silently discards 1 of 25 val samples \u2014 4% of the metric `ModelCheckpoint` selects on | `wave_common.build_wave_loader(..., drop_last=False)` |\n| `prefetch_factor` not exposed | the only bound on worker RAM \u2014 and the thing that just killed a run | `build_wave_loader(..., prefetch_factor=1)` |\n| no test split; disk-cache read path (EFS, 9 MB/s) | no held-out evaluation; 40\u00d7 slower reads | `wave_common.build_wave_datasets(cfg, scalers, include_test=True, in_memory=True)` \u2192 400 MB/s |\n\nAll four already exist in `experiments/wave_common.py` and are exactly what `4_finetune_wave_1D.py`\nuses, so putting them in nb1 cell 10 makes the notebook and the script agree \u2014 and nb2 cell 11\nbecomes a copy of the same cell. `config_script.yaml` needs **no edit**: `build_wave_datasets` passes\n`max_number_of_samples=None` itself, and the config's `time_delta_input_minutes: [-12, 0]` /\n`time_dim: 2` are already right. Both notebooks and the script read the same\n`surya_aws_s3_{train,val,test}.csv`, so the split is identical by construction.\n\nOne picklability gain worth naming: the current `label_transform=lambda s: \u2026` **cannot survive\n`spawn`**, so nb2's `num_workers=4` is latently broken. `build_wave_datasets` uses the module-level\n`wave_common.wave_label_transform`, which pickles \u2014 that is what makes workers usable in a notebook.\n\n### Cells to modify in `1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb`\n\nModifying existing cells, not adding a parallel path. Nothing is deleted.\n\n| cell | change |\n|---|---|\n| 3 | add `import wandb`, `import pandas as pd`, `import matplotlib.pyplot as plt` \u2014 cell 42's `wandb.finish()` currently raises `NameError` because `wandb` is never imported |\n| 10 | **the dataset fix above**: `wc.build_wave_datasets(...)` + `wc.event_stratified_subset(...)` on each split + two `wc.build_wave_loader(...)` calls; print `SubsetInfo.describe()` per split so the counts and class balance are visible |\n| 13 | keep `aia193_index` (now `cfg.data.channels.index(\"aia193\")`, which is what the new docstring demands); `img_size` is no longer needed by the model |\n| 15 | also import `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference` for the feature diagnostic below |\n| 17 | **drop the removed `img_size`** \u2192 `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(channel_index=aia193_index, pool_kernel=32)`; print the parameter count so \"2 parameters\" is on the page |\n| 34 | baseline lr \u2014 `cfg.learning_rate` (0.01) is ~2 orders too small for this model per its own docstring (feature ~0.1, needed weight ~10\u2013100); set an explicit `baseline_lr` with that reasoning in a comment, leaving `cfg.learning_rate` for Surya in nb2 |\n| 37 | keep `make_loggers()` as-is; the bug is that cell 39 calls it **twice in one cell**, so both baselines land in one wandb run (\"a wandb run already in progress \u2026 will reuse this run\") |\n| 39/41/44 | restructure into one fit sequence per model: `wandb.finish()` if a run is live \u2192 `make_loggers(name)` \u2192 `Trainer` \u2192 `fit` \u2192 `wandb.finish()`. Un-comment the constant-probability fit (cell 41) so the base-rate reference is actually trained |\n| 42 | `if wandb.run is not None: wandb.finish()` \u2014 guarded, so re-running is safe |\n| new, after each fit | read `metrics.csv` from that run's `CSVLogger`, `groupby(\"epoch\").mean()`, plot per-epoch `train_loss`/`val_loss` vs `ln 2` and `val_metric_accuracy`/`val_metric_auroc` vs 0.5; save PNG to `experiments/results/` **and** `wandb.log({\u2026 wandb.Image \u2026})`, so disk and wandb carry the same numbers |\n| new, one cell | **feature diagnostic** \u2014 the whole result for a 1-feature model: `mean_abs_pooled_running_difference` over the val split, wave vs no-wave, plus its ROC. AUROC here is a property of the feature, so this figure explains the outcome that the loss curves cannot |\n\nThe plotting logic already exists as `plot_run()` in `4_finetune_wave_1D.py:518`, but that module name\nstarts with a digit and cannot be imported. Per CLAUDE.md's reusability directive I will **move it to\n`wave_common.py`** and have both the script and the notebook call it, rather than write a second copy.\n\nAlso fix `4_finetune_wave_1D.py:355\u2013358` \u2014 uncomment, drop `img_size` \u2014 so the script's\n`--train_baseline` path works again with the new model.\n\n### Cost, and the one decision that dominates it\n\nThe baseline needs only AIA193, but the dataset decodes whatever `data.channels` lists. Measured:\n**15.2 s/sample for 13 channels vs 1.2 s/sample for AIA193 alone** \u2014 12.7\u00d7. At `num_workers=4` that is\n3.8 s/sample vs 0.3 s/sample, i.e. for N=384 train + 25 val, **\u224826 min/epoch vs \u22482 min/epoch**.\n\nNarrowing `cfg.data.channels = [\"aia193\"]` in nb1 keeps the **same events, same split, same seed** and\nonly changes which channels are read; the new `channel_index` docstring is written for exactly this\n(0 vs 3). nb2 keeps all 13 for Surya. **My recommendation: narrow it** \u2014 it is what makes a full-N run\nfeasible inside a notebook, and the scientific comparison is over events, not tensors.\n\n### Open decisions \u2014 defaults in bold, I will proceed on these unless you say otherwise\n\n1. **Channels in nb1: `[\"aia193\"]` only (13\u00d7 faster, same events)** vs all 13 (byte-identical tensors,\n   ~26 min/epoch at N=384).\n2. **Train subset `N = 384`** (the full matched train split, val 25, test 49) \u2014 cheap under option 1,\n   the number Surya must beat. Smaller N only if you want the notebook to run in minutes.\n3. **`baseline_lr = 1.0`** for the 2-param model (reaches weight ~30 in ~30 Adam steps; 0.01 needs\n   ~3000). Conservative alternative 0.1 with more epochs.\n4. **Train both baselines** (constant-probability *and* running-difference), each in its own wandb run,\n   so the base rate is on the same axes.\n5. **`num_workers=4, prefetch_factor=1`** in the notebook (\u224812 GB worst case, well under the cliff that\n   killed the script run). Falls back to 0 if `spawn` misbehaves under the kernel.\n\n### Verification\n\n- Cell 10 prints, per split, `N samples / N events (n wave, n no wave)` with **exactly equal** class\n  counts (`event_stratified_subset` raises otherwise) and val/test sample counts **not** reduced by 1.\n- `next(iter(train_data_loader))[\"ts\"].shape == (2, C, 2, 4096, 4096)` with `C == 1` under option 1.\n- Cell 17 prints **2 trainable parameters**.\n- Each fit produces its **own** wandb run (no \"reusing this run\" warning) with `train_loss`,\n  `val_loss`, `val_metric_accuracy`, `val_metric_auroc` series, plus the PNGs as wandb media.\n- Per-epoch train and val loss both plotted, on disk in `experiments/results/` and identical in wandb.\n- The running-difference baseline's val loss beats the constant baseline's; if it does not, the feature\n  diagnostic figure says why, and that is a real result rather than a bug.\n- `python -m downstream_apps.test.4_finetune_wave_1D --train_baseline --train-n 10 --no-eval-test`\n  reaches its first training step (proves the `NameError` fix and probes the RAM cliff at low N).\n\n---\n\n## SUPERSEDED (kept for reference) \u2014 re-run the baseline through the real pipeline, then Surya\n\nRequirement: the baseline must use the **existing** model, loss and metrics \u2014 exactly as\n`1_baseline_template_diego.ipynb` builds them \u2014 must log to wandb, and must produce per-epoch\ntrain/val loss plots carrying the same information as wandb. **No new model, loss or metric code.**\n\n**This already exists in full; nothing needs to be written.** `4_finetune_wave_1D.py --train_baseline`\n(flag at line 96, branch at lines 348-365) is the notebook's recipe, component for component:\n\n| what the notebook does | where `4_finetune_wave_1D.py` does the same |\n|---|---|\n| `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(channel_index=aia193, img_size=\u2026)` from `models/simple_baseline.py` | line 355, same class, same defaults (`pool_kernel=32`) |\n| `partial(destandardize_channels, channel_order=cfg.data.channels, scalers=scalers)` as `preprocess_fn` | line 359, identical |\n| four `WaveMetrics` modes from `metrics/template_metrics.py` | `build_metrics()`, line 334 |\n| `WaveLightningModule(model, metrics, lr, batch_size, preprocess_fn)` | line 364, identical |\n| `WandbLogger(entity, project, name)` + `CSVLogger(\"runs\", \u2026)` | lines 411-419 |\n| `ModelCheckpoint(monitor=\"val_loss\", mode=\"min\")` | line 423 |\n\nTwo properties make this the right instrument rather than a lookalike:\n- `build_metrics()` is called at **line 346, before** the `if train_baseline:` branch, so the baseline\n  and every Surya run share one metrics construction. They cannot drift apart.\n- `plot_run()` (line 518) builds its figures from `metrics.csv` \u2014 the *same* values Lightning sends to\n  wandb \u2014 then `wandb.log({... wandb.Image ...})` (line 701) uploads those same PNGs. So \"the local\n  plot contains the same information as wandb\" holds by construction. Left panel is per-epoch\n  `train_loss` and `val_loss` vs `ln 2`; right panel is accuracy/AUROC.\n\n`wandb.finish()` runs in a `finally` (line 726), so runs cannot bleed into each other.\n\n**Supersedes the cached-feature study.** `run_baseline_study.py`'s reimplementation stays on disk as\nthe cheap N-sweep, but the headline baseline numbers now come from the real pipeline.\n\n### Runs, in order (~3.5 h, before any Surya work)\n\nCost driver: `--train_baseline` reads through the real dataset, so it pays the full 13-channel decode\n\u2014 **2.97 s/sample** at `--num-workers 8` (measured), not the 1.2 s/sample of the AIA193-only cache.\n\n| run | N | epochs | flags | est. wall |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `baseline_rd_n10` | 10 | 15 | `--train-n 10 --accum 1 --no-eval-test` | ~10 min |\n| `baseline_rd_n384` | 384 | 10 | `--train-n 384 --accum 1 --max-time 03:30:00` | ~3.2 h |\n\nBoth at the config's `lr` (0.01, the value the notebook used) and `--num-workers 8\n--prefetch-factor 1 --subset-seed 42`, on the same 384/24/48 split the Surya runs use, so the\nsame-dataset requirement holds across tasks 1 and 2. `n10` is the faithful 1.2 reproduction and should\nshow train collapsing while val rises; `n384` is the number Surya has to beat, with a held-out test\nevaluation.\n\nThen Surya: relaunch `run_surya_schedule.sh` from the `probe` phase, **detached via `setsid nohup`** so\na Jupyter-server restart cannot kill it again.\n\n**Budget from 18:00:** 3.5 h baseline + ~11.5 h Surya \u2248 15 h of the 18 h available.\n\n---\n\n## Measurements that drive every decision\n\n**Hardware** \u2014 1\u00d7 L40S, 47.7 GB VRAM, bf16 OK; 16 CPUs; 124 GB RAM; GPU idle.\n\n**Surya throughput / VRAM** (LoRA + bf16 autocast + grad checkpointing on all 10 layers):\n\n| batch | input dtype | s/step | **s/sample** | peak alloc | peak reserved |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | fp32 | 3.05 | **3.05** | 17.5 GB | 18.0 GB |\n| 2 | fp32 | 6.22 | **3.11** | 33.4 GB | 34.6 GB |\n| 3 | fp32 | \u2014 | OOM | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| 2 | bf16 | 6.22 | **3.11** | 31.6 GB | 32.8 GB |\n| 3 | bf16 | 10.76 | **3.59** | 46.5 GB | 47.0 GB |\n| 4 | bf16 | \u2014 | OOM | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n\n**Maximizing VRAM is the wrong objective here.** The GPU is already compute-saturated by a single\n65,536-token sample, so throughput is flat-to-worse in batch size: B=3 fits (with bf16 inputs) but\nis **18% slower per sample**. Use **batch_size 2 + `accumulate_grad_batches` 4** (effective batch 8,\n34.6 GB, near-optimal 3.11 s/sample); B=1 is equally valid and halves both VRAM and worker RAM.\nBudget **3.1 s per training sample**.\n\n**LoRA rank is not a memory lever.** Adapters at r=8 are 1,515,520 params \u2248 **24 MB** of\nweights+grads+Adam, i.e. **0.07%** of the 33 GB peak (r=32 is still only 97 MB). Memory is dominated\nby the 1.74 GB/sample fp32 input plus activations. Rank remains a useful *capacity* knob on a small\ntraining set, so r \u2208 {4, 8} is an ablation, not a memory fix.\n\n**RAM is the real hazard.** `ts` is `(B,13,2,4096,4096)` fp32 = **1.74 GB/sample**. Worst-case RSS \u2248\n`num_workers \u00d7 prefetch_factor \u00d7 batch_size \u00d7 1.74 GB`; the config's `num_workers: 8` with default\n`prefetch_factor=2` at batch 2 is **56 GB** before the main process, pinned copies and page cache.\nMust be bounded explicitly. Chosen: **`num_workers=8, prefetch_factor=1`** \u2192 27.8 GB, which is both\nfaster *and* lighter than `6 \u00d7 2` (41.8 GB). Set `OMP_NUM_THREADS=2` (8 workers on 16 CPUs).\n\n**Per-sample CPU cost, and why task 1 is far cheaper than task 2:**\n\n| read | time | signum-log | total/sample | RAM/sample |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| all 13 channels | 4.69 s \u00d72 frames | 4.67 s | **15.2 s** | 1.74 GB |\n| **aia193 only** | 0.40 s \u00d72 frames | 0.39 s | **1.2 s** | 0.134 GB |\n\nThe RD baseline uses only AIA193, so task 1 runs **12.7\u00d7 cheaper** than task 2. Note the 15.2 s\nfigure means the Surya pipeline is **CPU/IO-balanced against the GPU, not GPU-dominated** \u2014 \u22655\nworkers are needed just to keep it fed, so budget the data-limited rate (~3.6 s/sample) for epochs.\n\n**Cadence and label geometry (both verified):** bucket modal spacing is **12 min** (445,606 of\n~449k gaps), so the RD pair `[-12, 0]` is exactly **one consecutive frame step** \u2014 as you wanted.\nSeparately, the catalog places `wave` at `start_time + 30.0 min` and `no wave` at\n`start_time \u2212 30.0 min` (std = 0 across all 914 events), so the positive and negative *samples* of\none event are 60 min apart. Both statements are true; they describe different things.\n\n**Dataset:** catalog is exactly 914/914 balanced. Matched against the day-disjoint Surya indices\nunder `[-12, 0]`: train **1231**, val **27**, test **54**. Cache coverage of the frames this task\nneeds is only **2%** (49/2457 train), so frames must be fetched; measured S3 saturates at\n**~600 MB/s** with \u22658 parallel fetches. Since you confirmed the dataset may shrink, we use a\n**seeded, event-level stratified subset capped at 384 train** \u2014 822 frames \u2248 460 GB \u2248 13 min of\ndownload \u2014 which buys many more epochs per run and cleaner curves than 4 epochs on 1231.\n\n---\n\n## Confirmed bugs (all verified by execution)\n\n1. **The pretrained tokenizer is silently discarded.** Checkpoint patch embedding is\n   `Conv2d(26,1280,16,16)` = 13 channels \u00d7 **2** timesteps. The config's `time_dim: 1` builds a\n   13-channel tokenizer, so it is skipped and stays **random**. Measured: `time_dim=1` \u2192 **156/159**\n   tensors load; `time_dim=2` \u2192 **157/159** (only the pretraining decoder `unembed.*`, correctly).\n   *Every Surya result so far was produced with a random tokenizer.* Fixing it also gives the RD\n   baseline its 2 frames, so one dataset serves both tasks.\n2. **LoRA never adapted attention.** `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]`\n   \u2014 Surya has none of those (q/k/v fused as `attn.qkv`, output `attn.proj`). PEFT only errors when\n   *nothing* matches, so only `fc1`/`fc2` were adapted: **2,666,241** trainable vs **3,157,761**\n   with `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`. Your diverged run logged \"2.7 M\", confirming it.\n3. **`learning_rate: 0.01` diverges Surya** (losses 10\u00b2\u201310\u2074, exact `0.0` bf16 values, val\n   acc/AUROC pinned at 0.5). That LR was tuned on the 16 K-param logistic baseline.\n4. **`max_samples` subsetting is chronologically biased** \u2014 `waveDSDataset` head-slices a frame\n   sorted by `ds_index`, so `max_samples: 10` gives the 5 earliest pairs (all June\u2013July 2010).\n   The scaling study must use seeded random **event-level** subsets instead.\n5. **`build_helio_dataloaders` sets `drop_last=True` for validation too**, silently dropping 1 of\n   27 val samples (3.7%); it also does not expose `prefetch_factor`, the knob needed to bound RAM.\n6. **The head is correct but half-redundant.** `head_unembed` gives a single logit,\n   `squeeze`\u2192`(B,)`, BCE-compatible, shapes match `WaveMetrics` \u2014 functionally fine for\n   classification. But `head_linear` (1280\u21921280) feeds `head_unembed` (1280\u21921) with **no\n   nonlinearity between them**, so at eval it is exactly one linear map: those **1,639,680 params\n   add zero representational capacity** while being 52% of all trainable weights. Measured with\n   `penultimate_linear_layer: false`: head = 2,561 params, total trainable **1,518,081**. Adopt\n   `false` as the default (less overfitting surface on a few-hundred-sample set) and A/B it.\n\n### Task 2.1 \u2014 state of `2_finetune_template_1D_diego.ipynb`\nYour fixes all landed: runs top-to-bottom with sequential exec counts, `load_wave_config`,\n`import numpy as np` + `label_transform`, `WaveMetrics` / `WaveLightningModule`,\n`max_epochs = cfg.max_epochs`. Remaining: bugs **1, 2, 3, 6** reach it via the shared YAML (random\ntokenizer, starved LoRA, divergent LR, redundant head); `max_number_of_samples=10` hardcoded rather\nthan `cfg.data.max_samples`; no `wandb.finish()` (causes run reuse); and several markdown cells\nstill describe the flare regression task (`FlareDataConfig`/`load_flare_config`, \"Mean Squared\nError\"/\"RRSE\" where the code computes BCE/accuracy/AUROC).\n\n---\n\n## Plan\n\nCheap, high-information work first; every Surya run gets a hard `Trainer(max_time=...)` cap so no\nrun can starve the rest.\n\n### Phase 0 \u2014 Fixes and pre-flight (~30 min, no GPU)\nFix bugs 1, 2, 6 in `configs/config_script.yaml` with old values kept as comments (so the notebooks\nbecome correct too), plus a new config for run-scale settings. Fix notebook 2's remaining items.\nPre-flight assertions that fail loudly rather than training something wrong: **157/159** tensors\nload, trainable == **1,518,081** (or 3,157,761 with the penultimate layer on), `ts.shape[2] == 2`,\nand the `[LoRA] Adapted modules` list contains `attn.qkv` and `attn.proj`.\n\n### Phase 1 \u2014 Data pass: cache warm + baseline features + 1.1 arrays (~30-45 min, no GPU)\nOne pass over the 384-train/27-val/54-test subset that does three jobs. Full 13-channel `.nc` files\nare downloaded (Surya needs them later, so pay it once), but **only AIA193 is decoded**, so CPU cost\nis 1.2 s/sample. Writes pooled 128\u00d7128 RD features + labels + timestamps to a small `.npz`\n(~64 KB/sample), and full-resolution arrays for the 1.1 figures. Runs at `num_workers=12,\nprefetch_factor=1` (~2 GB RAM at 1 channel). **Caching is exactly equivalent to the live dataset**:\nI verified no augmentation is active \u2014 `num_mask_aia_channels` defaults to 0 and is never passed,\n`drop_hmi_probability` is 0.0, `random_vert_flip` defaults to False \u2014 so there is no augmentation\ndraw to freeze and train/val differ only by index.\n\n### Phase 2 \u2014 Task 1.1, including the wave-visibility test (~30 min, CPU only)\n- **1.1 as asked:** for 2-3 matched wave/no-wave pairs, plot AIA193 at *t*, the full-resolution\n  running difference, and the **32\u00d732-pooled 128\u00d7128 map the baseline actually sees**, side by side\n  within each pair. Because the negative is the same event 1 h earlier, this is a controlled\n  comparison. Frame ordering is verified correct: `time_delta_input_minutes` is sorted ascending\n  with the offset-0 \"now\" frame last, so the model's `x[:,-1] - x[:,0]` really is\n  now-minus-12-min and the plotted difference carries the sign the baseline trains on.\n- **1.1 extended \u2014 the risk your 40-min figure exposes.** Positives sit at onset **+30 min**, so\n  their RD spans **+18 \u2192 +30 min**, i.e. the faint tail of the visibility window. For ~6 events I\n  will extract the RD at onset **+12, +24, +36, +48 min** and measure RD contrast versus offset.\n  If signal peaks near +12 and has decayed by +30, then the label timing \u2014 not the sample count \u2014 is\n  the primary reason the baseline cannot generalize. Remedy if confirmed: emit a **new** catalog CSV\n  (original untouched) with the positive offset moved to the measured optimum, and re-run. This is\n  cheap and could be the most consequential result of the whole 18 hours, which is why it comes\n  before any GPU time.\n\n### Phase 3 \u2014 Task 1.2 and the baseline arm (~20 min, CPU only)\nThe cached features make the baseline instant, so 1.2 gets demonstrated rather than asserted.\n`runs/.../baseline_running_diff/version_3` trained **16,385 params on 10 samples at lr=0.01**: train\nloss 0.814\u21920.0058, train accuracy 1.0 from epoch 1, val loss rising monotonically 0.760\u21921.006. The\ntell that it is memorization and not miscalibration is that **val AUROC froze at exactly\n0.4000000059604645 from epoch 0 through 13** \u2014 the *ranking* of val samples never changed, so the\nmodel only rescaled a direction fixed in the first few steps. Separately your Surya run did not\noverfit at all, it **diverged** (bug 3). I will reproduce the overfit, then run a learning curve over\nN \u2208 {8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 384} (event-level stratified, seconds per fit) plus weight-decay and\npooling-kernel sweeps \u2014 giving the baseline arm of the \"does val loss improve with more images\"\nquestion at negligible cost.\n\n### Phase 4 \u2014 Surya LR probe (~30 min GPU)\nInsurance against repeating bug 3: short runs at lr \u2208 {3e-5, 1e-4, 3e-4}, ~60 steps each. Keep the\nlargest LR whose loss decreases without bf16 saturation. All real runs use `gradient_clip_val=1.0`.\n\n### Phase 5 \u2014 Surya scaling study + ablations (~11 h GPU, remainder as slack)\n\n| run | train N (events) | epochs | est. wall | `max_time` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 48 (24 pairs) | 30 | ~1.3 h | 1.8 h |\n| B | 96 (48 pairs) | 24 | ~2.0 h | 2.5 h |\n| C | 192 (96 pairs) | 18 | ~3.0 h | 3.5 h |\n| D | 384 (192 pairs) | 14 | ~4.7 h | 5.5 h |\n| E | ablations at best N: r=4 vs 8, penultimate head on/off | \u2014 | slack | rest |\n\nIncreasing-N order, so an overrun still leaves the smaller runs complete. Each run: `batch_size=2`,\n`accumulate_grad_batches=4`, `num_workers=8`, `prefetch_factor=1` (\u224827.8 GB RAM),\n`OMP_NUM_THREADS=2`, bf16-mixed, `gradient_clip_val=1.0`, `max_time` cap, its own wandb run with an\nexplicit `wandb.finish()` between runs. Primary result: **best val loss vs N** with the baseline on\nthe same axes; I will also report the iso-compute slice (val loss at equal samples-seen) from the\nlogged curves so both readings are available without extra runs. **2.4** is read off these curves.\n\n---\n\n## Files to create (nothing removed or overwritten)\n\n| path | responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `downstream_apps/test/4_finetune_wave_1D.py` | Adapted copy of `3_finetune_template_1D.py` (task 2.2). Wave imports; `--lr`, `--num-workers`, `--prefetch-factor`, `--accum`, `--max-time`, `--train-n`, `--subset-seed`, `--lora-r`, `--no-penultimate`, `--run-name`; builds its own DataLoaders from `build_helio_datasets` so it can set `prefetch_factor`, use `drop_last=False` for val, and take event-level stratified subsets; `gradient_clip_val`; pre-flight assertions; plots saved locally **and** to wandb. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/extract_baseline_features.py` | Phase 1: cache warm + AIA193-only pooled RD `.npz` + 1.1 arrays. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/wave_visibility_scan.py` | Phase 2 extended: RD contrast vs onset offset (+12/+24/+36/+48). |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_baseline_study.py` | Phase 3: 1.2 reproduction, learning curve, sweeps. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/run_surya_schedule.sh` | Phases 4-5 driver: sequential runs, per-run `max_time`, continue-on-failure. |\n| `downstream_apps/test/configs/config_wave_full.yaml` | Run-scale settings (new file). |\n| `downstream_apps/test/experiments/results/` | Local PNGs + metrics JSON, mirrored to wandb. |\n\nReused as-is: `build_helio_datasets`, `build_scalers`, `apply_peft_lora`, `load_pretrained_weights`,\n`HelioSpectformer1D.from_config`, `WaveMetrics`, `WaveLightningModule`, `waveDSDataset`,\n`load_wave_config`, `destandardize_channels`, `ConstantProbabilityModel`,\n`RunningDifferenceLogisticModel`.\n\n---\n\n## Verification\n\n- **Pre-flight (blocks training):** 157/159 tensors load; trainable == 1,518,081 (or 3,157,761);\n  `ts.shape == (B,13,2,4096,4096)`; `attn.qkv`/`attn.proj` present in the adapted-modules list;\n  sample counts match the requested subset with exact class balance.\n- **Resource guards:** log `torch.cuda.max_memory_reserved()` and process RSS every N steps; abort\n  a run if RSS crosses a ceiling (default 70 GB) rather than letting the machine die.\n- **Phase 1:** `.npz` row count and label split correct; a pooled feature map is finite and\n  non-constant.\n- **Phase 2:** RD-contrast-vs-offset curve produced for \u22656 events, with an explicit verdict on\n  whether +30 min is a good sampling point.\n- **Phase 3:** the reproduced 10-sample run shows train \u2193 / val \u2191 with frozen AUROC, matching\n  `version_3` \u2014 confirming the diagnosis is real and not a logging artifact.\n- **Phase 4:** at least one LR decreases monotonically with no bf16 saturation.\n- **Phase 5:** each run leaves a `best-{epoch}-{val_loss}.ckpt`, a CSV log, a finished wandb run and\n  PNGs on disk; final val-loss-vs-N figure plus the written answer to 2.4.\n- **Smoke test before the unattended stretch:** `4_finetune_wave_1D.py --train-n 8 --max-epochs 1`\n  completes, logs, checkpoints and finishes wandb cleanly.\n\n**Budget:** 0.5 h fixes + 0.75 h data pass + 0.5 h task 1.1 + 0.35 h task 1.2/baseline + 0.5 h LR\nprobe + 11 h Surya \u2248 13.6 h, leaving ~4 h slack for ablations, the test-split evaluation, overruns\nand writeup. Tasks 1.1, 1.2 and the full baseline arm all land inside the first ~2.5 h, so you have\nreal results early regardless of what happens overnight.\n\n## Open items I still need from you (answer on approval)\n1. **Validation is only 27 samples** (1 sample = 3.7%). Default: monitor on the 27-sample val and\n   additionally evaluate the best checkpoint on the official 54-sample test split (81 held out,\n   +60 GB, no extra training time, official splits intact).\n2. **Match tolerance** \u2014 default: tighten `ds_time_tolerance` from `4d` to `15min`. 99.2% of matches\n   already land within 15 min, and the 10 loosest train matches (up to 1 day) are 9-to-1 \"no wave\",\n   which is exactly what skews balance to 612/619 instead of 611/611.\n"
