      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Done. The notebook now runs top to bottom and trains both baselines. What I changed:\n\n**The two hard errors**\n- `RunningDifferenceLogisticModel(..., img_size=...)` \u2192 dropped the removed argument. Verified: 2 trainable parameters, forward returns `(B, 1)`.\n- `import wandb` added to cell 3, and the bare `wandb.finish()` is now guarded (`if wandb.run is not None`), so re-running a cell is safe.\n\n**The dataset (cell `70e9c5b2`)** now calls `wave_common.build_wave_datasets` \u2192 `event_stratified_subset` \u2192 two `build_wave_loader` calls, which is exactly what `4_finetune_wave_1D.py` uses. That replaces the chronological `max_samples=10` head-slice with a seeded event-level draw (`TRAIN_N = 384`, `SUBSET_SEED = 42`), turns off `drop_last` for validation, bounds worker RAM via `prefetch_factor=1`, and adds the held-out test split. `label_transform` is now the module-level `wave_label_transform`, so `NUM_WORKERS = 4` actually works \u2014 the old inline lambda couldn't be pickled under `spawn`. Notebook 2 can use this cell verbatim; that's what makes \"same dataset across tasks 1 and 2\" hold by construction rather than by coincidence.\n\nThe cell prints per-split counts with class balance and the worst-case loader RAM, so you can see before training whether you're near the limit that killed the scripted run.\n\n**Logging** \u2014 `make_trainer(run_name)` builds each Trainer immediately before its own `.fit()`, and `make_loggers` ends any live run first. Each baseline now gets its own wandb run instead of the second overwriting the first at the same step numbers. The constant-probability fit is uncommented, so you have the ln 2 floor to compare against. `finish_run()` plots the per-epoch curves from that run's `metrics.csv`, saves the PNG to `experiments/results/`, attaches it to wandb, displays it inline, and closes the run.\n\n**Two judgement calls you should know about:**\n\n`baseline_lr = 0.1`, not the 1.0 in the approved plan. At `TRAIN_N=384` an epoch is 192 steps, so 0.1 reaches the needed weight (~30) in under two epochs while leaving the residual oscillation ~10\u00d7 tighter than 1.0 would \u2014 better calibration, same AUROC. The cell says to raise it to 1.0 if you shrink `TRAIN_N` to where an epoch is only a few steps. `cfg.learning_rate` is untouched for Surya.\n\n`BASELINE_CHANNELS_ONLY = True` narrows `cfg.data.channels` to `[\"aia193\"]` \u2014 same events, same split, same seed, 12.7\u00d7 less decoding (~2 min/epoch vs ~26). Flip it to `False` for byte-identical 13-channel tensors. Verified safe: `channels` is a free list (`helio.py:468`) and the masker is inert at your config's settings.\n\n**Shared code, not copies:** `plot_run()` moved from `4_finetune_wave_1D.py` into `wave_common.py` (that module's name starts with a digit, so the notebook could never have imported it) and the script now aliases to it. Also uncommented and fixed the script's baseline construction at line 355 \u2014 `--train_baseline` was raising `NameError: name 'model' is not defined` and couldn't run at all.\n\nEverything compiles, all 48 notebook cells parse, `wave_common` imports cleanly, and `plot_run` is smoke-tested against a synthetic interleaved `metrics.csv`. I have **not** executed the notebook \u2014 the first `next(iter(train_data_loader))` will hit S3, and the RAM cliff that killed `baseline_rd_n10` is still unconfirmed. Run cell `70e9c5b2` and check the printed worst-case RAM figure before starting the fits; if it looks tight, drop `NUM_WORKERS` to 2.",
