    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "b85d518b-cbec-4333-8cd7-c459982d3614",
      "body": "Fixed. Here's what was actually happening.\n\n## The error you saw is not the error\n\n`RuntimeError: Please call iter(combined_loader) first.` is a secondary failure raised during Lightning's teardown. The real error is further up the traceback:\n\n```\nRuntimeError: DataLoader worker (pid(s) 2374, 2392, 2410, 2428) exited unexpectedly\n  <- queue.Empty  (dataloader.py:1275, _try_get_data)\n  <- _MultiProcessingDataLoaderIter._reset(self)   (dataloader.py:1254)\n  <- DataLoader.__iter__ -> else branch            (dataloader.py:491)\n```\n\nThe chain:\n\n1. `build_wave_loader` set `persistent_workers=True`. That keeps the spawned worker pool alive after the iterator is exhausted, which makes the DataLoader **stateful** \u2014 `DataLoader.__iter__` then takes `self._iterator._reset(self)` instead of building a fresh pool. Your batch-inspection cell created that pool at exec 10.\n2. The constant-probability fit (exec 24) was interrupted \u2014 its output says `Detected KeyboardInterrupt`. Lightning's graceful shutdown reaped those four workers.\n3. The running-difference fit (exec 27) reused the **same loader objects**. `_reset()` sent its resume handshake to dead PIDs, waited, got `queue.Empty`, and raised \"worker exited unexpectedly\".\n4. Lightning caught that inside `fit_loop.setup_data()` and tore the loop down. Teardown calls `_DataFetcher.reset()`, which needs `len(combined_loader)` \u2014 but `iter()` had never succeeded, so `CombinedLoader._iterator` was still `None`, and *that* is the message that got printed on top.\n\nWorth noting: the workers themselves are fine. Cell 14 pulled `(2, 1, 2, 4096, 4096)` through those same four spawn workers, so `spawn` works under this kernel \u2014 the old comment warning that it \"crashes on startup here\" is wrong. And `NUM_WORKERS = 0` is **not** a valid fallback: nothing is cached between epochs, so every sample re-fetches ~1.18 GB from S3, which needs parallel readers.\n\n## The fix\n\n- `experiments/wave_common.py:build_wave_loader()` \u2014 new `persistent_workers: bool = True` parameter. The default preserves `4_finetune_wave_1D.py`'s behaviour (one `fit()`, pay spawn's re-import once); the docstring explains why a notebook must pass `False`.\n- Notebook cell 11 (the loader cell) \u2014 `PERSISTENT_WORKERS = False`, passed to both loaders, with the full failure chain written into the comment block.\n\nVerified: `persistent_workers=False` leaves `_iterator is None`, so the `_reset` branch is never reached and the loader survives repeated passes:\n\n```\npersistent_workers=True:  _iterator is retained (stateful)\npersistent_workers=False: _iterator is None (fresh pool each iter)\n```\n\nBoth modules compile; all 48 notebook code cells parse; the script's three `build_wave_loader` call sites use keyword args, so the new trailing default doesn't touch them.\n\n## Two things before you re-run\n\n1. **Re-run cell 11 first.** Your live kernel still holds the old stateful loader objects \u2014 the fix only takes effect once they're rebuilt. Then cell 45.\n2. **The constant-probability fit (cell 42) is fully commented out again**, so the `ln 2 = 0.6931` reference the conclusion cell tells you to compare against won't exist. If you want that floor on the same axes, uncomment those three lines \u2014 it's a 1-parameter model, so it's only as slow as one data pass.",
