      "id": "a1306ee3-7fdc-4018-b389-c68571c63ea4",
      "body": "The parameter *is* in the file \u2014 your kernel just wasn't reading the file. Python caches modules in `sys.modules`, so re-running `from downstream_apps.test.experiments import wave_common as wc` rebinds the same stale module object and never re-executes the source. The signature the kernel had was still the old seven-argument one.\n\nCell 11 now calls `importlib.reload(wc)` right after the import, so it picks up edits to `wave_common.py` on every run. That's safe here because nothing surviving the cell holds a reference into the old module \u2014 the datasets, both loaders and the label transform are all rebuilt below from the reloaded module, and `finish_run()` resolves `wc.plot_run` at call time rather than capturing it.\n\nConfirmed on disk:\n\n```\nbuild_wave_loader(dataset, batch_size, num_workers, prefetch_factor,\n                  seed, shuffle, drop_last, persistent_workers=True)\n```\n\nRun cell 11 again, then cell 45.\n\nOne caveat on `reload()`: it re-executes functions fine, but if you ever change a **class** in `wave_common.py`, objects already built from the old class definition keep the old one \u2014 restart the kernel for that case. It's noted in the cell comment.",
